{"turn": 6, "role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "call_00_q4Xl8ev8mErKyCQ4EkBZ7745", "tool_name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/workspace/documents/brightpath-lease.docx\"}", "result": "**[LEASE AGREEMENT]{.underline}**\n\nbetween\n\n**THORNFIELD REALTY HOLDINGS LP** a North Carolina limited partnership (\\\"Landlord\\\")\n\nand\n\n**BRIGHTPATH LEARNING CENTERS LLC** a North Carolina limited liability company (\\\"Tenant\\\")\n\nDated: January 10, 2017\n\nProperty: Millbrook Medical Plaza 1585 Millbrook Road, Suite 100 Raleigh, NC 27609\n\nRight-click to update Table of Contents\n\n**[ARTICLE 1 --- BASIC LEASE INFORMATION]{.underline}**\n\nThe following is a summary of the key terms and data of this Lease Agreement (this \\\"Lease\\\"). Each reference in this Article 1 is qualified in its entirety by the detailed provisions of the applicable Article, Section, or Exhibit of this Lease. In the event of any conflict between the provisions of this Article 1 and the more detailed provisions of this Lease, the more detailed provisions shall control.\n\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Item**                            **Description**\n  ----------------------------------- --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Lease Date:**                     January 10, 2017\n\n  **Landlord:**                       Thornfield Realty Holdings LP, a North Carolina limited partnership\n\n  **Landlord\\'s Address:**            500 South Tryon Street, Suite 2200, Charlotte, NC 28202\n\n  **General Partner:**                Thornfield Management Corp., a North Carolina corporation\n\n  **Tenant:**                         BrightPath Learning Centers LLC, a North Carolina limited liability company\n\n  **Tenant\\'s Address:**              1585 Millbrook Road, Suite 100, Raleigh, NC 27609\n\n  **Registered Agent:**               DC Agent Services Inc., 150 Fayetteville Street, Raleigh, NC 27601\n\n  **Property:**                       Millbrook Medical Plaza, 1585 Millbrook Road, Raleigh, NC 27609\n\n  **Premises:**                       Suite 100, ground floor, approximately 5,800 rentable square feet (\\\"RSF\\\")\n\n  **Commencement Date:**              April 1, 2017\n\n  **Expiration Date:**                March 31, 2032\n\n  **Lease Term:**                     Fifteen (15) years\n\n  **Base Rent:**                      Year 1: \\$22.00/RSF/year (\\$127,600.00/year; \\$10,633.33/month). See Article 4 and Exhibit B for the full rent schedule.\n\n  **Rent Escalation:**                Two and one-half percent (2.5%) annual compounding, effective each anniversary of the Commencement Date\n\n  **Lease Type:**                     Triple Net (NNN)\n\n  **Security Deposit:**               \\$25,440.00, equal to two (2) months of Base Rent\n\n  **Permitted Use:**                  Childcare, daycare, and early childhood education facility\n\n  **Renewal Options:**                Three (3) consecutive options to renew for five (5) years each\n\n  **Broker:**                         Ridgeview Realty Brokers Inc. (Landlord\\'s broker)\n\n  **Property Manager:**               Karen Osgood, Thornfield Realty Holdings LP\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**[ARTICLE 2 --- PREMISES AND PROPERTY]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 2.1 --- Demise of Premises]{.underline}**\n\nLandlord hereby leases to Tenant, and Tenant hereby leases from Landlord, Suite 100 on the ground floor of the building commonly known as Millbrook Medical Plaza, located at 1585 Millbrook Road, Raleigh, NC 27609 (the \\\"Building\\\"), consisting of approximately five thousand eight hundred (5,800) rentable square feet, as more particularly described and depicted on the floor plan attached hereto as **Exhibit A** and incorporated herein by reference (the \\\"Premises\\\"). The Premises are situated on the ground floor of the Building, with direct access to the ground-level courtyard and patio area adjacent thereto.\n\n**[Section 2.2 --- Common Areas]{.underline}**\n\nTenant shall have the non-exclusive right, in common with Landlord, other tenants of the Building, and their respective employees, agents, customers, and invitees, to use the common areas of the Building and the Property, including but not limited to lobbies, corridors, restrooms, elevators, stairways, parking areas, driveways, sidewalks, and landscaped areas (collectively, the \\\"Common Areas\\\"). Landlord reserves the right to modify, reconfigure, or relocate Common Areas from time to time, provided that such modifications do not materially adversely affect Tenant\\'s access to or use of the Premises or the parking areas serving the Premises.\n\n**[Section 2.3 --- Measurement]{.underline}**\n\nThe rentable square footage of the Premises has been determined in accordance with the Building Owners and Managers Association International (\\\"BOMA\\\") 2010 Standard Method of Measurement. Landlord and Tenant hereby stipulate and agree that the Premises contain five thousand eight hundred (5,800) RSF. This measurement shall not be subject to re-measurement or adjustment during the Lease Term or any Renewal Term.\n\n**[ARTICLE 3 --- TERM]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 3.1 --- Lease Term]{.underline}**\n\nThe term of this Lease (the \\\"Lease Term\\\") shall be fifteen (15) years, commencing on April 1, 2017 (the \\\"Commencement Date\\\") and expiring on March 31, 2032 (the \\\"Expiration Date\\\"), unless sooner terminated or extended in accordance with the terms and provisions of this Lease.\n\n**[Section 3.2 --- Early Access]{.underline}**\n\nLandlord shall provide Tenant with early access to the Premises beginning February 1, 2017, for the sole purpose of installing fixtures, furniture, and equipment and completing Tenant\\'s improvements and build-out work as described in **Exhibit D**. During any period of early access, Tenant shall not interfere with Landlord\\'s ongoing construction activities, if any, and shall maintain all insurance required under Article 9 of this Lease. No Base Rent or Additional Rent shall be due during the early access period, but Tenant shall be responsible for the cost of any utilities consumed by Tenant during such period.\n\n**[Section 3.3 --- Holdover]{.underline}**\n\nIf Tenant remains in possession of the Premises after the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease without the express written consent of Landlord, Tenant shall be deemed a holdover tenant on a month-to-month basis and shall pay holdover rent equal to one hundred fifty percent (150%) of the Base Rent in effect during the last month of the Lease Term (or the last Renewal Term, as applicable), plus all Additional Rent and other charges due under this Lease. Such holdover tenancy shall be subject to all other terms and conditions of this Lease and may be terminated by either party upon thirty (30) days\\' prior written notice to the other party. Acceptance of holdover rent by Landlord shall not constitute a waiver of Landlord\\'s right to terminate the holdover tenancy or to recover possession of the Premises.\n\n**[ARTICLE 4 --- BASE RENT]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 4.1 --- Base Rent Payments]{.underline}**\n\nTenant shall pay Base Rent to Landlord in equal monthly installments, in advance, on or before the first (1st) day of each calendar month during the Lease Term, without prior demand, notice, deduction, or setoff of any kind. If the Commencement Date falls on a day other than the first day of a calendar month, the Base Rent for the partial month in which the Commencement Date occurs shall be prorated on a per diem basis based on the actual number of days in such month. All payments of Base Rent and Additional Rent shall be made to Landlord at Landlord\\'s Address stated in Article 1, or to such other address or payee as Landlord may designate by written notice to Tenant from time to time.\n\n**[Section 4.2 --- Base Rent Schedule]{.underline}**\n\nBase Rent shall escalate at a rate of two and one-half percent (2.5%) per annum, compounded annually, effective on each anniversary of the Commencement Date. The Base Rent for each Lease Year during the Lease Term shall be as set forth in the following schedule, and as further set forth in **Exhibit B** attached hereto:\n\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Lease Year**   **Period**                 **Base Rent (\\$/RSF/Year)**   **Annual Base Rent**   **Monthly Base Rent**\n  ---------------- -------------------------- ----------------------------- ---------------------- -----------------------\n  Year 1           04/01/2017 -- 03/31/2018   \\$22.00                       \\$127,600.00           \\$10,633.33\n\n  Year 2           04/01/2018 -- 03/31/2019   \\$22.55                       \\$130,790.00           \\$10,899.17\n\n  Year 3           04/01/2019 -- 03/31/2020   \\$23.11                       \\$134,038.00           \\$11,169.83\n\n  Year 4           04/01/2020 -- 03/31/2021   \\$23.69                       \\$137,402.00           \\$11,450.17\n\n  Year 5           04/01/2021 -- 03/31/2022   \\$24.28                       \\$140,824.00           \\$11,735.33\n\n  Year 6           04/01/2022 -- 03/31/2023   \\$24.89                       \\$144,362.00           \\$12,030.17\n\n  Year 7           04/01/2023 -- 03/31/2024   \\$25.51                       \\$147,958.00           \\$12,329.83\n\n  Year 8           04/01/2024 -- 03/31/2025   \\$25.90                       \\$150,220.00           \\$12,518.33\n\n  Year 9           04/01/2025 -- 03/31/2026   \\$26.55                       \\$153,990.00           \\$12,832.50\n\n  Year 10          04/01/2026 -- 03/31/2027   \\$27.21                       \\$157,818.00           \\$13,151.50\n\n  Year 11          04/01/2027 -- 03/31/2028   \\$27.89                       \\$161,762.00           \\$13,480.17\n\n  Year 12          04/01/2028 -- 03/31/2029   \\$28.59                       \\$165,822.00           \\$13,818.50\n\n  Year 13          04/01/2029 -- 03/31/2030   \\$29.30                       \\$169,940.00           \\$14,161.67\n\n  Year 14          04/01/2030 -- 03/31/2031   \\$30.04                       \\$174,232.00           \\$14,519.33\n\n  Year 15          04/01/2031 -- 03/31/2032   \\$30.79                       \\$178,582.00           \\$14,881.83\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nFor purposes of this Section 4.2, a \\\"Lease Year\\\" shall mean each twelve (12) month period commencing on the Commencement Date or any anniversary thereof. All figures in the foregoing schedule have been calculated based on the Premises containing 5,800 RSF and rounded to the nearest cent.\n\n**[Section 4.3 --- Late Payment]{.underline}**\n\nIf any installment of Base Rent or Additional Rent is not received by Landlord within five (5) business days after the date on which such payment is due, Tenant shall pay to Landlord a late charge equal to five percent (5%) of the overdue amount, which late charge the parties agree represents a fair and reasonable estimate of the costs Landlord will incur by reason of such late payment. In addition, all amounts of Base Rent, Additional Rent, or other sums payable by Tenant under this Lease that remain unpaid for more than thirty (30) days after the due date shall bear interest from the due date until paid at the rate of one and one-half percent (1.5%) per month (eighteen percent (18%) per annum), or the maximum rate permitted by applicable North Carolina law, whichever is less. The foregoing late charge and interest provisions shall not be deemed to excuse or cure any default by Tenant or to limit any other remedy available to Landlord.\n\n**[ARTICLE 5 --- ADDITIONAL RENT / TRIPLE NET PROVISIONS]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 5.1 --- NNN Lease; Tenant\\'s Obligations]{.underline}**\n\nThis Lease is intended to be, and shall be construed as, a triple net (\\\"NNN\\\") lease. In addition to Base Rent, Tenant shall pay as additional rent (collectively, \\\"Additional Rent\\\") Tenant\\'s Pro Rata Share of all Operating Expenses, Real Estate Taxes, and Insurance Costs incurred by Landlord in connection with the ownership, operation, and management of the Building and Property. As used herein, \\\"Tenant\\'s Pro Rata Share\\\" shall mean a fraction, the numerator of which is the RSF of the Premises (5,800 RSF) and the denominator of which is the total RSF of the Building as reasonably determined by Landlord. Landlord shall provide Tenant with written notice of the total RSF of the Building and Tenant\\'s Pro Rata Share within thirty (30) days following the Commencement Date.\n\n**[Section 5.2 --- Operating Expenses]{.underline}**\n\nAs used herein, \\\"Operating Expenses\\\" shall mean all costs and expenses incurred by Landlord in the operation, management, maintenance, and repair of the Building and the Property, including but not limited to the following: common area maintenance and repair; landscaping and grounds maintenance; snow and ice removal; property management fees (which shall not exceed five percent (5%) of gross revenues from the Building); utilities serving common areas; janitorial services for common areas; security services and systems; pest control; fire and life safety systems maintenance; elevator maintenance; general administrative costs reasonably allocable to the Building; and any other costs customarily included in operating expenses for comparable commercial properties in the Raleigh, North Carolina metropolitan area.\n\nNotwithstanding the foregoing, Operating Expenses shall not include: (a) capital expenditures, except those required by any law, ordinance, or governmental regulation enacted or promulgated after the Commencement Date, which capital expenditures shall be amortized over their useful life in accordance with generally accepted accounting principles with interest at a rate not to exceed eight percent (8%) per annum; (b) leasing commissions, brokerage fees, and advertising costs; (c) tenant improvement costs for other tenants of the Building; (d) Landlord\\'s income taxes, franchise taxes, or inheritance taxes; (e) depreciation of the Building or any improvements thereto; (f) costs of correcting defects in the original construction of the Building; and (g) costs of any items to the extent reimbursed by insurance proceeds, condemnation awards, or other tenants of the Building.\n\n**[Section 5.3 --- Real Estate Taxes]{.underline}**\n\nTenant shall pay Tenant\\'s Pro Rata Share of all real estate taxes, assessments, special assessments, and governmental charges of every kind and nature levied or assessed against the Property or any portion thereof (collectively, \\\"Real Estate Taxes\\\"). Real Estate Taxes shall include any tax or assessment levied in lieu of or in addition to real estate taxes. If Landlord obtains a reduction in Real Estate Taxes through protest or appeal, the net savings (after deduction of costs incurred in obtaining such reduction) shall be passed through to Tenant as a credit against Tenant\\'s Pro Rata Share.\n\n**[Section 5.4 --- Insurance Costs]{.underline}**\n\nTenant shall pay Tenant\\'s Pro Rata Share of all premiums and costs for property insurance, commercial general liability insurance, and umbrella or excess liability insurance policies maintained by Landlord on the Building and the Property (collectively, \\\"Insurance Costs\\\"). Insurance Costs shall also include the cost of any insurance deductibles paid by Landlord in connection with insured losses affecting the Common Areas or the Building structure.\n\n**[Section 5.5 --- Estimates and Reconciliation]{.underline}**\n\nPrior to the commencement of each calendar year during the Lease Term (or, with respect to the first year, within thirty (30) days after the Commencement Date), Landlord shall provide Tenant with a written estimate of the total Operating Expenses, Real Estate Taxes, and Insurance Costs anticipated for such calendar year, together with a calculation of Tenant\\'s estimated monthly Additional Rent payment. Tenant shall pay the estimated Additional Rent in equal monthly installments, due on the first day of each calendar month, concurrently with the payment of Base Rent.\n\nWithin ninety (90) days following the end of each calendar year, Landlord shall deliver to Tenant a reasonably detailed statement (the \\\"Annual Reconciliation Statement\\\") setting forth the actual Operating Expenses, Real Estate Taxes, and Insurance Costs for the prior calendar year and Tenant\\'s Pro Rata Share thereof. If the actual Additional Rent exceeds the estimated amounts paid by Tenant during such calendar year, Tenant shall pay the deficiency to Landlord within thirty (30) days after receipt of the Annual Reconciliation Statement. If the estimated amounts paid by Tenant exceed the actual Additional Rent, Landlord shall credit the overpayment against the next installment(s) of Additional Rent due from Tenant, or, if the Lease Term has expired, refund the overpayment to Tenant within thirty (30) days.\n\nTenant shall have the right, at Tenant\\'s sole cost and expense, to audit Landlord\\'s books and records relating to Operating Expenses, Real Estate Taxes, and Insurance Costs upon not less than thirty (30) days\\' prior written notice to Landlord. Any such audit shall be conducted during normal business hours at the offices of Landlord or Landlord\\'s property manager, and must be commenced within twelve (12) months following Tenant\\'s receipt of the Annual Reconciliation Statement for the applicable calendar year. If such audit reveals an overcharge of more than five percent (5%), Landlord shall reimburse Tenant for the reasonable cost of the audit.\n\n**[ARTICLE 6 --- SECURITY DEPOSIT]{.underline}**\n\nUpon execution of this Lease, Tenant shall deposit with Landlord the sum of Twenty-Five Thousand Four Hundred Forty Dollars (\\$25,440.00) as a security deposit (the \\\"Security Deposit\\\"), equal to two (2) months of Base Rent. Landlord shall hold the Security Deposit as security for the faithful performance by Tenant of all terms, covenants, and conditions of this Lease. Landlord may, but shall not be required to, apply all or any portion of the Security Deposit to cure any default by Tenant under this Lease, including but not limited to the payment of any rent or other amounts due and unpaid, the repair of damage to the Premises caused by Tenant, or the payment of any other sum which Landlord may expend or be required to expend by reason of Tenant\\'s default. If Landlord applies any portion of the Security Deposit, Tenant shall, within ten (10) days after written notice from Landlord, deposit with Landlord an amount sufficient to restore the Security Deposit to its original amount. Landlord shall not be required to hold the Security Deposit in a separate account or in an interest-bearing account, and Landlord shall not be required to pay interest on the Security Deposit. The Security Deposit, or any balance remaining after application by Landlord as permitted herein, shall be returned to Tenant within thirty (30) days after the expiration of the Lease Term and Tenant\\'s complete vacation and surrender of the Premises in the condition required by this Lease, less any amounts applied by Landlord in accordance with this Article 6.\n\n**[ARTICLE 7 --- USE OF PREMISES]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 7.1 --- Permitted Use]{.underline}**\n\nTenant shall use and occupy the Premises solely for the operation of a childcare, daycare, and early childhood education facility (the \\\"Permitted Use\\\") and for no other purpose whatsoever without the prior written consent of Landlord, which consent may be withheld in Landlord\\'s sole and absolute discretion. Tenant shall comply with all applicable federal, state, and local laws, rules, regulations, and ordinances governing the operation of a childcare and daycare facility, including but not limited to the licensing requirements of the North Carolina Division of Child Development and Early Education, and shall maintain in full force and effect at all times during the Lease Term all licenses and permits required for the Permitted Use.\n\n**[Section 7.2 --- Exclusive Use]{.underline}**\n\nDuring the Lease Term and any Renewal Term, Landlord shall not lease, permit, or suffer any other space within Millbrook Medical Plaza to be used for the operation of a childcare, daycare, or early childhood education facility. This exclusive use covenant shall run with the Property and shall be binding upon Landlord and Landlord\\'s successors and assigns with respect to the Building and the Property. In the event Landlord breaches this Section 7.2, Tenant\\'s sole remedy shall be to seek injunctive relief and to recover actual damages incurred by Tenant as a result of such breach.\n\n**[Section 7.3 --- Compliance with Law]{.underline}**\n\nTenant shall, at its sole cost and expense, comply with all applicable laws, ordinances, rules, regulations, codes, and governmental requirements relating to the use, occupancy, and condition of the Premises, including without limitation all applicable fire codes, building codes, health and sanitation regulations, zoning ordinances, and the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, as amended (\\\"ADA\\\"). Tenant shall obtain and maintain at all times during the Lease Term all permits, licenses, and governmental approvals required for the Permitted Use, and shall promptly furnish copies thereof to Landlord upon request.\n\n**[ARTICLE 8 --- MAINTENANCE AND REPAIRS]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 8.1 --- Tenant\\'s Obligations]{.underline}**\n\nTenant shall, at its sole cost and expense, keep and maintain the interior of the Premises in good order, condition, and repair throughout the Lease Term, including but not limited to interior walls, ceilings, floors, floor coverings, doors, windows (excluding exterior glazing), plumbing fixtures and connections within the Premises, electrical fixtures and wiring within the Premises, lighting, and all mechanical systems, including heating, ventilation, and air conditioning (\\\"HVAC\\\") systems, exclusively serving the Premises. Tenant shall be responsible for the regular maintenance, service, and repair of all HVAC equipment serving the Premises, including regular filter replacement and seasonal preventive maintenance performed in accordance with the manufacturer\\'s specifications and recommendations. Tenant shall enter into a maintenance contract with a qualified HVAC service provider, a copy of which shall be provided to Landlord upon request. Tenant shall promptly make all repairs and replacements necessary to maintain the interior of the Premises in good condition, ordinary wear and tear and damage by fire or other casualty excepted.\n\n**[Section 8.2 --- Landlord\\'s Obligations]{.underline}**\n\nLandlord shall be responsible for the maintenance, repair, and replacement of the structural elements of the Building, including but not limited to the roof, foundation, exterior walls, structural columns, load-bearing walls, and the building envelope (collectively, the \\\"Structural Elements\\\"). Landlord shall maintain the roof in watertight condition throughout the Lease Term. The costs of structural repairs and replacements shall not be included in Operating Expenses except to the extent of capital expenditures amortized as permitted in Section 5.2 of this Lease. Landlord shall perform all structural repairs and replacements in a workmanlike manner and shall use commercially reasonable efforts to minimize disruption to Tenant\\'s operations.\n\n**[ARTICLE 9 --- INSURANCE]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 9.1 --- Tenant\\'s Insurance]{.underline}**\n\nTenant shall procure and maintain in full force and effect at all times during the Lease Term, at Tenant\\'s sole cost and expense, the following insurance coverages:\n\n> \\(a\\) **Commercial General Liability Insurance** with limits of not less than One Million Dollars (\\$1,000,000.00) per occurrence and Two Million Dollars (\\$2,000,000.00) in the aggregate, covering bodily injury, personal injury, and property damage arising out of or in connection with Tenant\\'s use and occupancy of the Premises, including products/completed operations and contractual liability coverage;\n>\n> \\(b\\) **Property Insurance** covering all of Tenant\\'s personal property, trade fixtures, inventory, equipment, and leasehold improvements located within the Premises, written on an \\\"all risks\\\" or \\\"special form\\\" basis, in an amount not less than the full replacement cost thereof;\n>\n> \\(c\\) **Workers\\' Compensation Insurance** as required by the laws of the State of North Carolina, including employer\\'s liability coverage with limits of not less than Five Hundred Thousand Dollars (\\$500,000.00) per accident;\n>\n> \\(d\\) **Business Income Insurance** (including extra expense coverage) in an amount sufficient to cover not less than twelve (12) months of Tenant\\'s gross earnings and continuing expenses, including the payment of rent under this Lease.\n\nAll policies required under this Section 9.1 shall name Landlord, Thornfield Management Corp., and any mortgagee of the Property designated by Landlord as additional insureds. All policies shall be issued by insurance companies licensed to do business in the State of North Carolina with an A.M. Best rating of not less than A-VII. Certificates of insurance evidencing the foregoing coverages shall be delivered to Landlord prior to the Commencement Date and annually thereafter, and shall provide that such policies shall not be cancelled or materially modified without at least thirty (30) days\\' prior written notice to Landlord.\n\n**[Section 9.2 --- Landlord\\'s Insurance]{.underline}**\n\nLandlord shall maintain property insurance on the Building (excluding Tenant\\'s personal property, trade fixtures, and leasehold improvements) and commercial general liability insurance covering the Common Areas, in such amounts and with such coverages as Landlord deems reasonably appropriate. The cost of such insurance shall be included in Insurance Costs and passed through to Tenant as Additional Rent in accordance with Article 5 of this Lease.\n\n**[Section 9.3 --- Waiver of Subrogation]{.underline}**\n\nEach party hereby waives any and all rights of recovery, claim, action, or cause of action against the other party, its agents, officers, employees, and representatives, for any loss or damage to property to the extent that such loss or damage is covered by insurance required to be carried under this Lease or by insurance actually carried by the waiving party, whichever is greater, notwithstanding that such loss or damage may result from the negligence or fault of the other party. Each party shall cause its respective insurance carrier(s) to include a waiver of subrogation endorsement or clause in all applicable policies, permitting the foregoing waiver.\n\n**[ARTICLE 10 --- ENVIRONMENTAL PROVISIONS]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 10.1 --- Tenant\\'s Environmental Obligations]{.underline}**\n\nTenant shall comply with all applicable federal, state, and local environmental laws, statutes, regulations, ordinances, and governmental requirements relating to the use and occupancy of the Premises and the operation of a childcare facility therein, including but not limited to the Comprehensive Environmental Response, Compensation, and Liability Act (\\\"CERCLA\\\"), the Resource Conservation and Recovery Act (\\\"RCRA\\\"), the Toxic Substances Control Act (\\\"TSCA\\\"), and analogous state laws. Tenant shall be responsible for the proper handling, storage, use, and disposal of all cleaning chemicals, sanitizers, craft materials, and any other substances or materials that may be classified as hazardous or regulated under applicable environmental laws (collectively, \\\"Hazardous Materials\\\"). Tenant shall not cause or permit any Hazardous Materials to be generated, manufactured, stored, released, discharged, or disposed of on, under, or from the Premises, except in de minimis quantities that are customarily used in the ordinary course of childcare operations and that are used, stored, and disposed of in full compliance with all applicable environmental laws.\n\n**[Section 10.2 --- Phase I ESA]{.underline}**\n\nPrior to the Commencement Date, Landlord provided Tenant with a copy of a Phase I Environmental Site Assessment of the Property prepared by Hanover Environmental Consultants Inc. and dated November 15, 2016 (the \\\"Phase I Report\\\"). The Phase I Report identified no recognized environmental conditions (\\\"RECs\\\"), controlled recognized environmental conditions (\\\"CRECs\\\"), or historical recognized environmental conditions (\\\"HRECs\\\") affecting the Property. Landlord represents and warrants that, to Landlord\\'s actual knowledge as of the Lease Date, the Phase I Report remains accurate and complete in all material respects and that no conditions have arisen since the date of the Phase I Report that would constitute a REC affecting the Property.\n\n**[Section 10.3 --- Indemnification]{.underline}**\n\nTenant shall indemnify, defend, and hold harmless Landlord, Landlord\\'s partners, officers, directors, agents, and employees from and against any and all claims, actions, demands, liabilities, losses, damages, costs, and expenses (including reasonable attorneys\\' fees and court costs) arising from or related to (a) Tenant\\'s violation of any applicable environmental law, (b) the presence, release, or threatened release of any Hazardous Materials on, under, or from the Premises that are introduced by Tenant, its agents, employees, contractors, or invitees, or (c) any environmental contamination or remediation obligation attributable to Tenant\\'s use and occupancy of the Premises. This indemnification obligation shall survive the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease.\n\n**[ARTICLE 11 --- ASSIGNMENT AND SUBLETTING]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 11.1 --- Landlord Consent Required]{.underline}**\n\nTenant shall not assign, transfer, mortgage, pledge, hypothecate, or encumber this Lease or any interest herein, nor sublet all or any portion of the Premises, nor permit the use or occupancy of the Premises by any party other than Tenant, without the prior written consent of Landlord in each instance. Landlord\\'s consent to any proposed assignment or subletting may be withheld in Landlord\\'s reasonable discretion, based upon factors including but not limited to the proposed assignee\\'s or subtenant\\'s financial condition, creditworthiness, business reputation, intended use of the Premises, and compatibility with the character and operation of the Building.\n\n**[Section 11.2 --- Landlord Recapture Right]{.underline}**\n\nUpon receipt of Tenant\\'s request for consent to an assignment or subletting, together with all information and documentation required under Section 11.3, Landlord shall have the option, exercisable within thirty (30) days after receipt of Tenant\\'s notice and all required documentation, to terminate this Lease as to the space proposed to be assigned or sublet (or as to the entire Premises in the case of a proposed assignment of this Lease) effective as of the proposed commencement date of the assignment or sublease (the \\\"Recapture Right\\\"). If Landlord exercises the Recapture Right, this Lease shall terminate as of the effective date specified in Landlord\\'s notice (or, in the case of a proposed sublease of less than the entire Premises, the Premises shall be reduced by the space proposed to be sublet, and the Base Rent and Tenant\\'s Pro Rata Share shall be equitably adjusted). Upon exercise of the Recapture Right, Landlord may lease the recaptured space directly to the proposed assignee or subtenant, or to any other party, on whatever terms and conditions Landlord deems appropriate, without any obligation or liability to Tenant, and Tenant shall have no right to share in any rent, premium, or other consideration received by Landlord in connection therewith. If Landlord does not exercise the Recapture Right within the thirty (30) day period, Landlord\\'s consent to the proposed assignment or subletting shall not be unreasonably withheld, conditioned, or delayed, subject to the conditions set forth in Section 11.3 below.\n\n**[Section 11.3 --- Conditions of Consent]{.underline}**\n\nAny consent by Landlord to an assignment or subletting shall be subject to the following conditions, each of which must be satisfied prior to the effective date of the assignment or sublease:\n\n> \\(a\\) The proposed assignee or subtenant shall satisfy Landlord\\'s reasonable financial, operational, and creditworthiness standards and shall demonstrate the ability to fulfill all obligations under this Lease (or the sublease, as applicable);\n>\n> \\(b\\) Tenant shall remain primarily liable under this Lease, jointly and severally with the assignee, for the payment of all rent and the performance of all obligations hereunder, unless Landlord expressly agrees in writing to release Tenant;\n>\n> \\(c\\) The assignment or sublease shall be on terms and conditions substantially consistent with the terms of this Lease, and the proposed use shall be limited to the Permitted Use or another use approved by Landlord;\n>\n> \\(d\\) Tenant shall provide Landlord with not less than thirty (30) days\\' prior written notice of the proposed assignment or subletting, together with complete information regarding the proposed assignee or subtenant, including audited financial statements for the most recent three (3) fiscal years, a business plan, references, and the proposed terms of the assignment or sublease;\n>\n> \\(e\\) Tenant shall pay to Landlord, as additional rent, fifty percent (50%) of any consideration received by Tenant from the assignee or subtenant in excess of the rent payable by Tenant under this Lease (net of Tenant\\'s reasonable costs of the assignment or subletting, including brokerage fees, legal fees, and tenant improvement costs).\n\n**[Section 11.4 --- Affiliate Transfers]{.underline}**\n\nNotwithstanding the provisions of Sections 11.1, 11.2, and 11.3 above, Tenant may, without Landlord\\'s consent and without triggering Landlord\\'s Recapture Right, assign this Lease or sublet all or any portion of the Premises to an Affiliate of Tenant, provided that (a) Tenant gives Landlord written notice of such transfer at least fifteen (15) days prior to the effective date thereof, (b) the Affiliate assumes in writing all obligations of Tenant under this Lease, and (c) Tenant remains primarily liable for all obligations under this Lease. As used herein, \\\"Affiliate\\\" shall mean any entity that directly or indirectly controls, is controlled by, or is under common control with Tenant, where \\\"control\\\" means the ownership of fifty percent (50%) or more of the voting interests or the power to direct the management and policies of such entity.\n\n**[ARTICLE 12 --- RENEWAL OPTIONS]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 12.1 --- Grant of Options]{.underline}**\n\nProvided that Tenant is not then in default beyond any applicable notice and cure period under this Lease at the time of exercise or at the commencement of the applicable Renewal Term, Tenant shall have three (3) consecutive options to renew the Lease Term for a period of five (5) years each (each, a \\\"Renewal Term\\\"), on the same terms and conditions as set forth in this Lease, except as to Base Rent, which shall be determined in accordance with Sections 12.3 and 12.4 below.\n\n**[Section 12.2 --- Exercise of Options]{.underline}**\n\nTenant shall exercise each Renewal Option by delivering irrevocable written notice to Landlord not later than twelve (12) months prior to the expiration of the then-current Lease Term or Renewal Term, as applicable. Time is of the essence with respect to the exercise of each Renewal Option. Failure by Tenant to timely deliver written notice of exercise in accordance with this Section 12.2 shall constitute an irrevocable waiver of the applicable Renewal Option and all subsequent unexercised Renewal Options. The exercise of each Renewal Option is personal to BrightPath Learning Centers LLC and may not be exercised by any assignee or subtenant of Tenant (other than a permitted Affiliate transferee under Section 11.4).\n\n**[Section 12.3 --- Renewal Rent]{.underline}**\n\n> \\(a\\) **First Renewal Term (Years 16--20):** The Base Rent for each year of the First Renewal Term shall be the lesser of (i) the then-prevailing fair market rent for comparable childcare and daycare space in the Raleigh, North Carolina metropolitan area, determined in accordance with Section 12.4, and (ii) the Base Rent resulting from three percent (3%) annual compounding from the Year 15 Base Rent. If the parties cannot agree upon the fair market rent within thirty (30) days following Tenant\\'s exercise of the First Renewal Option, the fair market rent shall be determined by arbitration in accordance with Section 12.4.\n>\n> \\(b\\) **Second Renewal Term (Years 21--25) and Third Renewal Term (Years 26--30):** The Base Rent for each year of the Second and Third Renewal Terms shall be the then-prevailing fair market rent for comparable childcare and daycare space in the Raleigh, North Carolina metropolitan area, determined in accordance with Section 12.4.\n\n**[Section 12.4 --- Fair Market Rent Determination]{.underline}**\n\nIf the parties are unable to agree upon the fair market rent for the applicable Renewal Term within thirty (30) days following Tenant\\'s exercise of the applicable Renewal Option, then each party shall, within fifteen (15) days thereafter, appoint a licensed MAI-designated appraiser with at least ten (10) years of experience appraising commercial properties in the Raleigh, North Carolina metropolitan area. The two appraisers so appointed shall attempt to agree upon the fair market rent within thirty (30) days following their appointment. If the two appraisers are unable to agree upon the fair market rent within such thirty (30) day period, they shall jointly appoint a third licensed MAI-designated appraiser meeting the same qualifications, and the fair market rent shall be the average of the two appraisals that are closest in value. The costs and fees of the third appraiser shall be borne equally by Landlord and Tenant. Each party shall bear the costs and fees of its own appointed appraiser. Pending the determination of fair market rent, Tenant shall pay Base Rent at the rate in effect during the last year of the immediately preceding Lease Term or Renewal Term, with an adjustment (credit or payment) to be made within thirty (30) days following the final determination.\n\n**[ARTICLE 13 --- LANDLORD\\'S ACCESS]{.underline}**\n\nLandlord and its authorized agents, employees, and contractors shall have the right to enter the Premises during normal business hours upon not less than twenty-four (24) hours\\' prior written or oral notice to Tenant for the purpose of (a) inspecting the Premises, (b) performing repairs, maintenance, or alterations to the Premises or the Building, (c) showing the Premises to prospective tenants during the last twelve (12) months of the Lease Term or any Renewal Term, (d) showing the Premises to prospective purchasers, lenders, or insurers at any time during the Lease Term, and (e) any other reasonable purpose related to Landlord\\'s ownership and operation of the Building. In the event of an emergency threatening imminent damage to the Premises, the Building, or persons therein, Landlord may enter the Premises at any time without prior notice. Given the nature of the Permitted Use as a childcare facility serving minor children, Landlord agrees to coordinate all access with Tenant\\'s on-site manager so as to minimize disruption to childcare operations and to ensure the safety and security of all children present on the Premises. All persons entering the Premises on behalf of Landlord shall comply with Tenant\\'s reasonable sign-in and identification procedures.\n\n**[ARTICLE 14 --- DAMAGE AND DESTRUCTION]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 14.1 --- Landlord\\'s Obligation to Restore]{.underline}**\n\nIf the Premises or the Building are damaged or destroyed by fire or other casualty, Landlord shall, subject to the provisions of this Article 14, commence restoration of the Premises and the Building within sixty (60) days after the date of such damage or destruction and shall diligently pursue such restoration to completion within one hundred eighty (180) days thereafter. Landlord\\'s restoration obligation shall be limited to the Structural Elements and the base building improvements and shall not include Tenant\\'s personal property, trade fixtures, leasehold improvements, or any other property of Tenant. If Landlord reasonably determines that restoration cannot be completed within one hundred eighty (180) days after the date of damage or destruction, either party may terminate this Lease by delivering written notice to the other party within thirty (30) days after Landlord\\'s notice of such determination, and this Lease shall terminate as of the date specified in such notice.\n\n**[Section 14.2 --- Rent Abatement]{.underline}**\n\nDuring any period in which the Premises or any portion thereof are rendered untenantable by reason of fire or other casualty, Base Rent and Additional Rent shall abate proportionately based on the ratio of the untenantable area to the total RSF of the Premises. Such abatement shall commence on the date of the casualty and shall continue until the earlier of (a) the date on which the Premises are restored to a tenantable condition or (b) the date on which this Lease is terminated pursuant to Section 14.1.\n\n**[ARTICLE 15 --- CONDEMNATION]{.underline}**\n\nIf all or a material portion of the Premises is taken or condemned for any public or quasi-public purpose by any lawful authority by exercise of the right of eminent domain, or by agreement between Landlord and such authority in lieu of or in anticipation of such exercise (a \\\"Taking\\\"), either Landlord or Tenant may terminate this Lease by delivering written notice to the other party within thirty (30) days after the date of such Taking, and this Lease shall terminate as of the date on which possession is required to be delivered to the condemning authority. If less than a material portion of the Premises is taken and the remaining portion of the Premises is reasonably usable by Tenant for the Permitted Use, this Lease shall continue in full force and effect but the Base Rent shall be equitably reduced in proportion to the area of the Premises so taken. Tenant shall be entitled to make a separate claim against the condemning authority for the value of Tenant\\'s trade fixtures, personal property, and moving expenses, and for business interruption losses, but Tenant shall have no right to claim or receive any portion of any award made to Landlord for the value of the leasehold interest, the Building, or the land. As used herein, a \\\"material portion\\\" shall mean a Taking of twenty-five percent (25%) or more of the RSF of the Premises.\n\n**[ARTICLE 16 --- DEFAULT AND REMEDIES]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 16.1 --- Events of Default]{.underline}**\n\nThe occurrence of any one or more of the following events shall constitute an \\\"Event of Default\\\" under this Lease:\n\n> \\(a\\) Tenant fails to pay any installment of Base Rent, Additional Rent, or any other sum due under this Lease within five (5) business days after Landlord delivers written notice to Tenant that such payment is past due;\n>\n> \\(b\\) Tenant fails to perform or observe any other term, covenant, or condition of this Lease and such failure continues for a period of thirty (30) days after Landlord delivers written notice to Tenant specifying the nature of such failure; provided, however, that if the nature of such failure is such that it cannot reasonably be cured within thirty (30) days, Tenant shall not be in default if Tenant commences the cure within such thirty (30) day period and thereafter diligently and continuously pursues such cure to completion within a reasonable time;\n>\n> \\(c\\) Tenant files a voluntary petition in bankruptcy or is adjudicated bankrupt or insolvent, or makes a general assignment for the benefit of creditors, or a receiver or trustee is appointed for substantially all of Tenant\\'s assets or Tenant\\'s interest in this Lease, or any proceeding is commenced under any bankruptcy or insolvency law by or against Tenant and is not dismissed within sixty (60) days;\n>\n> \\(d\\) Tenant abandons or vacates the Premises for a period of thirty (30) or more consecutive days without maintaining the Premises in accordance with this Lease;\n>\n> \\(e\\) Tenant\\'s childcare license issued by the North Carolina Division of Child Development and Early Education is revoked, suspended, or otherwise rendered ineffective for a period of more than sixty (60) consecutive days.\n\n**[Section 16.2 --- Landlord\\'s Remedies]{.underline}**\n\nUpon the occurrence and continuation of an Event of Default, Landlord shall have the following remedies, which shall be cumulative and in addition to any other remedies available at law or in equity:\n\n> \\(a\\) Landlord may terminate this Lease by delivering written notice of termination to Tenant, whereupon Tenant shall immediately surrender possession of the Premises to Landlord. Upon termination, Landlord may recover from Tenant all unpaid rent accrued through the date of termination, the cost of recovering possession of the Premises, the cost of any reletting, and all damages incurred by Landlord as a result of Tenant\\'s default;\n>\n> \\(b\\) Landlord may re-enter and take possession of the Premises without terminating this Lease and may relet the Premises, or any portion thereof, on behalf of Tenant for such term, at such rent, and upon such other terms and conditions as Landlord may deem advisable, with the right to make alterations and repairs to the Premises. Tenant shall remain liable for all rent and other amounts due under this Lease, less the net proceeds of any reletting;\n>\n> \\(c\\) Landlord may recover as damages the present value of all future Base Rent and Additional Rent that would have been payable during the remainder of the Lease Term, discounted to present value at the rate of six percent (6%) per annum, less the fair market rental value of the Premises for the same period, similarly discounted, together with all other damages incurred by Landlord, including reasonable attorneys\\' fees and court costs.\n\nLandlord shall use commercially reasonable efforts to mitigate its damages following any Event of Default, in accordance with applicable North Carolina law.\n\n**[ARTICLE 17 --- SUBORDINATION AND ATTORNMENT]{.underline}**\n\nThis Lease shall be subject and subordinate to any mortgage, deed of trust, or ground lease now existing or hereafter placed upon the Property or any portion thereof, and to all renewals, modifications, consolidations, replacements, and extensions thereof (each, a \\\"Superior Instrument\\\"). Tenant agrees to execute and deliver, within ten (10) days after request, any instrument or certificate that Landlord or the holder of any Superior Instrument may reasonably require to confirm such subordination. Tenant further agrees to attorn to any successor landlord, including any purchaser at a foreclosure sale or any grantee under a deed in lieu of foreclosure, and to recognize such successor as Landlord under this Lease. Landlord shall use commercially reasonable efforts to obtain from any current or future holder of a Superior Instrument a subordination, non-disturbance, and attornment agreement (an \\\"SNDA\\\") in such holder\\'s customary form, providing that Tenant\\'s possession of the Premises shall not be disturbed so long as Tenant is not in default under this Lease, and Landlord shall deliver such SNDA to Tenant within thirty (30) days after Tenant\\'s written request therefor.\n\n**[ARTICLE 18 --- NOTICES]{.underline}**\n\nAll notices, demands, requests, consents, approvals, and other communications required or permitted under this Lease shall be in writing and shall be delivered by (a) personal hand delivery, (b) nationally recognized overnight courier service (such as FedEx or UPS Next Day), or (c) United States Certified Mail, return receipt requested, postage prepaid, addressed as follows:\n\n**If to Landlord:**\n\n> Thornfield Realty Holdings LP 500 South Tryon Street, Suite 2200 Charlotte, NC 28202\n>\n> Attention: Property Management --- Karen Osgood\n\n**If to Tenant:**\n\n> BrightPath Learning Centers LLC 1585 Millbrook Road, Suite 100 Raleigh, NC 27609\n>\n> Attention: Manager\n\nEither party may change its notice address by delivering written notice to the other party in accordance with this Article 18. Notices shall be deemed effective (i) upon receipt, if delivered by personal hand delivery; (ii) on the next business day following deposit with a nationally recognized overnight courier service; or (iii) on the third (3rd) business day following deposit in the United States Mail in accordance with this Article 18.\n\n**[ARTICLE 19 --- MISCELLANEOUS PROVISIONS]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 19.1 --- Entire Agreement]{.underline}**\n\nThis Lease, including all Exhibits attached hereto and incorporated herein by reference, constitutes the entire agreement between Landlord and Tenant with respect to the subject matter hereof and supersedes all prior negotiations, representations, warranties, commitments, proposals, and understandings, whether written or oral, relating thereto. No amendment, modification, or supplement to this Lease shall be valid or binding unless set forth in a written instrument executed by both Landlord and Tenant.\n\n**[Section 19.2 --- Governing Law]{.underline}**\n\nThis Lease shall be governed by and construed in accordance with the laws of the State of North Carolina, without giving effect to any choice of law or conflict of law principles that would result in the application of the laws of any other jurisdiction.\n\n**[Section 19.3 --- Severability]{.underline}**\n\nIf any provision of this Lease or the application thereof to any person or circumstance is held to be invalid, illegal, or unenforceable, the validity, legality, and enforceability of the remaining provisions of this Lease shall not be affected or impaired thereby, and the parties shall negotiate in good faith to replace the invalid provision with a valid provision that achieves, to the greatest extent possible, the economic, business, and other purposes of the invalid provision.\n\n**[Section 19.4 --- Successors and Assigns]{.underline}**\n\nThis Lease shall be binding upon and inure to the benefit of the parties hereto and their respective heirs, executors, administrators, successors, and permitted assigns, subject to the provisions of Article 11 governing assignments and subletting.\n\n**[Section 19.5 --- Waiver]{.underline}**\n\nNo waiver by either party of any breach of or default under any provision of this Lease shall be deemed a waiver of any other breach or default, whether of the same or a different provision, and no waiver shall be effective unless in writing and signed by the waiving party. No failure or delay by either party in exercising any right or remedy under this Lease shall operate as a waiver thereof.\n\n**[Section 19.6 --- Broker Representation]{.underline}**\n\nLandlord represents and warrants to Tenant that Ridgeview Realty Brokers Inc. acted as Landlord\\'s broker in connection with this Lease and that Landlord shall be solely responsible for the payment of all brokerage commissions due to Ridgeview Realty Brokers Inc. Tenant represents and warrants to Landlord that no broker, agent, or finder acted on behalf of Tenant in connection with this Lease. Each party hereby indemnifies and holds the other party harmless from and against any and all claims, losses, damages, costs, and expenses (including reasonable attorneys\\' fees) arising from any breach of the foregoing representations or from any claim for commissions or fees by any broker, agent, or finder claiming to have acted on behalf of the indemnifying party.\n\n**[Section 19.7 --- Force Majeure]{.underline}**\n\nExcept for the payment of Base Rent, Additional Rent, and any other monetary obligations under this Lease, neither party shall be liable for any delay or failure in the performance of any obligation under this Lease to the extent that such delay or failure is caused by fire, flood, earthquake, hurricane, tornado, pandemic, epidemic, governmental action or inaction, labor disputes, strikes, lockouts, material shortages, acts of terrorism, war, civil disturbance, or any other cause or event beyond the reasonable control of the affected party (each, a \\\"Force Majeure Event\\\"). The time for performance of any such obligation shall be extended by the duration of the Force Majeure Event.\n\n**[Section 19.8 --- Quiet Enjoyment]{.underline}**\n\nSo long as Tenant is not in default under this Lease beyond any applicable notice and cure period, Landlord covenants and agrees that Tenant shall peaceably and quietly have, hold, and enjoy the Premises for the full Lease Term and any Renewal Term without hindrance, disturbance, or molestation by Landlord or any person lawfully claiming by, through, or under Landlord.\n\n**[Section 19.9 --- Recording]{.underline}**\n\nNeither Landlord nor Tenant shall record this Lease or any memorandum thereof without the prior written consent of the other party. At the written request of either party, Landlord and Tenant shall promptly execute and deliver a short form memorandum of this Lease in recordable form, which memorandum shall set forth the names of the parties, the description of the Premises, the Commencement Date, the Expiration Date, and the Renewal Options, but shall not set forth the rental terms or other financial provisions of this Lease. The cost of recording such memorandum shall be borne by the requesting party.\n\n**[Section 19.10 --- Counterparts]{.underline}**\n\nThis Lease may be executed in any number of counterparts, each of which shall be deemed an original and all of which together shall constitute one and the same instrument. Execution and delivery of this Lease by facsimile or electronic transmission of a signed counterpart shall be deemed effective as delivery of an originally signed counterpart.\n\n**IN WITNESS WHEREOF**, the parties hereto have executed this Lease Agreement as of the date first written above.\n\n**LANDLORD:**\n\nTHORNFIELD REALTY HOLDINGS LP, a North Carolina limited partnership\n\nBy: Thornfield Management Corp.,\n\na North Carolina corporation, its General Partner\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: Renata Thornfield-Kiess\n\nTitle: President\n\nDate: January 10, 2017\n\n**TENANT:**\n\nBRIGHTPATH LEARNING CENTERS LLC, a North Carolina limited liability company\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nTitle: Manager\n\nDate: January 10, 2017\n\n**[EXHIBIT A]{.underline}**\n\n**[FLOOR PLAN OF PREMISES]{.underline}**\n\nThe following floor plan depicts the location and configuration of Suite 100 on the ground floor of Millbrook Medical Plaza, located at 1585 Millbrook Road, Raleigh, NC 27609, consisting of approximately 5,800 rentable square feet. The floor plan illustrates the primary entrance to Suite 100 from the Building lobby, the secondary entrance providing direct access to the ground-level courtyard and patio area, all demising walls separating the Premises from adjacent suites and Common Areas, and the points of connection to Common Area corridors, restrooms, and elevator lobbies.\n\n*\\[Floor plan to be attached. The floor plan shall be prepared by Landlord\\'s architect and shall depict Suite 100 with approximate dimensions, entrances, Common Area access points, demising walls, and the location of the outdoor courtyard/play area accessible from the Premises.\\]*\n\n**[EXHIBIT B]{.underline}**\n\n**[BASE RENT SCHEDULE]{.underline}**\n\n**Base Rent Escalation: 2.5% per annum, compounded annually, effective on each anniversary of the Commencement Date.**\n\nThe following schedule sets forth the Base Rent payable by Tenant for each Lease Year during the fifteen (15) year Lease Term, based on the Premises containing 5,800 rentable square feet:\n\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Lease Year**   **Period**                 **Base Rent (\\$/RSF/Year)**   **Annual Base Rent**   **Monthly Base Rent**\n  ---------------- -------------------------- ----------------------------- ---------------------- -----------------------\n  Year 1           04/01/2017 -- 03/31/2018   \\$22.00                       \\$127,600.00           \\$10,633.33\n\n  Year 2           04/01/2018 -- 03/31/2019   \\$22.55                       \\$130,790.00           \\$10,899.17\n\n  Year 3           04/01/2019 -- 03/31/2020   \\$23.11                       \\$134,038.00           \\$11,169.83\n\n  Year 4           04/01/2020 -- 03/31/2021   \\$23.69                       \\$137,402.00           \\$11,450.17\n\n  Year 5           04/01/2021 -- 03/31/2022   \\$24.28                       \\$140,824.00           \\$11,735.33\n\n  Year 6           04/01/2022 -- 03/31/2023   \\$24.89                       \\$144,362.00           \\$12,030.17\n\n  Year 7           04/01/2023 -- 03/31/2024   \\$25.51                       \\$147,958.00           \\$12,329.83\n\n  Year 8           04/01/2024 -- 03/31/2025   \\$25.90                       \\$150,220.00           \\$12,518.33\n\n  Year 9           04/01/2025 -- 03/31/2026   \\$26.55                       \\$153,990.00           \\$12,832.50\n\n  Year 10          04/01/2026 -- 03/31/2027   \\$27.21                       \\$157,818.00           \\$13,151.50\n\n  Year 11          04/01/2027 -- 03/31/2028   \\$27.89                       \\$161,762.00           \\$13,480.17\n\n  Year 12          04/01/2028 -- 03/31/2029   \\$28.59                       \\$165,822.00           \\$13,818.50\n\n  Year 13          04/01/2029 -- 03/31/2030   \\$29.30                       \\$169,940.00           \\$14,161.67\n\n  Year 14          04/01/2030 -- 03/31/2031   \\$30.04                       \\$174,232.00           \\$14,519.33\n\n  Year 15          04/01/2031 -- 03/31/2032   \\$30.79                       \\$178,582.00           \\$14,881.83\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nAll figures are based on the Premises containing 5,800 rentable square feet and have been rounded to the nearest cent. Base Rent for any partial month shall be prorated on a per diem basis based on the actual number of days in such month.\n\n**[EXHIBIT C]{.underline}**\n\n**[RULES AND REGULATIONS]{.underline}**\n\nThe following Rules and Regulations are applicable to Tenant and all other tenants and occupants of Millbrook Medical Plaza. Landlord reserves the right to amend or supplement these Rules and Regulations from time to time upon reasonable written notice to Tenant, provided that no amendment shall materially and adversely affect Tenant\\'s ability to conduct the Permitted Use.\n\n1\\. **Common Areas.** The sidewalks, entrances, lobbies, corridors, stairways, and elevators of the Building shall not be obstructed or used for any purpose other than ingress to and egress from the Premises and other portions of the Building. Tenant shall not place or permit any furniture, equipment, displays, or other objects in the Common Areas without Landlord\\'s prior written consent.\n\n2\\. **Signage.** Tenant shall not place or install any sign, awning, canopy, or other advertising material on the exterior of the Building or in any Common Area without the prior written approval of Landlord. Landlord shall provide Tenant with standard Building directory signage and suite identification signage at Landlord\\'s expense.\n\n3\\. **Trash and Refuse.** Tenant shall dispose of all trash, garbage, and refuse in the designated dumpster and recycling areas provided by Landlord. Tenant shall not permit the accumulation of trash or refuse on the Premises or in the Common Areas.\n\n4\\. **Parking.** Tenant and Tenant\\'s employees, agents, and invitees shall park only in the areas designated by Landlord. Tenant shall not park in fire lanes, handicapped spaces (unless authorized), or areas reserved for other tenants or visitors.\n\n5\\. **Noise and Disturbances.** Tenant shall not cause or permit any unreasonable noise, odor, or vibration that would disturb other tenants or occupants of the Building.\n\n6\\. **Prohibited Activities.** Tenant shall not use the Premises for any unlawful purpose or in any manner that would constitute a nuisance, increase insurance premiums, or void any insurance coverage on the Building.\n\n7\\. **Hours of Operation.** Landlord acknowledges that, due to the nature of Tenant\\'s childcare operation, Tenant\\'s hours of operation may commence as early as 6:30 a.m. and extend until 6:30 p.m. on weekdays. Landlord shall ensure that Building access, lighting, and climate control systems accommodate Tenant\\'s operating hours.\n\n8\\. **Outdoor Play Areas.** Landlord acknowledges that Tenant\\'s childcare operation requires the use of the ground-floor patio and courtyard area adjacent to Suite 100 as an outdoor play area for children during reasonable daytime hours, subject to Tenant\\'s compliance with all applicable safety, fencing, and noise regulations.\n\n9\\. **Loading and Deliveries.** Deliveries to the Premises shall be made through the designated loading area and service entrance. Tenant shall coordinate delivery schedules with Landlord\\'s property manager to avoid conflicts with other tenants.\n\n10\\. **General Conduct.** Tenant shall ensure that its employees, agents, contractors, and invitees conduct themselves in a professional and courteous manner while on the Property and in the Common Areas.\n\n**[EXHIBIT D]{.underline}**\n\n**[LANDLORD\\'S WORK / TENANT IMPROVEMENTS]{.underline}**\n\n**1. Landlord\\'s Work.**\n\nLandlord shall deliver the Premises to Tenant in shell condition, which shall include the following base building improvements: (a) concrete slab flooring in broom-finished condition; (b) finished exterior walls with insulation and vapor barrier; (c) base building mechanical, electrical, and plumbing systems stubbed to the boundary of the Premises, including electrical panels, water supply, and sanitary sewer connections; (d) fire sprinkler system installed to code and providing coverage throughout the Premises; and (e) one (1) ADA-compliant restroom within the Premises, fully plumbed, tiled, and finished. Landlord\\'s Work shall be completed in a good and workmanlike manner and in compliance with all applicable building codes and regulations. Landlord shall use commercially reasonable efforts to substantially complete Landlord\\'s Work prior to February 1, 2017, to permit Tenant\\'s early access as provided in Section 3.2.\n\n**2. Tenant\\'s Work.**\n\nTenant shall be responsible, at Tenant\\'s sole cost and expense, for all interior build-out and improvements to the Premises necessary for the Permitted Use (collectively, \\\"Tenant\\'s Work\\\"), including but not limited to: interior partitions and walls; flooring and floor coverings; ceiling tiles and grid; interior painting and finishes; electrical wiring, outlets, and lighting fixtures beyond base building provisions; plumbing fixtures and connections beyond the base building restroom; HVAC distribution ductwork and controls within the Premises; childproofing and safety modifications; fencing for the outdoor play area; kitchen and food preparation facilities; and any other specialized improvements required for compliance with North Carolina childcare licensing requirements. All Tenant\\'s Work shall be performed in accordance with plans and specifications approved by Landlord in advance (such approval not to be unreasonably withheld) and in compliance with all applicable laws, codes, and regulations.\n\n**3. Tenant Improvement Allowance.**\n\nNo tenant improvement allowance shall be provided by Landlord. Tenant\\'s Work shall be entirely self-funded by Tenant.\n\n**4. Ownership of Improvements.**\n\nAll improvements constructed or installed in the Premises by or on behalf of Tenant (other than Tenant\\'s trade fixtures, personal property, and movable equipment) shall become the property of Landlord upon installation and shall remain upon and be surrendered with the Premises upon the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease, unless Landlord notifies Tenant in writing at least sixty (60) days prior to the expiration of the Lease Term that Landlord requires the removal of any or all of Tenant\\'s improvements, in which case Tenant shall remove the specified improvements and restore the Premises to their condition prior to installation, reasonable wear and tear excepted, at Tenant\\'s sole cost and expense.\n\n*\\[End of Exhibit D\\]*\n\n*\\[End of Lease Agreement\\]*\n"}
{"turn": 7, "role": "assistant", "text": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_0iMWVIVogRkKl2rZGi3d9233", "name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/workspace/documents/se-fire-safety-lease.docx\"}"}], "input_tokens": 61521, "output_tokens": 2019, "finish_reason": "completed"}
{"turn": 7, "role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "call_00_0iMWVIVogRkKl2rZGi3d9233", "tool_name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/workspace/documents/se-fire-safety-lease.docx\"}", "result": "**[LEASE AGREEMENT]{.underline}**\n\n**This Lease Agreement (this \\\"Lease\\\")** **is entered into as of July 1, 2014**\n\n**by and between**\n\n**THORNFIELD REALTY HOLDINGS LP** *(as \\\"Landlord\\\")*\n\n**and**\n\n**SOUTHEASTERN FIRE & SAFETY EQUIPMENT CO.** *(as \\\"Tenant\\\")*\n\n**Property: Greystone Industrial Park** **770 Greystone Boulevard, Unit 12** **Gastonia, NC 28052**\n\nThis Lease Agreement (this \\\"Lease\\\") is entered into as of July 1, 2014 (the \\\"Effective Date\\\"), by and between:\n\n**LANDLORD:** Thornfield Realty Holdings LP, a North Carolina limited partnership, whose general partner is Thornfield Management Corp., a North Carolina corporation, with its registered office at 500 South Tryon Street, Suite 2200, Charlotte, NC 28202 (\\\"Landlord\\\"); and\n\n**TENANT:** Southeastern Fire & Safety Equipment Co., a North Carolina corporation, with its principal office at 770 Greystone Boulevard, Unit 12, Gastonia, NC 28052 (\\\"Tenant\\\").\n\n**RECITALS**\n\n**WHEREAS**, Landlord is the owner of certain real property and improvements known as Greystone Industrial Park, located in Gastonia, Gaston County, North Carolina, consisting of multiple industrial and flex-space units designed for light industrial, warehouse, and commercial uses; and\n\n**WHEREAS**, Landlord desires to lease to Tenant, and Tenant desires to lease from Landlord, certain premises within Greystone Industrial Park, more particularly described herein, upon the terms and conditions set forth in this Lease; and\n\n**WHEREAS**, the parties wish to memorialize their respective rights and obligations with respect to the leased premises.\n\n**NOW, THEREFORE**, in consideration of the mutual covenants and agreements set forth herein, and for other good and valuable consideration, the receipt and sufficiency of which are hereby acknowledged, the parties agree as follows:\n\n**[ARTICLE 1 --- DEFINITIONS]{.underline}**\n\nAs used in this Lease, the following terms shall have the meanings set forth below:\n\n**1.1 \\\"Premises\\\"** shall mean Unit 12, Greystone Industrial Park, 770 Greystone Boulevard, Gastonia, NC 28052, consisting of approximately twenty-two thousand (22,000) rentable square feet (\\\"RSF\\\") of industrial/flex space, including warehouse area, loading dock with two (2) drive-in bays, and office/flex area, as more particularly depicted on the floor plan attached hereto as **Exhibit A** and incorporated herein by reference.\n\n**1.2 \\\"Building\\\"** shall mean the building in which the Premises are located within Greystone Industrial Park, together with all appurtenant structures, improvements, and fixtures forming a part thereof.\n\n**1.3 \\\"Commencement Date\\\"** shall mean July 1, 2014, which is the date upon which the Term of this Lease shall commence and Tenant\\'s obligation to pay Base Rent shall begin.\n\n**1.4 \\\"Expiration Date\\\"** shall mean June 30, 2024, which is the date upon which the initial Term of this Lease shall expire, unless the Term is sooner terminated or extended in accordance with the provisions of this Lease.\n\n**1.5 \\\"Lease Term\\\" or \\\"Term\\\"** shall mean the period commencing on the Commencement Date and ending on the Expiration Date, unless sooner terminated or extended as provided herein. The Term shall consist of ten (10) Lease Years.\n\n**1.6 \\\"Lease Year\\\"** shall mean each successive twelve (12) month period during the Term. The first Lease Year shall commence on July 1, 2014 and end on June 30, 2015, and each subsequent Lease Year shall commence on the anniversary thereof and end on the day immediately preceding the next anniversary of the Commencement Date.\n\n**1.7 \\\"Base Rent\\\"** shall mean the annual base rental amount payable by Tenant to Landlord during each Lease Year, as set forth in Article 4 and in the Rent Schedule attached hereto as **Exhibit B**.\n\n**1.8 \\\"Common Areas\\\"** shall mean all areas and facilities within Greystone Industrial Park that are designated by Landlord for common use by tenants and their respective employees, agents, customers, and invitees, including without limitation parking areas, driveways, access roads, loading areas, sidewalks, landscaped areas, trash enclosures, and signage areas.\n\n**1.9 \\\"Operating Expenses\\\"** shall mean all costs and expenses of operating, maintaining, repairing, replacing, and managing the Building and Greystone Industrial Park, including but not limited to real estate taxes and assessments, property insurance premiums, utilities for Common Areas, landscaping and grounds maintenance, snow and ice removal, security services, management fees (not to exceed five percent (5%) of gross rental income), common area maintenance and repair, and reasonable reserves for capital replacements.\n\n**1.10 \\\"Hazardous Materials\\\"** shall have the meaning set forth in Article 14 of this Lease.\n\n**1.11 \\\"Applicable Law\\\"** shall mean all federal, state, and local laws, statutes, regulations, rules, ordinances, codes, permits, and orders applicable to the Premises, the Building, Greystone Industrial Park, or Tenant\\'s use and occupancy thereof, including without limitation all environmental laws and regulations, zoning ordinances, building codes, fire codes, and health and safety regulations.\n\n**1.12 \\\"Landlord\\'s Address\\\"** shall mean the address set forth in Article 17 for the delivery of notices to Landlord, or such other address as Landlord may designate from time to time in accordance with Article 17.\n\n**[ARTICLE 2 --- PREMISES]{.underline}**\n\n**2.1** Landlord hereby leases to Tenant, and Tenant hereby leases from Landlord, the Premises described as Unit 12, Greystone Industrial Park, 770 Greystone Boulevard, Gastonia, NC 28052, together with the non-exclusive right to use the Common Areas of Greystone Industrial Park in common with other tenants and occupants, subject to the rules and regulations established by Landlord from time to time.\n\n**2.2** The Premises consist of approximately twenty-two thousand (22,000) rentable square feet of industrial/flex space, as more particularly depicted on the floor plan attached hereto as **Exhibit A** and incorporated herein by reference. The RSF measurement set forth herein shall be conclusive and binding upon both parties for all purposes under this Lease, including without limitation the calculation of Base Rent and Tenant\\'s pro rata share of Operating Expenses.\n\n**2.3** Tenant acknowledges that it has inspected and examined the Premises prior to the execution of this Lease and accepts the Premises in their \\\"as-is\\\" condition as of the Commencement Date, subject to any punch-list items identified by the parties in writing on or before the Commencement Date. Landlord makes no representations or warranties regarding the condition of the Premises, the Building, or Greystone Industrial Park, express or implied, except as expressly set forth in this Lease.\n\n**[ARTICLE 3 --- TERM]{.underline}**\n\n**3.1 Initial Term.** The initial Term of this Lease shall be ten (10) Lease Years, commencing on the Commencement Date (July 1, 2014) and expiring on the Expiration Date (June 30, 2024), unless sooner terminated in accordance with the provisions of this Lease.\n\n**3.2 Renewal Option.** Tenant shall have one (1) option to renew the Term for an additional period of five (5) years (the \\\"Renewal Term\\\"), commencing on the day immediately following the Expiration Date and ending on June 30, 2029, upon all of the same terms and conditions as this Lease, except that Base Rent during the Renewal Term shall be at the then-prevailing fair market rental rate for comparable industrial/flex space in the Gastonia, North Carolina market, as determined in good faith by Landlord and Tenant. If the parties are unable to agree upon the fair market rental rate within thirty (30) days after Tenant\\'s exercise of the Renewal Option, the fair market rental rate shall be determined by a licensed commercial real estate appraiser mutually selected by the parties, the cost of which shall be shared equally.\n\n**3.3 Exercise of Renewal Option.** To exercise the Renewal Option set forth in Section 3.2, Tenant must deliver written notice of its election to renew to Landlord no later than ninety (90) days prior to the Expiration Date (i.e., by no later than April 1, 2024). Time is of the essence with respect to such notice. Failure by Tenant to timely deliver written notice of its election to renew in strict compliance with this Section 3.3 shall be deemed an irrevocable waiver of the Renewal Option, and the Term shall expire on the Expiration Date without further action by either party. The Renewal Option may not be exercised by Tenant if Tenant is in default under this Lease at the time of such exercise or at the time the Renewal Term is to commence.\n\n**[ARTICLE 4 --- BASE RENT]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 4.1 --- Base Rent Schedule**\n\nTenant shall pay to Landlord annual Base Rent in the amounts set forth in this Section 4.1 and as detailed in the Rent Schedule attached hereto as **Exhibit B** and incorporated herein by reference, payable in equal monthly installments in advance on the first (1st) day of each calendar month during the Term. Base Rent shall increase by two percent (2%) per annum on each anniversary of the Commencement Date, as set forth in the Rent Schedule attached hereto as Exhibit B. The Base Rent for each Lease Year shall be as follows:\n\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Lease Year**   **Period**                    **Base Rent per RSF per Year**   **Annual Base Rent**   **Monthly Base Rent**\n  ---------------- ----------------------------- -------------------------------- ---------------------- -----------------------\n  1                Jul 1, 2014 -- Jun 30, 2015   \\$7.50                           \\$165,000.00           \\$13,750.00\n\n  2                Jul 1, 2015 -- Jun 30, 2016   \\$7.65                           \\$168,300.00           \\$14,025.00\n\n  3                Jul 1, 2016 -- Jun 30, 2017   \\$7.80                           \\$171,600.00           \\$14,300.00\n\n  4                Jul 1, 2017 -- Jun 30, 2018   \\$7.96                           \\$175,120.00           \\$14,593.33\n\n  5                Jul 1, 2018 -- Jun 30, 2019   \\$8.12                           \\$178,640.00           \\$14,886.67\n\n  6                Jul 1, 2019 -- Jun 30, 2020   \\$8.28                           \\$182,160.00           \\$15,180.00\n\n  7                Jul 1, 2020 -- Jun 30, 2021   \\$8.45                           \\$185,900.00           \\$15,491.67\n\n  8                Jul 1, 2021 -- Jun 30, 2022   \\$8.62                           \\$189,640.00           \\$15,803.33\n\n  9                Jul 1, 2022 -- Jun 30, 2023   \\$8.79                           \\$193,380.00           \\$16,115.00\n\n  10               Jul 1, 2023 -- Jun 30, 2024   \\$9.25                           \\$203,500.00           \\$16,958.33\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nAll Base Rent amounts are calculated based on 22,000 rentable square feet. In the event of any conflict between the rent amounts stated in this Section 4.1 and those stated in Exhibit B, the amounts stated in Exhibit B shall control.\n\n**Section 4.2 --- Payment**\n\nAll Base Rent and additional rent and other charges payable by Tenant under this Lease shall be due and payable on the first (1st) day of each calendar month, in advance, without notice, demand, deduction, abatement, or offset of any kind, except as expressly provided in this Lease. All payments shall be made in lawful currency of the United States by check or wire transfer to Landlord at Landlord\\'s address set forth in Article 17, or to such other address or account as Landlord may designate from time to time by written notice to Tenant. If any payment of Base Rent or additional rent is not received by Landlord within five (5) days after its due date, Tenant shall pay a late charge equal to five percent (5%) of the overdue amount, which the parties agree represents a fair and reasonable estimate of the costs Landlord will incur as a result of such late payment. In addition, all amounts overdue by more than five (5) days shall bear interest from the due date until paid at the rate of one and one-half percent (1.5%) per month, or the maximum rate permitted by applicable North Carolina law, whichever is less.\n\n**Section 4.3 --- Proration**\n\nIf the Commencement Date or the Expiration Date falls on a day other than the first or last day of a calendar month, respectively, Base Rent and additional rent for such partial month shall be prorated on a per diem basis based on the actual number of days in such calendar month. The parties acknowledge that, as of the Effective Date, the Commencement Date of July 1, 2014 and the Expiration Date of June 30, 2024 each align with the first and last days of a calendar month, respectively, and accordingly no proration is anticipated at this time.\n\n**[ARTICLE 5 --- TRIPLE NET LEASE; OPERATING EXPENSES]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 5.1 --- NNN Lease**\n\nThe parties acknowledge and agree that this Lease is intended to be and shall be construed as a triple net lease (\\\"NNN\\\"). Accordingly, in addition to Base Rent, Tenant shall be responsible for and shall pay, as additional rent, Tenant\\'s pro rata share of all Operating Expenses, real estate taxes and assessments, and insurance premiums attributable to the Premises and the Building, as more particularly described in this Article 5. The Base Rent set forth in Article 4 shall be absolutely net to Landlord, and all costs, expenses, and obligations of every kind relating to the Premises which may arise or become due during the Term shall be paid by Tenant, except as otherwise expressly provided herein.\n\n**Section 5.2 --- Pro Rata Share**\n\nTenant\\'s pro rata share of Operating Expenses, real estate taxes, and insurance premiums shall be determined based on the ratio of the rentable square footage of the Premises (22,000 RSF) to the total rentable square footage of the Building or, where applicable, Greystone Industrial Park as a whole, as reasonably determined by Landlord. Landlord shall provide Tenant with a written statement of Tenant\\'s pro rata share upon commencement of the Term and shall notify Tenant of any change in such pro rata share resulting from re-measurement or changes in the total rentable area of the Building or Greystone Industrial Park.\n\n**Section 5.3 --- Real Estate Taxes**\n\nTenant shall pay its pro rata share of all real estate taxes, ad valorem taxes, special assessments, and governmental charges of every kind levied against the Building and/or the land on which Greystone Industrial Park is situated. Landlord shall provide Tenant with copies of applicable tax bills and reasonable supporting documentation upon Tenant\\'s written request. In the event Landlord elects to contest any real estate tax assessment, Tenant shall pay its pro rata share of the reasonable costs of such contest.\n\n**Section 5.4 --- Insurance**\n\nTenant shall pay its pro rata share of all premiums for property insurance, casualty insurance, and any other insurance maintained by Landlord on the Building and Common Areas of Greystone Industrial Park, as part of Operating Expenses.\n\n**Section 5.5 --- Estimated Payments; Reconciliation**\n\nLandlord may, at the beginning of each calendar year or Lease Year, estimate the total Operating Expenses for such year and collect from Tenant, as additional rent, one-twelfth (1/12) of Tenant\\'s estimated pro rata share on a monthly basis, together with each monthly Base Rent payment. Within ninety (90) days after the end of each calendar year, Landlord shall furnish Tenant with a reasonably detailed statement of actual Operating Expenses for such year and Tenant\\'s pro rata share thereof. If Tenant\\'s payments on account of estimated Operating Expenses exceed Tenant\\'s actual pro rata share, Landlord shall refund the overpayment to Tenant within thirty (30) days. If Tenant\\'s payments are less than Tenant\\'s actual pro rata share, Tenant shall pay the shortfall to Landlord within thirty (30) days of receipt of such statement.\n\n**[ARTICLE 6 --- PERMITTED USE]{.underline}**\n\n**6.1** Tenant shall use and occupy the Premises solely for the purposes of light industrial operations, warehousing, equipment storage, equipment testing, and the service, repair, maintenance, and reconditioning of fire safety equipment, fire extinguishers, fire suppression systems, and related safety apparatus, and for general office use incidental thereto, and for no other purpose without the prior written consent of Landlord, which consent shall not be unreasonably withheld, conditioned, or delayed.\n\n**6.2** Tenant shall comply with all Applicable Laws in connection with its use and occupancy of the Premises, including without limitation all applicable zoning ordinances, building codes, fire codes, health and safety regulations, and environmental laws and regulations, and shall obtain and maintain all permits and licenses required for the conduct of its business on the Premises.\n\n**6.3** Tenant shall not use or occupy the Premises in any manner that would (a) increase the rate of or cause cancellation of any insurance policy maintained by Landlord on the Building, (b) constitute a nuisance or unreasonable disturbance to other tenants of Greystone Industrial Park, (c) violate any certificate of occupancy for the Premises or the Building, or (d) cause structural damage to the Building.\n\n**[ARTICLE 7 --- INSURANCE]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 7.1 --- Tenant\\'s Insurance Obligations**\n\nTenant shall maintain at all times during the Term, at Tenant\\'s sole cost and expense, the following insurance coverage:\n\n> \\(a\\) **Commercial General Liability Insurance.** Commercial general liability insurance covering claims for bodily injury, death, personal injury, and property damage arising out of Tenant\\'s use and occupancy of the Premises, with combined single limits of not less than Two Million Dollars (\\$2,000,000) per occurrence and Five Million Dollars (\\$5,000,000) in the aggregate per policy year.\n>\n> \\(b\\) **Pollution Legal Liability Insurance.** Pollution legal liability insurance with limits of not less than One Million Dollars (\\$1,000,000) per occurrence, covering claims arising from the storage, handling, use, transportation, and disposal of fire suppressant chemicals, aqueous film-forming foam (\\\"AFFF\\\"), foam concentrates, and related materials on or about the Premises.\n>\n> \\(c\\) **Property Insurance.** All-risk property insurance covering Tenant\\'s personal property, equipment, inventory, trade fixtures, and leasehold improvements in the Premises for their full replacement value.\n>\n> \\(d\\) **Workers\\' Compensation Insurance.** Workers\\' compensation insurance as required by the laws of the State of North Carolina, and employer\\'s liability insurance with limits of not less than Five Hundred Thousand Dollars (\\$500,000) per accident.\n\nAll insurance policies required under this Section 7.1 shall name Landlord, Thornfield Realty Holdings LP, Thornfield Management Corp., and their respective partners, officers, directors, employees, and agents as additional insureds (except for workers\\' compensation insurance). All policies shall be written on an occurrence basis (not claims-made) by insurers licensed to do business in the State of North Carolina with an A.M. Best rating of not less than A-VII. Certificates of insurance evidencing the required coverage shall be delivered to Landlord prior to the Commencement Date and annually thereafter not later than ten (10) days prior to the expiration of each policy. Each policy shall provide that it may not be canceled or materially modified without at least thirty (30) days\\' prior written notice to Landlord.\n\n**Section 7.2 --- Landlord\\'s Insurance**\n\nLandlord shall maintain property insurance on the Building structure (excluding Tenant\\'s personal property, equipment, and trade fixtures) and commercial general liability insurance covering the Common Areas of Greystone Industrial Park. The cost of such insurance shall be included in Operating Expenses and allocated to Tenant in accordance with Article 5.\n\n**Section 7.3 --- Waiver of Subrogation**\n\nLandlord and Tenant each hereby waive any right of recovery against the other party, and against the officers, directors, partners, employees, and agents of the other party, for any loss or damage to the extent that such loss or damage is covered by valid and collectible insurance in effect at the time of such loss or damage, or would have been covered by the insurance required to be maintained under this Lease. Each party shall cause its insurance carriers to include appropriate waiver of subrogation endorsements in all policies carried by such party in connection with the Premises and the Building.\n\n**[ARTICLE 8 --- MAINTENANCE AND REPAIRS]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 8.1 --- Tenant\\'s Obligations**\n\nTenant shall, at Tenant\\'s sole cost and expense, maintain the interior of the Premises in good condition, order, and repair throughout the Term, including without limitation all interior walls, floors, ceilings, doors, windows, plumbing fixtures and systems, electrical systems and wiring, lighting, interior fire suppression systems, and all HVAC equipment and systems serving the Premises. Tenant shall be responsible for all HVAC maintenance, including regular preventive maintenance service, filter replacement, coil cleaning, and all necessary repairs and replacements. Tenant shall maintain a service contract with a licensed HVAC contractor approved by Landlord (such approval not to be unreasonably withheld) and shall provide evidence of such service contract to Landlord upon request. Tenant shall keep all loading docks, bay doors, and the parking area dedicated to Unit 12 in clean, orderly, and safe condition, free of debris, obstructions, and environmental contamination.\n\n**Section 8.2 --- Landlord\\'s Obligations**\n\nLandlord shall be responsible for the maintenance, repair, and replacement of the roof and structural elements of the Building, including the foundation, load-bearing walls, structural columns, and exterior walls (excluding glass, windows, and doors), provided that Landlord shall not be required to make any repairs or replacements necessitated by the negligence, willful misconduct, or misuse by Tenant, its employees, agents, contractors, or invitees. Landlord shall also maintain and repair Common Area infrastructure of Greystone Industrial Park, including parking lots, access drives, landscaping, exterior signage, stormwater drainage systems, and exterior lighting. The cost of such maintenance and repair shall be included in Operating Expenses and allocated in accordance with Article 5.\n\n**[ARTICLE 9 --- ASSIGNMENT AND SUBLETTING]{.underline}**\n\n**9.1** Tenant shall not assign, transfer, mortgage, pledge, hypothecate, or encumber this Lease or any interest therein, and shall not sublet the Premises or any part thereof, or grant any concession or license for the use of any portion of the Premises, without the prior written consent of Landlord, which consent may be withheld in Landlord\\'s sole and absolute discretion. Any attempted assignment, subletting, or other transfer without Landlord\\'s prior written consent shall be void and of no force or effect and shall constitute an Event of Default under this Lease.\n\n**9.2** No assignment or subletting, whether with or without Landlord\\'s consent, shall relieve Tenant of any of its obligations under this Lease, and Tenant shall remain primarily liable for the full and faithful performance of all covenants, conditions, and obligations of Tenant hereunder.\n\n**9.3** In the event of any permitted assignment or subletting, any sublease rent, assignment consideration, or other amounts received by Tenant from the assignee or subtenant in excess of the Base Rent and additional rent then payable by Tenant under this Lease (after deducting Tenant\\'s reasonable brokerage commissions and legal fees incurred in connection with such assignment or subletting) shall be paid over to Landlord promptly upon receipt.\n\n**9.4** Landlord\\'s consent to any one assignment or subletting shall not be deemed consent to any subsequent assignment or subletting, and each proposed transfer shall require a separate and independent consent.\n\n**[ARTICLE 10 --- ALTERATIONS AND IMPROVEMENTS]{.underline}**\n\n**10.1** Tenant shall not make any structural alterations, additions, or improvements to the Premises without Landlord\\'s prior written consent, which consent may be granted or withheld in Landlord\\'s reasonable discretion. All requests for consent to structural alterations shall be accompanied by detailed plans and specifications prepared by a licensed architect or engineer.\n\n**10.2** Non-structural alterations, additions, or improvements with an estimated cost of less than Twenty-Five Thousand Dollars (\\$25,000) may be made by Tenant without Landlord\\'s prior written consent, provided that Tenant delivers written notice to Landlord describing the scope and estimated cost of such work at least fifteen (15) days in advance of commencing such work. All alterations shall be performed in a good and workmanlike manner, in compliance with all Applicable Laws, and shall not adversely affect the structural integrity or systems of the Building.\n\n**10.3** All alterations, additions, and improvements made to the Premises by Tenant (excluding Tenant\\'s trade fixtures, equipment, and movable personal property) shall become the property of Landlord upon installation and shall remain upon the Premises at the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease, unless Landlord elects to require their removal by written notice delivered to Tenant not later than thirty (30) days prior to the Expiration Date. Tenant\\'s trade fixtures, equipment, and movable personal property shall remain the property of Tenant and shall be removed by Tenant upon the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease, provided that Tenant shall repair any damage to the Premises caused by such removal.\n\n**[ARTICLE 11 --- INDEMNIFICATION AND LIABILITY]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 11.1 --- Tenant\\'s Indemnification**\n\nTenant shall indemnify, defend (with counsel reasonably acceptable to Landlord), and hold harmless Landlord and its partners, members, officers, directors, agents, employees, contractors, successors, and assigns from and against any and all claims, demands, actions, causes of action, losses, damages, liabilities, costs, and expenses (including reasonable attorneys\\' fees and court costs) arising from or related to: (a) Tenant\\'s use and occupancy of the Premises; (b) any act, omission, negligence, or willful misconduct of Tenant, its employees, agents, contractors, licensees, or invitees in or about the Premises, the Building, or Greystone Industrial Park; (c) any breach or default by Tenant under this Lease; or (d) any environmental contamination of the Premises, the Building, Greystone Industrial Park, or any adjacent or nearby property caused by or attributable to Tenant\\'s storage, use, handling, release, or disposal of Hazardous Materials, as more particularly described in Article 14. This indemnification obligation shall survive the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease.\n\n**Section 11.2 --- Landlord\\'s Indemnification**\n\nLandlord shall indemnify, defend, and hold harmless Tenant and its officers, directors, employees, and agents from and against any and all claims, losses, damages, liabilities, costs, and expenses (including reasonable attorneys\\' fees) arising from Landlord\\'s negligence or willful misconduct in connection with Landlord\\'s performance of its obligations under this Lease, except to the extent such claims arise from the negligence or willful misconduct of Tenant.\n\n**Section 11.3 --- Limitation of Liability**\n\nIn no event shall Landlord\\'s liability under this Lease exceed Landlord\\'s equity interest in the Building and the proceeds thereof. Tenant shall look solely to Landlord\\'s interest in the Building for the satisfaction of any claim or judgment against Landlord, and no other property or assets of Landlord, its partners, members, officers, directors, or agents shall be subject to levy, execution, or other enforcement procedure for the satisfaction of Tenant\\'s remedies. In no event shall either party be liable to the other for consequential, incidental, special, or punitive damages in connection with this Lease.\n\n**[ARTICLE 12 --- DEFAULT AND REMEDIES]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 12.1 --- Events of Default**\n\nEach of the following shall constitute an \\\"Event of Default\\\" under this Lease:\n\n> \\(a\\) Tenant fails to pay Base Rent, additional rent, or any other sum due under this Lease within five (5) days after the date on which such payment is due;\n>\n> \\(b\\) Tenant fails to perform or observe any other covenant, condition, or obligation of Tenant under this Lease and such failure continues for thirty (30) days after Landlord delivers written notice thereof to Tenant, provided that if such default is of a nature that it cannot reasonably be cured within thirty (30) days, Tenant shall not be in default if Tenant commences the cure within such thirty (30) day period and diligently and continuously pursues the cure to completion;\n>\n> \\(c\\) Tenant files a voluntary petition in bankruptcy or is adjudicated bankrupt or insolvent, or makes a general assignment for the benefit of creditors, or a receiver or trustee is appointed for all or substantially all of Tenant\\'s assets, or any proceeding is filed by or against Tenant under any bankruptcy or insolvency law and is not dismissed within sixty (60) days;\n>\n> \\(d\\) Tenant abandons the Premises or vacates the Premises for a period of thirty (30) or more consecutive days without Landlord\\'s prior written consent; or\n>\n> \\(e\\) Any representation or warranty made by Tenant in this Lease is materially false or misleading when made.\n\n**Section 12.2 --- Landlord\\'s Remedies**\n\nUpon the occurrence of any Event of Default, Landlord shall have the right, at Landlord\\'s sole election, to exercise one or more of the following remedies:\n\n> \\(a\\) Terminate this Lease by delivering written notice of termination to Tenant, whereupon Tenant\\'s right to possession of the Premises shall cease and Tenant shall immediately surrender the Premises to Landlord;\n>\n> \\(b\\) Re-enter and take possession of the Premises, with or without terminating this Lease, and remove Tenant and all persons and property therefrom, in accordance with applicable North Carolina law;\n>\n> \\(c\\) Recover from Tenant all damages incurred by Landlord as a result of such default, including without limitation all unpaid Base Rent and additional rent accrued through the date of termination, the present value of all Base Rent and additional rent that would have been payable during the remainder of the Term (discounted at the rate of six percent (6%) per annum), all costs of re-letting the Premises (including brokerage commissions, tenant improvement costs, and advertising expenses), and all other damages recoverable by Landlord at law or in equity; and\n>\n> \\(d\\) Exercise any other remedies available to Landlord at law or in equity, including the right to seek specific performance or injunctive relief.\n\nLandlord\\'s remedies under this Lease are cumulative and not exclusive, and the exercise of any one remedy shall not preclude the exercise of any other remedy.\n\n**Section 12.3 --- Tenant\\'s Remedies**\n\nIf Landlord defaults in the performance of any obligation of Landlord under this Lease, Tenant shall deliver written notice of such default to Landlord specifying the nature of the default in reasonable detail, and Landlord shall have thirty (30) days after receipt of such notice within which to cure such default (or, if the default is of a nature that cannot reasonably be cured within thirty (30) days, such longer period as is reasonably necessary, provided Landlord commences the cure within such thirty (30) day period and diligently pursues the same to completion). If Landlord fails to cure such default within the applicable cure period, Tenant may pursue any remedies available at law or in equity; provided, however, that in no event shall Tenant have the right to terminate this Lease or to withhold, abate, or offset any rent or other amounts payable under this Lease on account of any Landlord default, except as expressly permitted by applicable North Carolina law.\n\n**[ARTICLE 13 --- HOLDOVER]{.underline}**\n\nIf Tenant remains in possession of the Premises after the Expiration Date or earlier termination of this Lease without Landlord\\'s prior written consent, Tenant shall be deemed a holdover tenant on a month-to-month basis, subject to all of the terms and conditions of this Lease, except as modified by this Article 13. During any holdover period, Tenant shall pay holdover rent equal to one hundred fifty percent (150%) of the Base Rent payable during the last month of the Term. Based on the final month\\'s Base Rent of \\$16,958.33 per month, holdover rent shall be \\$25,437.50 per month, payable in advance on the first day of each calendar month during the holdover period, together with all additional rent and other charges payable under this Lease.\n\nAll other terms and conditions of this Lease shall continue to apply during any holdover period, except that the holdover tenancy may be terminated by either party upon thirty (30) days\\' prior written notice to the other party. Landlord\\'s acceptance of holdover rent shall not constitute a consent to any renewal or extension of the Term and shall not waive Landlord\\'s right to terminate the holdover tenancy or to recover possession of the Premises at any time. Tenant shall be liable for all damages, costs, and expenses incurred by Landlord as a result of Tenant\\'s holdover occupancy, including without limitation any loss of prospective tenants or rental income.\n\n**[ARTICLE 14 --- HAZARDOUS MATERIALS AND ENVIRONMENTAL PROVISIONS]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 14.1 --- Definitions**\n\nAs used in this Lease, \\\"Hazardous Materials\\\" shall mean any substance, chemical, material, compound, mixture, or waste that is regulated by, or forms the basis of liability under, any federal, state, or local environmental law, regulation, ordinance, rule, or order, including but not limited to: (a) substances defined as \\\"hazardous substances,\\\" \\\"hazardous materials,\\\" \\\"hazardous wastes,\\\" or \\\"toxic substances\\\" under the Comprehensive Environmental Response, Compensation, and Liability Act (\\\"CERCLA\\\") (42 U.S.C. § 9601 et seq.), the Resource Conservation and Recovery Act (\\\"RCRA\\\") (42 U.S.C. § 6901 et seq.), the Toxic Substances Control Act (15 U.S.C. § 2601 et seq.), or any state equivalent thereof, including the North Carolina Hazardous Waste Management Act and the North Carolina Oil Pollution and Hazardous Substances Control Act; (b) petroleum and petroleum products, including crude oil and any fraction thereof; (c) polychlorinated biphenyls (\\\"PCBs\\\"); (d) per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances (\\\"PFAS\\\"), including without limitation perfluorooctanoic acid (\\\"PFOA\\\") and perfluorooctane sulfonate (\\\"PFOS\\\"); (e) aqueous film-forming foam (\\\"AFFF\\\") and fire suppressant concentrates containing fluorinated surfactants; and (f) any other substance that is classified as hazardous, toxic, or dangerous under any Applicable Law.\n\n**Section 14.2 --- Permitted Use of Hazardous Materials**\n\nNotwithstanding any other provision of this Lease restricting the use of Hazardous Materials on the Premises, Tenant shall be permitted to store, handle, use, and transport fire suppressant chemicals, including aqueous film-forming foam (\\\"AFFF\\\"), foam concentrates, dry chemical agents, and related fire safety materials, on the Premises in connection with Tenant\\'s business operations as described in Article 6, provided that such storage, handling, use, and transportation shall at all times be conducted in strict compliance with all Applicable Laws, including without limitation all federal, state, and local environmental laws and regulations governing the storage, handling, use, transportation, and disposal of Hazardous Materials, PFAS, and AFFF. Tenant shall obtain and maintain all permits, licenses, and approvals required by any governmental authority for the storage, handling, use, and disposal of such materials on the Premises. Tenant shall maintain a current and accurate inventory of all Hazardous Materials stored on the Premises and shall provide a copy of such inventory to Landlord upon Landlord\\'s written request, but in any event not less than annually. Tenant shall also provide Landlord with copies of all Material Safety Data Sheets (\\\"MSDS\\\") or Safety Data Sheets (\\\"SDS\\\") for all Hazardous Materials stored or used on the Premises, promptly upon Landlord\\'s request.\n\n**Section 14.3 --- Tenant\\'s Environmental Indemnification**\n\nTenant shall indemnify, defend (with counsel reasonably acceptable to Landlord), and hold harmless Landlord and its partners, officers, directors, agents, employees, successors, and assigns from and against any and all claims, demands, actions, causes of action, losses, damages, liabilities, penalties, fines, costs, and expenses (including without limitation reasonable attorneys\\' fees, environmental consultant and engineering fees, investigation costs, and remediation and cleanup costs) arising from or related to any environmental contamination of the Premises, the Building, Greystone Industrial Park, or any adjacent, nearby, or downgradient property, to the extent caused by or attributable to Tenant\\'s storage, use, handling, generation, release, discharge, spill, or disposal of Hazardous Materials, including without limitation AFFF, foam concentrates, and PFAS-containing materials. This environmental indemnification obligation shall survive the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease and shall not be limited by any other indemnification provisions set forth herein.\n\n**Section 14.4 --- Remediation**\n\nIf, at any time during the Term or after the expiration or termination of this Lease, any release, discharge, spill, or contamination of Hazardous Materials is discovered on, under, or about the Premises, the Building, or Greystone Industrial Park that was caused by or attributable to Tenant, Tenant\\'s employees, agents, contractors, or invitees, Tenant shall promptly, at Tenant\\'s sole cost and expense, investigate, remediate, and clean up such contamination to the satisfaction of all applicable governmental authorities having jurisdiction and to the reasonable satisfaction of Landlord, in accordance with all Applicable Laws. Landlord shall have the right, at Landlord\\'s cost, to conduct environmental assessments, inspections, and testing of the Premises at any reasonable time during the Term upon reasonable prior notice to Tenant; provided, however, that if any such assessment reveals contamination attributable to Tenant, Tenant shall reimburse Landlord for the reasonable cost of such assessment within thirty (30) days of Landlord\\'s written demand therefor.\n\n**Section 14.5 --- Compliance**\n\nTenant shall comply with all environmental laws and regulations applicable to Tenant\\'s operations on the Premises throughout the Term, including without limitation all laws and regulations governing PFAS, AFFF, fire suppressant chemicals, and the storage, handling, use, and disposal thereof, as the same may be enacted, amended, or modified from time to time. Tenant shall promptly notify Landlord in writing of any known or suspected release of Hazardous Materials on or from the Premises, any notice of violation received from any governmental authority, or any environmental claim, action, or proceeding relating to the Premises.\n\n**[ARTICLE 15 --- CASUALTY AND CONDEMNATION]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 15.1 --- Casualty**\n\nIf the Premises are damaged or destroyed by fire or other casualty, Landlord shall promptly notify Tenant of Landlord\\'s good-faith estimate of the time required to restore the Premises. If the damage is such that the Premises are rendered substantially untenantable, Landlord may, at Landlord\\'s election, either (a) restore the Premises to substantially the same condition as existed immediately prior to the casualty, using commercially reasonable efforts to complete such restoration within one hundred eighty (180) days after the date of the casualty, or (b) terminate this Lease by delivering written notice of termination to Tenant within sixty (60) days after the date of the casualty. If Landlord elects to restore and restoration cannot reasonably be completed within one hundred eighty (180) days, either party may terminate this Lease by written notice to the other delivered within thirty (30) days after the expiration of such one hundred eighty (180) day period. During any period in which the Premises are untenantable as a result of casualty damage, Base Rent and additional rent shall abate in proportion to the area of the Premises rendered untenantable.\n\n**Section 15.2 --- Condemnation**\n\nIf all or substantially all of the Premises are taken by eminent domain or condemnation by any governmental authority, this Lease shall terminate as of the date of such taking and all rent shall be apportioned as of such date. If a partial taking of the Premises does not render the remaining portion of the Premises unsuitable for Tenant\\'s continued use, this Lease shall continue in effect with a proportionate reduction in Base Rent corresponding to the area taken. All condemnation awards and proceeds shall belong exclusively to Landlord, provided that Tenant shall have the right to make a separate claim against the condemning authority for the value of Tenant\\'s trade fixtures and personal property and for Tenant\\'s reasonable relocation expenses, to the extent such separate claim does not diminish the award otherwise payable to Landlord.\n\n**[ARTICLE 16 --- SUBORDINATION]{.underline}**\n\nThis Lease shall be subject and subordinate to any mortgage, deed of trust, ground lease, or other lien or encumbrance now existing or hereafter placed upon the Building, the land on which the Building is situated, or Greystone Industrial Park, and to all renewals, modifications, consolidations, replacements, and extensions thereof. This subordination shall be self-operative, and no further instrument shall be required to effect such subordination. Notwithstanding the foregoing, Tenant agrees to execute promptly such documents as may be reasonably required by Landlord or any mortgagee or beneficiary under any deed of trust to evidence or confirm such subordination, and Tenant hereby irrevocably appoints Landlord as Tenant\\'s attorney-in-fact to execute any such document on Tenant\\'s behalf if Tenant fails to do so within ten (10) days after written request.\n\n**[ARTICLE 17 --- NOTICES]{.underline}**\n\nAll notices, demands, consents, approvals, requests, and other communications required or permitted under this Lease shall be in writing and shall be deemed duly given and received if delivered by any of the following methods:\n\n> \\(a\\) Personal delivery, in which case notice shall be deemed effective upon delivery;\n>\n> \\(b\\) Nationally recognized overnight courier service (such as FedEx, UPS, or DHL), in which case notice shall be deemed effective on the next business day following deposit with such courier; or\n>\n> \\(c\\) Certified mail, return receipt requested, postage prepaid, deposited in the United States mail, in which case notice shall be deemed effective three (3) business days after deposit.\n\nNotices shall be addressed as follows:\n\n**If to Landlord:**\n\n> Thornfield Realty Holdings LP\n>\n> Attn: Property Manager\n>\n> 500 South Tryon Street, Suite 2200 Charlotte, NC 28202\n\n**If to Tenant:**\n\n> Southeastern Fire & Safety Equipment Co. 770 Greystone Boulevard, Unit 12 Gastonia, NC 28052\n\nEither party may change its address for notices by delivering written notice of such change to the other party in accordance with this Article 17.\n\n**[ARTICLE 18 --- MISCELLANEOUS]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 18.1 --- Entire Agreement**\n\nThis Lease, together with all Exhibits attached hereto and incorporated herein by reference, constitutes the entire agreement between the parties with respect to the subject matter hereof and supersedes all prior and contemporaneous agreements, representations, warranties, and understandings, whether oral or written, between the parties relating to the Premises or the leasing thereof. No prior oral or written agreement, representation, or understanding shall have any force or effect.\n\n**Section 18.2 --- Amendments**\n\nThis Lease may not be amended, modified, supplemented, or waived except by a written instrument executed by both Landlord and Tenant. No oral agreement or course of dealing shall operate as an amendment or modification of this Lease.\n\n**Section 18.3 --- Governing Law**\n\nThis Lease shall be governed by, construed under, and enforced in accordance with the laws of the State of North Carolina, without regard to its conflicts of law principles. The parties consent to the exclusive jurisdiction and venue of the state and federal courts located in Gaston County or Mecklenburg County, North Carolina, for any dispute arising under or in connection with this Lease.\n\n**Section 18.4 --- Severability**\n\nIf any provision of this Lease is held by a court of competent jurisdiction to be invalid, illegal, void, or unenforceable, such provision shall be modified to the minimum extent necessary to make it valid and enforceable, or if it cannot be so modified, it shall be severed from this Lease, and the remaining provisions of this Lease shall continue in full force and effect and shall not be affected or impaired thereby.\n\n**Section 18.5 --- Binding Effect**\n\nThis Lease shall be binding upon and inure to the benefit of the parties hereto and their respective heirs, executors, administrators, legal representatives, successors, and permitted assigns. Nothing in this Lease shall be construed to confer any rights or benefits upon any person or entity other than the parties hereto and their respective successors and permitted assigns.\n\n**Section 18.6 --- No Waiver**\n\nNo failure or delay by either party in exercising any right, power, or remedy under this Lease shall operate as a waiver thereof. No single or partial exercise of any right, power, or remedy shall preclude any other or further exercise thereof or the exercise of any other right, power, or remedy. No waiver of any provision of this Lease shall be effective unless made in writing and signed by the party to be charged with such waiver.\n\n**Section 18.7 --- Quiet Enjoyment**\n\nLandlord covenants and agrees that, so long as Tenant is not in default under this Lease beyond any applicable notice and cure period, Tenant shall peaceably and quietly hold, occupy, and enjoy the Premises during the Term, free from interference by Landlord or any person claiming by, through, or under Landlord, subject to the terms and conditions of this Lease and to any mortgage, deed of trust, or other encumbrance to which this Lease is subordinate.\n\n**Section 18.8 --- Broker**\n\nEach party hereby represents and warrants to the other that it has not dealt with any real estate broker, finder, or agent in connection with this Lease or the negotiation thereof, and each party shall indemnify and hold harmless the other from and against any claim for brokerage commission, finder\\'s fee, or similar compensation arising from a breach of such representation.\n\n**Section 18.9 --- Counterparts**\n\nThis Lease may be executed in two or more counterparts, each of which shall be deemed an original and all of which together shall constitute one and the same instrument. Facsimile and electronic signatures shall be deemed original signatures for all purposes.\n\n**IN WITNESS WHEREOF**, the parties have executed this Lease Agreement as of the date first above written.\n\n**LANDLORD:**\n\nTHORNFIELD REALTY HOLDINGS LP, a North Carolina limited partnership\n\nBy: Thornfield Management Corp.,\n\nits General Partner\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: Renata Thornfield-Kiess\n\nTitle: President\n\nDate: July 1, 2014\n\n**TENANT:**\n\nSOUTHEASTERN FIRE & SAFETY EQUIPMENT CO., a North Carolina corporation\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nTitle: President\n\nDate: July 1, 2014\n\n**[EXHIBIT A]{.underline}**\n\n**[FLOOR PLAN / SITE PLAN OF PREMISES]{.underline}**\n\n**Unit 12, Greystone Industrial Park** **770 Greystone Boulevard, Gastonia, NC 28052**\n\nThe Premises consist of approximately twenty-two thousand (22,000) rentable square feet of industrial/flex space within Greystone Industrial Park, including warehouse area, loading dock with two (2) drive-in bays, and office/flex area, as depicted on the site plan and floor plan below.\n\n*\\[See attached site plan --- not reproduced in this document.\\]*\n\nThe floor plan and site plan attached to this Exhibit A are incorporated into and made a part of the Lease Agreement by reference. In the event of any discrepancy between the floor plan and the description of the Premises set forth in the body of the Lease, the description set forth in the body of the Lease shall control.\n\n**[EXHIBIT B]{.underline}**\n\n**[RENT SCHEDULE]{.underline}**\n\n**Base Rent Schedule --- 2% Annual Escalation**\n\nBase Rent shall escalate by two percent (2%) per annum on each anniversary of the Commencement Date. The Base Rent payable during each Lease Year of the Term is set forth in the following schedule, based on 22,000 rentable square feet:\n\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Lease Year**   **Period**                    **Base Rent per RSF per Year**   **Annual Base Rent**   **Monthly Base Rent**\n  ---------------- ----------------------------- -------------------------------- ---------------------- -----------------------\n  1                Jul 1, 2014 -- Jun 30, 2015   \\$7.50                           \\$165,000.00           \\$13,750.00\n\n  2                Jul 1, 2015 -- Jun 30, 2016   \\$7.65                           \\$168,300.00           \\$14,025.00\n\n  3                Jul 1, 2016 -- Jun 30, 2017   \\$7.80                           \\$171,600.00           \\$14,300.00\n\n  4                Jul 1, 2017 -- Jun 30, 2018   \\$7.96                           \\$175,120.00           \\$14,593.33\n\n  5                Jul 1, 2018 -- Jun 30, 2019   \\$8.12                           \\$178,640.00           \\$14,886.67\n\n  6                Jul 1, 2019 -- Jun 30, 2020   \\$8.28                           \\$182,160.00           \\$15,180.00\n\n  7                Jul 1, 2020 -- Jun 30, 2021   \\$8.45                           \\$185,900.00           \\$15,491.67\n\n  8                Jul 1, 2021 -- Jun 30, 2022   \\$8.62                           \\$189,640.00           \\$15,803.33\n\n  9                Jul 1, 2022 -- Jun 30, 2023   \\$8.79                           \\$193,380.00           \\$16,115.00\n\n  10               Jul 1, 2023 -- Jun 30, 2024   \\$9.25                           \\$203,500.00           \\$16,958.33\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nAll Base Rent amounts set forth above are calculated based on twenty-two thousand (22,000) rentable square feet and are payable in equal monthly installments in advance on the first (1st) day of each calendar month during the applicable Lease Year.\n\n**Holdover:** In the event Tenant holds over beyond the Expiration Date, holdover rent shall be one hundred fifty percent (150%) of the monthly Base Rent payable during the final Lease Year, as set forth in Article 13 of the Lease. Based on the Year 10 monthly Base Rent of \\$16,958.33, holdover rent shall be \\$25,437.50 per month.\n\nThis Exhibit B is attached to and forms an integral part of the Lease Agreement dated July 1, 2014 between Thornfield Realty Holdings LP, as Landlord, and Southeastern Fire & Safety Equipment Co., as Tenant.\n"}
