{"turn": 8, "role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "call_00_RTldiri9bEtrOKGOU0ih7243", "tool_name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/workspace/documents/verdana-software-lease.docx\"}", "result": "**[OFFICE LEASE AGREEMENT]{.underline}**\n\n**by and between**\n\n**THORNFIELD REALTY HOLDINGS LP** a North Carolina Limited Partnership (\\\"Landlord\\\")\n\n**and**\n\n**VERDANA SOFTWARE SOLUTIONS INC.** a Delaware Corporation Qualified to Do Business in Georgia (\\\"Tenant\\\")\n\n**Property:** Concord Office Tower 300 Concord Plaza Drive Atlanta, Georgia 30309\n\n**Lease Date: February 28, 2022**\n\n**[ARTICLE I --- BASIC LEASE INFORMATION]{.underline}**\n\nThe following is a summary of certain basic lease terms and definitions used in this Office Lease Agreement (the \\\"Lease\\\"). Each term below shall have the meaning set forth in this Article I, subject to the more detailed provisions of the Article or Exhibit referenced. In the event of any conflict between this Article I and the detailed provisions of any other Article or Exhibit, the more detailed provision shall control.\n\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Item**                                **Description**\n  --------------------------------------- ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Lease Date:**                         February 28, 2022\n\n  **Landlord:**                           Thornfield Realty Holdings LP, a North Carolina limited partnership. General Partner: Thornfield Management Corp., a North Carolina corporation. Address: 500 South Tryon Street, Suite 2200, Charlotte, NC 28202.\n\n  **Tenant:**                             Verdana Software Solutions Inc., a Delaware corporation qualified to do business in the State of Georgia. Address: 300 Concord Plaza Drive, 8th Floor, Atlanta, GA 30309.\n\n  **Property/Building:**                  Concord Office Tower, 300 Concord Plaza Drive, Atlanta, GA 30309 (the \\\"Building\\\").\n\n  **Premises:**                           The entire 8th Floor of the Building, consisting of approximately 18,750 rentable square feet (\\\"RSF\\\"), as more particularly depicted on Exhibit A.\n\n  **Commencement Date:**                  July 1, 2022.\n\n  **Lease Term:**                         Ten (10) years.\n\n  **Expiration Date:**                    June 30, 2032.\n\n  **Permitted Use:**                      General office purposes, software development, and ancillary uses reasonably related thereto.\n\n  **Base Rent (Year 1):**                 \\$34.00 per RSF per annum (\\$637,500.00 per annum; \\$53,125.00 per month), with three percent (3%) annual compounding escalation commencing on the first anniversary of the Commencement Date. See Exhibit B for the full Rent Schedule.\n\n  **Lease Type:**                         Full-Service Gross with Base Year 2022 Expense Stop.\n\n  **Base Year for Operating Expenses:**   Calendar Year 2022.\n\n  **Building Total RSF:**                 Approximately 150,000 RSF.\n\n  **Tenant\\'s Pro Rata Share:**           12.50% (18,750 RSF ÷ 150,000 RSF).\n\n  **Security Deposit:**                   None.\n\n  **Tenant Improvement Allowance:**       \\$55.00 per RSF (\\$1,031,250.00). See Exhibit C.\n\n  **Parking:**                            56 unreserved spaces (no additional charge); 8 reserved executive spaces at \\$175.00 per space per month. See Exhibit D.\n\n  **Renewal Options:**                    Two (2) consecutive five-year renewal options at 95% of fair market rent. See Article XII.\n\n  **Landlord\\'s Broker:**                 Ridgeview Realty Brokers Inc.\n\n  **Tenant\\'s Broker:**                   None.\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**[ARTICLE II --- PREMISES]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 2.01 --- Lease of Premises.** Landlord hereby leases to Tenant and Tenant hereby leases from Landlord the entire 8th Floor of Concord Office Tower, located at 300 Concord Plaza Drive, Atlanta, Georgia 30309, containing approximately 18,750 rentable square feet (the \\\"Premises\\\"), as more particularly shown on the floor plan attached hereto as **Exhibit A** and incorporated herein by this reference.\n\n**Section 2.02 --- Measurement.** The RSF of the Premises has been measured in substantial accordance with the Building Owners and Managers Association (BOMA) 2017 Office Buildings: Standard Methods of Measurement (ANSI/BOMA Z65.1-2017). The RSF stated herein shall be deemed conclusive and shall not be subject to re-measurement or adjustment unless mutually agreed in writing by both parties.\n\n**Section 2.03 --- Condition.** The Premises shall be delivered to Tenant in their \\\"as-is\\\" condition as of the Commencement Date, subject to (a) Landlord\\'s obligation to complete all base building improvements necessary to bring the Building\\'s core and shell, including the 8th Floor\\'s base building mechanical, electrical, plumbing, and life safety systems, to a condition suitable for Tenant\\'s construction of Tenant Improvements, and (b) Landlord\\'s provision of the Tenant Improvement Allowance in accordance with the Work Letter attached hereto as **Exhibit C**.\n\n**Section 2.04 --- Common Areas.** Tenant shall have the non-exclusive right, in common with Landlord, other tenants, and their respective invitees, to use the common areas of the Building, including without limitation the main lobby, elevators, stairwells, restrooms located on the 8th Floor, corridors, loading dock facilities, and other common areas designated by Landlord from time to time for common use.\n\n**[ARTICLE III --- TERM]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 3.01 --- Lease Date.** This Lease is entered into as of February 28, 2022 (the \\\"Lease Date\\\").\n\n**Section 3.02 --- Commencement Date and Lease Term.** The term of this Lease (the \\\"Lease Term\\\") shall commence on July 1, 2022 (the \\\"Commencement Date\\\") and shall expire on June 30, 2032 (the \\\"Expiration Date\\\"), unless sooner terminated or extended in accordance with the terms hereof. The Lease Term shall be for a period of ten (10) years.\n\n**Section 3.03 --- Early Access.** Landlord shall grant Tenant access to the Premises beginning on April 1, 2022, for the sole purpose of constructing Tenant Improvements in accordance with the Work Letter (Exhibit C). During such early access period, Tenant shall not be obligated to pay Base Rent; provided, however, that Tenant shall (a) maintain all insurance required under Article IX, (b) comply with all applicable provisions of this Lease relating to the construction of improvements, use, indemnification, and Landlord\\'s rules and regulations, and (c) not interfere with Landlord\\'s completion of any base building work. The early access period shall not be deemed part of the Lease Term for purposes of calculating the Expiration Date.\n\n**Section 3.04 --- Commencement Date Confirmation.** Within thirty (30) days following the Commencement Date, Landlord and Tenant shall execute a written confirmation letter (the \\\"Commencement Date Memorandum\\\") confirming the actual Commencement Date and Expiration Date. Failure to execute such memorandum shall not affect the validity of the Commencement Date or the Expiration Date as set forth herein.\n\n**[ARTICLE IV --- BASE RENT]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 4.01 --- Payment of Base Rent.** Commencing on the Commencement Date, Tenant shall pay to Landlord Base Rent in equal monthly installments, in advance, on or before the first (1st) day of each calendar month during the Lease Term, without prior notice, demand, deduction, abatement, or set-off, except as expressly provided in this Lease.\n\n**Section 4.02 --- Base Rent Amount.** Base Rent for the first Lease Year (July 1, 2022, through June 30, 2023) shall be Thirty-Four and 00/100 Dollars (\\$34.00) per RSF per annum, which equals Six Hundred Thirty-Seven Thousand Five Hundred and 00/100 Dollars (\\$637,500.00) per annum, payable in monthly installments of Fifty-Three Thousand One Hundred Twenty-Five and 00/100 Dollars (\\$53,125.00).\n\n**Section 4.03 --- Annual Escalation.** On each anniversary of the Commencement Date (i.e., commencing July 1, 2023), the Base Rent rate per RSF per annum shall increase by three percent (3%) over the Base Rent rate in effect for the immediately preceding Lease Year, calculated on a compounded annual basis. The full Rent Schedule, setting forth the Base Rent for each Lease Year during the Lease Term, is attached hereto as **Exhibit B** and incorporated herein by this reference.\n\n**Section 4.04 --- Partial Month Proration.** If the Commencement Date falls on a day other than the first day of a calendar month, Base Rent for such partial month shall be prorated on a per diem basis (based on the actual number of days in such month). As the Commencement Date is July 1, 2022, no proration shall be required for the initial month.\n\n**Section 4.05 --- Late Payment Fee.** If any installment of Base Rent or Additional Rent is not received by Landlord within five (5) business days after the date such payment is due, Tenant shall pay a late charge equal to five percent (5%) of the overdue amount. The parties agree that such late charge represents a reasonable estimate of the costs Landlord will incur as a result of late payment.\n\n**Section 4.06 --- Interest on Past-Due Amounts.** Any amount owed by Tenant under this Lease that remains unpaid for more than ten (10) days after the date due shall bear interest from the date due until paid at the lesser of (a) one and one-half percent (1.5%) per month (18% per annum) or (b) the maximum rate permitted by the laws of the State of Georgia.\n\n**Section 4.07 --- Place of Payment.** All rent and other sums payable by Tenant under this Lease shall be paid to Landlord at the address specified in Article I, or at such other address as Landlord may designate in writing from time to time.\n\n**[ARTICLE V --- OPERATING EXPENSES AND EXPENSE STOP]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 5.01 --- Full-Service Gross Lease.** This Lease is a full-service gross lease with a Base Year expense stop. Landlord shall pay all Operating Expenses for the Building during the Lease Term. However, commencing January 1, 2023, Tenant shall pay, as Additional Rent, Tenant\\'s Pro Rata Share (12.50%) of the amount by which actual Operating Expenses for any calendar year exceed the Operating Expenses incurred during the Base Year (Calendar Year 2022) (the \\\"Expense Stop\\\").\n\n**Section 5.02 --- Definition of Operating Expenses.** \\\"Operating Expenses\\\" shall mean all costs and expenses of every kind and nature incurred by Landlord in the ownership, operation, management, maintenance, and repair of the Building, the common areas, and the land upon which the Building is situated, including without limitation: (a) real estate taxes, assessments (general and special), and governmental charges levied upon the Building; (b) insurance premiums for all insurance maintained by Landlord on the Building; (c) utility costs, including electricity, gas, water, sewer, and trash removal for common areas and base building systems; (d) janitorial services and supplies; (e) landscaping and groundskeeping; (f) security services and systems; (g) management fees, not to exceed four percent (4%) of the Building\\'s gross revenues; (h) repairs, maintenance, and replacement of Building systems, including HVAC, elevators, plumbing, and electrical systems; (i) common area maintenance; (j) reasonable reserves for capital replacements as determined by Landlord in its reasonable discretion; and (k) all other costs reasonably incurred in the operation of the Building consistent with the operation of comparable Class A office buildings in the Atlanta midtown submarket.\n\n**Section 5.03 --- Exclusions from Operating Expenses.** The following shall be excluded from Operating Expenses: (a) capital improvements, except those mandated by governmental laws, codes, or regulations enacted after the Base Year or those which are reasonably expected to reduce Operating Expenses, in either case amortized over their useful life with interest at eight percent (8%) per annum; (b) leasing commissions, advertising costs, and tenant procurement expenses; (c) tenant improvement costs, allowances, or concessions for other tenants; (d) costs reimbursed by insurance proceeds or condemnation awards; (e) depreciation of the Building; (f) debt service, including mortgage principal and interest payments; (g) Landlord\\'s federal, state, or local income taxes; (h) costs arising from Landlord\\'s negligence or willful misconduct; (i) costs of correcting defects in original construction; and (j) legal fees incurred in disputes with tenants.\n\n**Section 5.04 --- Estimated Payments.** Landlord shall provide Tenant with a good-faith estimate of Tenant\\'s share of Operating Expense increases for each calendar year prior to or at the beginning of such year. Tenant shall pay one-twelfth (1/12) of such estimated amount monthly, concurrently with Base Rent. Landlord may revise such estimate from time to time during the calendar year upon reasonable notice to Tenant.\n\n**Section 5.05 --- Annual Reconciliation.** Within one hundred twenty (120) days after the end of each calendar year, Landlord shall provide Tenant with a reasonably detailed statement (the \\\"Annual Statement\\\") setting forth the actual Operating Expenses for such calendar year and a calculation of Tenant\\'s share. If Tenant\\'s estimated payments exceed Tenant\\'s actual share, Landlord shall credit the overpayment against Tenant\\'s next monthly installment(s) of Additional Rent (or refund such amount if the Lease has expired). If Tenant\\'s estimated payments are less than Tenant\\'s actual share, Tenant shall pay the shortfall within thirty (30) days after receipt of the Annual Statement.\n\n**Section 5.06 --- Audit Right.** Tenant shall have the right, at Tenant\\'s sole cost and expense, to audit Landlord\\'s books and records relating to Operating Expenses for any calendar year, upon not less than thirty (30) days\\' prior written notice to Landlord, exercised within twelve (12) months after Tenant\\'s receipt of the Annual Statement for such year. Such audit shall be conducted during normal business hours at Landlord\\'s office or at the office of Landlord\\'s property manager. If the audit reveals that Landlord has overcharged Tenant by more than five percent (5%) for the audited year, Landlord shall reimburse Tenant for Tenant\\'s reasonable out-of-pocket audit costs, in addition to refunding the overpayment. In no event shall Tenant engage an auditor compensated on a contingency fee basis.\n\n**Section 5.07 --- Controllable Expense Cap.** Landlord\\'s controllable operating expenses --- defined as all Operating Expenses other than real estate taxes, insurance premiums, utilities, and snow/ice removal --- shall not increase by more than five percent (5%) per annum on a cumulative, compounding basis over the amount of such controllable operating expenses incurred during the Base Year.\n\n**Section 5.08 --- Gross-Up Provision.** If the Building is less than ninety-five percent (95%) occupied during any calendar year (including the Base Year), Operating Expenses that vary with occupancy shall be adjusted (\\\"grossed up\\\") to reflect the Operating Expenses that would have been incurred had the Building been ninety-five percent (95%) occupied for the entire calendar year.\n\n**[ARTICLE VI --- USE OF PREMISES]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 6.01 --- Permitted Use.** Tenant shall use and occupy the Premises solely for general office purposes, software development, and ancillary uses reasonably related thereto (the \\\"Permitted Use\\\"), and for no other purpose without Landlord\\'s prior written consent.\n\n**Section 6.02 --- Prohibited Uses.** Tenant shall not use the Premises for any purpose that (a) violates any applicable federal, state, or local law, ordinance, regulation, or code; (b) creates a nuisance or unreasonable disturbance to other occupants of the Building; (c) increases Landlord\\'s insurance premiums for the Building, unless Tenant agrees to pay any such increase; or (d) violates the Building\\'s rules and regulations as set forth in Exhibit E.\n\n**Section 6.03 --- Compliance with Laws.** Tenant shall comply with all applicable federal, state, and local laws, ordinances, regulations, and codes relating to its use and occupancy of the Premises, including without limitation the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, as amended, and all regulations promulgated thereunder.\n\n**Section 6.04 --- Hazardous Materials.** Tenant shall not generate, store, treat, or dispose of hazardous materials on the Premises or in the Building, except for customary office supplies and cleaning products used in the ordinary course of business in compliance with all applicable environmental laws.\n\n**[ARTICLE VII --- TENANT IMPROVEMENTS]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 7.01 --- Tenant Improvement Allowance.** Landlord shall provide Tenant with a tenant improvement allowance (the \\\"TI Allowance\\\") in the amount of Fifty-Five and 00/100 Dollars (\\$55.00) per RSF of the Premises, for a total of One Million Thirty-One Thousand Two Hundred Fifty and 00/100 Dollars (\\$1,031,250.00). The TI Allowance shall be applied toward the cost of designing and constructing Tenant\\'s initial improvements to the Premises in accordance with the Work Letter attached hereto as **Exhibit C**.\n\n**Section 7.02 --- Disbursement.** The TI Allowance shall be disbursed in accordance with the procedures set forth in the Work Letter (Exhibit C). Any costs of Tenant\\'s improvements that exceed the TI Allowance shall be borne solely by Tenant.\n\n**Section 7.03 --- Unused Allowance.** Any unused portion of the TI Allowance remaining after the completion of Tenant\\'s improvements shall not be paid to Tenant as cash, credited against Base Rent or Additional Rent, or otherwise refunded. Any such unused portion shall be forfeited and retained by Landlord.\n\n**Section 7.04 --- Amortization for Recapture.** For the purpose of calculating any recapture amounts payable by Tenant upon early termination of this Lease or contraction of the Premises pursuant to Article XIV, the TI Allowance shall be amortized over the initial ten (10) year Lease Term at a rate of seven percent (7%) per annum, with the full amount of \\$1,031,250.00 as the initial principal balance. The unamortized balance at any point during the Lease Term shall be determined using a standard mortgage-style amortization schedule with equal monthly payments based on a seven percent (7%) annual interest rate and a one-hundred-twenty (120) month amortization period. For purposes of calculating the contraction fee under Article XIV, the amortization of the TI Allowance allocable to the Contraction Space shall be calculated in the same manner, using \\$55.00 per RSF of the Contraction Space as the initial principal.\n\n**Section 7.05 --- Ownership of Improvements.** All Tenant Improvements constructed with the TI Allowance, once installed, shall become the property of Landlord and shall remain upon the Premises at the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease; provided, however, that Landlord may require Tenant to remove any non-standard improvements or specialty installations designated by Landlord in writing at the time of Landlord\\'s approval of Tenant\\'s construction plans, and Tenant shall restore the affected areas to their condition prior to the installation of such improvements.\n\n**[ARTICLE VIII --- MAINTENANCE AND REPAIRS]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 8.01 --- Landlord\\'s Obligations.** Landlord shall maintain and repair, at Landlord\\'s expense (subject to inclusion in Operating Expenses), the structural components of the Building (including roof, foundation, and exterior walls), base building systems (including base building HVAC, elevators, plumbing, and electrical systems), common areas, and the Building\\'s exterior. Landlord shall provide the following standard building services: (a) heating, ventilation, and air conditioning during business hours (8:00 AM to 6:00 PM, Monday through Friday, and 8:00 AM to 1:00 PM, Saturday, excluding Building holidays); (b) janitorial service for the Premises five (5) nights per week; and (c) passenger elevator service.\n\n**Section 8.02 --- Tenant\\'s Obligations.** Tenant shall maintain the interior of the Premises in good condition and repair throughout the Lease Term, including all of Tenant\\'s furniture, fixtures, equipment, supplemental HVAC units or other specialized systems installed by or for Tenant, and any improvements or alterations constructed by Tenant. Tenant shall promptly notify Landlord of any condition in the Premises requiring repair by Landlord.\n\n**Section 8.03 --- After-Hours HVAC.** After-hours HVAC service shall be available to Tenant upon request at a rate of Seventy-Five and 00/100 Dollars (\\$75.00) per hour per zone, subject to annual adjustment by Landlord based on actual utility costs. Tenant shall request after-hours HVAC by providing reasonable advance notice to Landlord\\'s building management office.\n\n**[ARTICLE IX --- INSURANCE]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 9.01 --- Tenant\\'s Insurance.** Tenant shall, at Tenant\\'s sole cost, obtain and maintain throughout the Lease Term the following insurance coverages: (a) Commercial General Liability insurance with limits of not less than \\$1,000,000 per occurrence and \\$3,000,000 in the aggregate; (b) Workers\\' Compensation insurance as required by the laws of the State of Georgia, and Employers\\' Liability insurance with a limit of not less than \\$500,000; (c) Business Personal Property insurance covering Tenant\\'s furniture, fixtures, equipment, and other personal property at full replacement cost; and (d) Business Interruption insurance sufficient to cover not less than twelve (12) months of Base Rent and Additional Rent.\n\n**Section 9.02 --- Landlord\\'s Insurance.** Landlord shall maintain property insurance on the Building on an all-risk or special-form basis, commercial general liability insurance, and such other insurance as Landlord deems reasonably necessary. The cost of Landlord\\'s insurance shall be included in Operating Expenses.\n\n**Section 9.03 --- Mutual Waiver of Subrogation.** Landlord and Tenant each hereby waive any right of recovery against the other party for any loss or damage to the extent covered (or required to be covered) by such party\\'s property insurance policies. Each party shall cause its insurer to issue appropriate waiver of subrogation endorsements.\n\n**Section 9.04 --- Additional Insured.** Tenant\\'s commercial general liability policy shall name Landlord, Landlord\\'s managing agent, and their respective officers, directors, partners, and employees as additional insureds. All policies shall be issued by carriers rated A-VII or better by A.M. Best.\n\n**Section 9.05 --- Certificates.** Tenant shall deliver certificates of insurance to Landlord prior to the Commencement Date (or the commencement of early access) and annually thereafter, and upon each renewal or replacement of coverage.\n\n**[ARTICLE X --- INDEMNIFICATION AND LIABILITY]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 10.01 --- Tenant\\'s Indemnification.** Tenant shall indemnify, defend, and hold harmless Landlord and its partners, officers, directors, agents, and employees from and against any and all claims, damages, losses, liabilities, costs, and expenses (including reasonable attorneys\\' fees) arising from (a) Tenant\\'s use and occupancy of the Premises, (b) any negligence or willful misconduct of Tenant or its agents, employees, contractors, or invitees, or (c) any breach by Tenant of its obligations under this Lease.\n\n**Section 10.02 --- Landlord\\'s Indemnification.** Landlord shall indemnify, defend, and hold harmless Tenant and its officers, directors, agents, and employees from and against any and all claims, damages, losses, liabilities, costs, and expenses (including reasonable attorneys\\' fees) arising from Landlord\\'s negligence or willful misconduct.\n\n**Section 10.03 --- Limitation of Damages.** In no event shall either party be liable to the other for consequential, incidental, special, or punitive damages arising out of or in connection with this Lease.\n\n**Section 10.04 --- Interruption of Services.** Landlord shall not be liable for any interruption or cessation of services or utilities provided to the Premises, except to the extent caused by Landlord\\'s gross negligence or willful misconduct. Any rent abatement in connection with such interruption shall be governed by the applicable provisions of this Lease.\n\n**[ARTICLE XI --- ASSIGNMENT AND SUBLETTING]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 11.01 --- General Restriction.** Tenant shall not assign this Lease or sublet all or any portion of the Premises without Landlord\\'s prior written consent, except as expressly provided in this Article XI. Any purported assignment or subletting in violation of this Article shall be void and of no force or effect and shall constitute an Event of Default under this Lease.\n\n**Section 11.02 --- Subletting Without Consent (40% Threshold).** Notwithstanding Section 11.01, Tenant may sublet up to forty percent (40%) of the rentable square footage of the Premises (i.e., up to 7,500 RSF) without obtaining Landlord\\'s prior written consent, provided that all of the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n> \\(a\\) the proposed subtenant is engaged in a use consistent with the Permitted Use as defined in this Lease;\n>\n> \\(b\\) the proposed subtenant is not a governmental entity, and is not a person or entity that is an existing tenant of the Building or a prospective tenant with whom Landlord is then in active lease negotiations;\n>\n> \\(c\\) Tenant provides Landlord with written notice of the sublease, together with a complete copy of the executed sublease agreement and reasonable financial and background information regarding the proposed subtenant, within ten (10) business days after Tenant\\'s execution of the sublease; and\n>\n> \\(d\\) Tenant shall remain fully and primarily liable under this Lease for all obligations, including the payment of all rent and the performance of all other covenants and conditions.\n\n**Section 11.03 --- Subletting Above 40%.** Any subletting of more than forty percent (40%) of the rentable square footage of the Premises shall require Landlord\\'s prior written consent, which consent shall not be unreasonably withheld, conditioned, or delayed. Landlord shall respond to Tenant\\'s written request for consent within twenty (20) business days after Landlord\\'s receipt of all required information, including a copy of the proposed sublease, financial statements of the proposed subtenant, and a description of the proposed subtenant\\'s intended use. Failure by Landlord to respond within such twenty (20) business day period shall be deemed consent.\n\n**Section 11.04 --- Profit-Sharing on Subletting.** In connection with any subletting of the Premises (whether or not Landlord\\'s consent is required pursuant to Sections 11.02 or 11.03), Landlord shall be entitled to receive fifty percent (50%) of all Subletting Profits realized by Tenant. For purposes of this Section, \\\"Subletting Profits\\\" shall mean the excess, if any, of (a) the aggregate rent and other monetary consideration payable by the subtenant to Tenant under the sublease, over (b) the Base Rent and Additional Rent payable by Tenant under this Lease allocable to the sublet space (determined on a per-RSF basis), after deducting Tenant\\'s reasonable, documented, out-of-pocket costs actually incurred in connection with such subletting, including brokerage commissions, legal fees, and the cost of any tenant improvement work performed by Tenant in the sublet space, all amortized on a straight-line basis over the term of the sublease. Subletting Profits shall be calculated and paid to Landlord on a monthly basis. Tenant shall provide Landlord with a detailed accounting of Subletting Profits, including supporting documentation, within thirty (30) days after the end of each calendar quarter during the term of any sublease.\n\n**Section 11.05 --- Assignment.** Any assignment of this Lease, whether voluntarily or by operation of law, shall require Landlord\\'s prior written consent, which consent shall not be unreasonably withheld, conditioned, or delayed. In the event of any approved assignment, the original Tenant named herein (as assignor) shall remain secondarily liable for all obligations under this Lease for the remainder of the Lease Term, unless Landlord agrees in writing to release Tenant from such liability.\n\n**Section 11.06 --- Affiliate Transfers.** Notwithstanding Sections 11.01 and 11.05, Tenant may assign this Lease or sublet all or any portion of the Premises to an Affiliate without Landlord\\'s prior consent. For purposes of this Section, \\\"Affiliate\\\" means any entity that controls, is controlled by, or is under common control with Tenant, where \\\"control\\\" means the direct or indirect ownership of more than fifty percent (50%) of the voting securities or equivalent ownership interests of such entity. Any such Affiliate transfer shall be subject to the following conditions: (a) the Affiliate has a tangible net worth, as of the effective date of such transfer, at least equal to Tenant\\'s tangible net worth as of the Lease Date; and (b) Tenant provides Landlord with written notice of such transfer at least fifteen (15) days prior to the effective date thereof, together with reasonable evidence that the foregoing net worth condition has been satisfied.\n\n**Section 11.07 --- No Recapture Right.** Landlord shall not have a recapture right, termination right, or right to cancel this Lease in connection with any proposed assignment or subletting under this Article XI.\n\n**Section 11.08 --- No Release.** No subletting or assignment, whether with or without Landlord\\'s consent, shall release Tenant from any of its obligations under this Lease unless Landlord expressly agrees otherwise in writing. Tenant shall remain fully liable for the performance of all terms, covenants, and conditions of this Lease.\n\n**[ARTICLE XII --- RENEWAL OPTIONS]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 12.01 --- Grant of Renewal Options.** Landlord hereby grants to Tenant two (2) consecutive options to renew this Lease (each, a \\\"Renewal Option\\\"), each for an additional period of five (5) years (each, a \\\"Renewal Term\\\"). The first Renewal Term, if exercised, shall commence on July 1, 2032, and expire on June 30, 2037. The second Renewal Term, if exercised, shall commence on July 1, 2037, and expire on June 30, 2042.\n\n**Section 12.02 --- Exercise of Renewal Option.** Tenant shall exercise each Renewal Option by delivering irrevocable written notice to Landlord no later than twelve (12) months prior to the expiration of the then-current Lease Term (or Renewal Term, as applicable). The Renewal Options must be exercised consecutively and may not be exercised if an Event of Default exists at the time of exercise or at the commencement of the applicable Renewal Term.\n\n**Section 12.03 --- Renewal Rent.** Base Rent during each Renewal Term shall be equal to ninety-five percent (95%) of the then-prevailing fair market rent for comparable Class A office space in the Atlanta midtown/Buckhead submarket, taking into account all relevant factors, including size, location, term, condition, and amenities, as determined in accordance with Section 12.04.\n\n**Section 12.04 --- Determination of Fair Market Rent.** Within thirty (30) days after Landlord\\'s receipt of Tenant\\'s renewal notice, Landlord and Tenant shall endeavor in good faith to agree upon the fair market rent for the applicable Renewal Term. If the parties are unable to agree within such thirty (30) day period, each party shall, within fifteen (15) days thereafter, appoint one (1) independent MAI-certified appraiser with at least ten (10) years of experience in the Atlanta commercial office market. Within fifteen (15) days after their appointment, the two appraisers shall jointly select a third independent MAI-certified appraiser meeting the same qualifications. Each appraiser shall independently determine the fair market rent for the Premises within thirty (30) days after the appointment of the third appraiser. The fair market rent shall be the average of the two closest appraisals. Each party shall bear the cost of its own appraiser, and the cost of the third appraiser shall be shared equally.\n\n**Section 12.05 --- Terms During Renewal.** All terms and conditions of this Lease shall apply during each Renewal Term, except that: (a) Base Rent shall be as determined pursuant to Section 12.03; (b) no additional Tenant Improvement Allowance shall be provided unless otherwise agreed in writing by the parties; and (c) the number of remaining Renewal Options shall be reduced by one for each Renewal Option exercised.\n\n**[ARTICLE XIII --- EXPANSION OPTION]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 13.01 --- Grant of Expansion Option.** Landlord hereby grants to Tenant the option (the \\\"Expansion Option\\\") to expand the Premises to include the 9th Floor of the Building (the \\\"Expansion Space\\\"), consisting of approximately 18,500 rentable square feet.\n\n**Section 13.02 --- Exercise Window.** Tenant may exercise the Expansion Option at any time during Lease Years 3 through 5 (i.e., from July 1, 2024, through June 30, 2027) by delivering written notice to Landlord at least six (6) months prior to Tenant\\'s desired commencement date for the Expansion Space (the \\\"Expansion Commencement Date\\\"). If Tenant does not exercise the Expansion Option on or before June 30, 2027, the Expansion Option shall terminate and be of no further force or effect.\n\n**Section 13.03 --- Expansion Rent.** Base Rent for the Expansion Space shall be at the same rate per RSF per annum as the Base Rent rate then in effect for the original Premises (i.e., the then-current Lease Year\\'s rate under the Rent Schedule), escalating thereafter on the same schedule and at the same three percent (3%) per annum compounding rate as Base Rent for the original Premises.\n\n**Section 13.04 --- Expansion TI Allowance.** Landlord shall provide a tenant improvement allowance for the Expansion Space in the amount of Forty and 00/100 Dollars (\\$40.00) per RSF (\\$40.00 × 18,500 RSF = \\$740,000.00), to be disbursed in accordance with the procedures set forth in the Work Letter (Exhibit C), mutatis mutandis.\n\n**Section 13.05 --- Coterminous Term.** The term for the Expansion Space shall be coterminous with the Lease Term for the original Premises, expiring on June 30, 2032, unless extended pursuant to Article XII.\n\n**Section 13.06 --- Lease Amendment.** Upon exercise of the Expansion Option, the parties shall execute an amendment to this Lease incorporating the Expansion Space on the terms set forth in this Article XIII and adjusting Tenant\\'s Pro Rata Share and parking allocation proportionally.\n\n**[ARTICLE XIV --- CONTRACTION OPTION]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 14.01 --- Contraction Right.** After the expiration of Lease Year 5 (i.e., on or after July 1, 2027), Tenant shall have the one-time right to contract the Premises by surrendering up to 5,000 RSF (the \\\"Contraction Space\\\") by delivering irrevocable written notice to Landlord at least nine (9) months prior to the effective date of contraction (the \\\"Contraction Date\\\"). The location of the Contraction Space within the Premises shall be mutually agreed upon by Landlord and Tenant, and shall be a contiguous area reasonably suitable for leasing by Landlord to a third party.\n\n**Section 14.02 --- Contraction Fee.** Tenant shall pay to Landlord a contraction fee (the \\\"Contraction Fee\\\") on or before the Contraction Date, equal to the sum of: (a) the unamortized TI Allowance allocable to the Contraction Space, calculated using a seven percent (7%) per annum amortization rate over the original ten (10) year Lease Term, with the initial principal being \\$55.00 per RSF of the Contraction Space; plus (b) six (6) months of Base Rent attributable to the Contraction Space, calculated at the Base Rent rate in effect as of the Contraction Date.\n\n**Section 14.03 --- Effect of Contraction.** Upon the Contraction Date and payment of the Contraction Fee, (a) the Premises shall be reduced by the RSF of the Contraction Space; (b) Base Rent shall be reduced proportionally based on the reduced RSF; (c) Tenant\\'s Pro Rata Share shall be adjusted to reflect the reduced Premises; and (d) Tenant\\'s parking allocation shall be adjusted proportionally.\n\n**Section 14.04 --- Limitation.** Tenant may exercise the contraction option only once during the Lease Term.\n\n**[ARTICLE XV --- PARKING]{.underline}**\n\nParking for the Premises shall be provided in accordance with the Parking Addendum attached hereto as **Exhibit D** and incorporated herein by this reference. In summary, Tenant shall be entitled to fifty-six (56) unreserved parking spaces in the Building\\'s parking garage at no additional charge during the Lease Term, reflecting a ratio of approximately 2.99 spaces per 1,000 RSF. Tenant shall also be entitled to eight (8) reserved executive parking spaces at a monthly rate of \\$175.00 per space (\\$1,400.00 per month; \\$16,800.00 per year), payable as Additional Rent. Reserved parking charges shall be subject to annual adjustment as set forth in Exhibit D. Parking rights are appurtenant to this Lease and may not be separately transferred except in connection with a permitted assignment or subletting. Landlord shall maintain the parking garage in good condition and provide adequate lighting and security measures.\n\n**[ARTICLE XVI --- ALTERATIONS]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 16.01 --- Minor Alterations.** Tenant may make non-structural interior alterations to the Premises costing less than Fifty Thousand and 00/100 Dollars (\\$50,000.00) per project without Landlord\\'s prior written consent, provided that Tenant gives Landlord prior written notice thereof and the alterations comply with all applicable laws, codes, and regulations and do not adversely affect the Building\\'s structural integrity or base building systems.\n\n**Section 16.02 --- Major Alterations.** Any alteration costing Fifty Thousand and 00/100 Dollars (\\$50,000.00) or more, or any structural alteration, shall require Landlord\\'s prior written consent, which shall not be unreasonably withheld, conditioned, or delayed. Tenant shall submit complete plans and specifications to Landlord for review.\n\n**Section 16.03 --- Standards.** All alteration work shall be performed in a good and workmanlike manner, in compliance with all applicable building codes and regulations, using licensed and adequately insured contractors. Tenant shall obtain all necessary permits prior to commencing work.\n\n**Section 16.04 --- Removal.** Landlord may, at the time of approving Tenant\\'s alteration plans, designate any non-standard alterations that Tenant shall be required to remove at the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease. Upon removal, Tenant shall restore the affected areas to their condition prior to such alterations, reasonable wear and tear excepted.\n\n**[ARTICLE XVII --- SUBORDINATION AND ATTORNMENT]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 17.01 --- Subordination.** This Lease is and shall be subject and subordinate to any mortgage, deed of trust, or ground lease now or hereafter placed upon the Building or the land upon which it is situated; provided, however, that any holder of such mortgage, deed of trust, or ground lease shall, as a condition to such subordination, execute and deliver to Tenant a commercially reasonable Subordination, Non-Disturbance, and Attornment Agreement (\\\"SNDA\\\") in a form reasonably acceptable to Tenant, confirming that Tenant\\'s rights under this Lease shall not be disturbed so long as Tenant is not in default hereunder.\n\n**Section 17.02 --- Current Status.** Landlord represents and warrants that, as of the Lease Date, no mortgage, deed of trust, or ground lease encumbers the Building that would require the execution of an SNDA.\n\n**Section 17.03 --- Attornment.** Tenant agrees to attorn to any successor to Landlord\\'s interest in the Building, whether through foreclosure, deed in lieu of foreclosure, or otherwise, provided that such successor recognizes Tenant\\'s rights under this Lease.\n\n**[ARTICLE XVIII --- DAMAGE AND DESTRUCTION]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 18.01 --- Repair Obligation.** If the Premises or the Building is damaged by fire or other casualty, Landlord shall, subject to the provisions of this Article, repair such damage with reasonable diligence, using the proceeds of Landlord\\'s insurance. Landlord\\'s repair obligation shall extend to the base building and any improvements originally funded by the TI Allowance, but shall not extend to Tenant\\'s personal property, furniture, trade fixtures, or any improvements funded by Tenant in excess of the TI Allowance.\n\n**Section 18.02 --- Termination Right.** If Landlord reasonably determines that the damage cannot be repaired within two hundred seventy (270) days after the date of the casualty, either party may terminate this Lease by delivering written notice to the other within thirty (30) days after Landlord\\'s determination.\n\n**Section 18.03 --- Rent Abatement.** If the Premises are rendered wholly or partially untenantable as a result of the casualty, Base Rent and Additional Rent shall abate proportionally (based on the portion of the Premises rendered untenantable) from the date of the casualty until the earlier of (a) the date the Premises are restored to a tenantable condition or (b) the date of termination of this Lease.\n\n**[ARTICLE XIX --- CONDEMNATION]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 19.01 --- Total Taking.** If all or substantially all of the Premises is taken by eminent domain or other governmental authority, this Lease shall terminate as of the date of the taking.\n\n**Section 19.02 --- Partial Taking.** If a partial taking of the Premises renders the remaining Premises unsuitable for Tenant\\'s continued use and occupancy, either party may terminate this Lease by delivering written notice to the other within thirty (30) days after the date of the taking. If neither party elects to terminate, the Lease shall continue in effect with respect to the remaining portion of the Premises, and Base Rent and Additional Rent shall abate proportionally.\n\n**Section 19.03 --- Condemnation Awards.** All condemnation awards and proceeds shall belong to and be the property of Landlord, except that Tenant shall have the right to separately claim and recover from the condemning authority compensation for Tenant\\'s moving expenses, the unamortized cost of Tenant-funded improvements (i.e., improvements paid for by Tenant in excess of the TI Allowance), and any loss of Tenant\\'s business goodwill, to the extent such separate claim does not reduce Landlord\\'s award.\n\n**[ARTICLE XX --- DEFAULT AND REMEDIES]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 20.01 --- Tenant Events of Default.** Each of the following shall constitute an \\\"Event of Default\\\" by Tenant under this Lease:\n\n> \\(a\\) Tenant\\'s failure to pay Base Rent, Additional Rent, or any other sum due under this Lease within five (5) business days after written notice from Landlord that such payment is overdue;\n>\n> \\(b\\) Tenant\\'s failure to perform or observe any other covenant, condition, or obligation under this Lease within thirty (30) days after written notice from Landlord specifying the nature of the default; provided, however, that if such default is of a nature that cannot reasonably be cured within thirty (30) days, Tenant shall not be deemed in default if Tenant commences a cure within such thirty (30) day period and diligently prosecutes the cure to completion;\n>\n> \\(c\\) Tenant\\'s filing of a petition in bankruptcy, making a general assignment for the benefit of creditors, or the filing of an involuntary petition against Tenant that is not dismissed within sixty (60) days; or\n>\n> \\(d\\) Tenant\\'s abandonment of the Premises.\n\n**Section 20.02 --- Landlord\\'s Remedies.** Upon the occurrence of an Event of Default, Landlord may, at its option and in addition to any other remedies available at law or in equity: (i) terminate this Lease by written notice to Tenant, whereupon Tenant shall immediately surrender the Premises; (ii) re-enter the Premises and relet the same on Tenant\\'s behalf on such terms as Landlord deems commercially reasonable, with Tenant remaining liable for all deficiencies; (iii) accelerate all Base Rent and Additional Rent for the remainder of the Lease Term, discounted to present value at a rate equal to the then-applicable Federal Reserve discount rate plus one percent (1%); or (iv) pursue any other remedy available under the laws of the State of Georgia.\n\n**Section 20.03 --- Landlord\\'s Default.** If Landlord fails to perform any obligation under this Lease within thirty (30) days after written notice from Tenant specifying the nature of the default (or such longer period as may be reasonably required for non-monetary defaults, provided Landlord commences cure within such thirty (30) day period and diligently pursues the same to completion), Tenant may pursue any remedies available at law or in equity; provided, however, that Tenant shall not have the right to terminate this Lease or withhold or offset rent except as expressly permitted by the laws of the State of Georgia.\n\n**Section 20.04 --- Holdover.** If Tenant remains in possession of the Premises after the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease without Landlord\\'s prior written consent, Tenant shall be deemed a tenant at sufferance, and Tenant shall pay holdover rent equal to one hundred fifty percent (150%) of the Base Rent in effect during the last month of the Lease Term (or Renewal Term, as applicable), plus all Additional Rent and other charges under this Lease. Tenant shall also be liable for all damages, costs, and expenses incurred by Landlord as a result of such holdover occupancy, including without limitation any claims by prospective tenants.\n\n**[ARTICLE XXI --- SURRENDER AND RESTORATION]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 21.01 --- Surrender.** Upon the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease, Tenant shall surrender the Premises to Landlord in good order and condition, ordinary wear and tear and casualty damage (to the extent Landlord is obligated to repair) excepted.\n\n**Section 21.02 --- Removal of Property.** Tenant shall remove all of its personal property, furniture, trade fixtures, and equipment from the Premises on or before the Expiration Date (or earlier termination date). Tenant shall also remove any alterations or improvements designated for removal by Landlord pursuant to Article XVI and shall restore the affected areas to their original condition, reasonable wear and tear excepted.\n\n**Section 21.03 --- Abandoned Property.** Any personal property of Tenant remaining in the Premises more than ten (10) days after the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease shall be deemed abandoned by Tenant, and Landlord may dispose of such property in any manner Landlord sees fit, without liability to Tenant.\n\n**[ARTICLE XXII --- QUIET ENJOYMENT]{.underline}**\n\nLandlord covenants and agrees that Tenant, upon paying the Base Rent, Additional Rent, and all other sums due under this Lease and performing all of Tenant\\'s obligations hereunder, shall peaceably and quietly have, hold, and enjoy the Premises during the Lease Term (and any Renewal Term) without hindrance or interference from Landlord or any person or entity lawfully claiming by, through, or under Landlord, subject to the terms and conditions of this Lease.\n\n**[ARTICLE XXIII --- NOTICES]{.underline}**\n\nAll notices, demands, consents, approvals, and other communications required or permitted under this Lease shall be in writing and shall be deemed duly given if delivered by (a) personal delivery, (b) a nationally recognized overnight courier service (e.g., FedEx or UPS), or (c) certified mail, return receipt requested, postage prepaid, addressed as follows:\n\n**To Landlord:** Thornfield Realty Holdings LP 500 South Tryon Street, Suite 2200 Charlotte, NC 28202\n\nAttn: Renata Thornfield-Kiess, President\n\nWith a copy to: Karen Osgood, Property Manager 500 South Tryon Street, Suite 2200 Charlotte, NC 28202\n\n**To Tenant:** Verdana Software Solutions Inc. 300 Concord Plaza Drive, 8th Floor Atlanta, GA 30309\n\nAttn: General Counsel\n\nNotices shall be effective upon receipt (if delivered personally or by overnight courier) or three (3) business days after deposit in the United States mail (if sent by certified mail). Either party may change its notice address by written notice to the other party given in accordance with this Article.\n\n**[ARTICLE XXIV --- ESTOPPEL CERTIFICATES]{.underline}**\n\nEach party shall, within fifteen (15) business days after written request by the other party, execute and deliver an estoppel certificate addressed to such persons as the requesting party may reasonably designate, certifying: (a) that this Lease is in full force and effect and has not been modified (or stating the nature of any modifications); (b) that no defaults exist under this Lease (or specifying any claimed defaults); (c) the Commencement Date and Expiration Date; (d) the current Base Rent and Additional Rent payable; (e) the amount of any security deposit held by Landlord; and (f) such other matters as may be reasonably requested. Failure by a party to deliver the estoppel certificate within such fifteen (15) business day period shall constitute an irrevocable acknowledgment that the statements set forth in the requesting party\\'s proposed form of certificate are true and correct.\n\n**[ARTICLE XXV --- BROKERS]{.underline}**\n\nLandlord represents and warrants that Ridgeview Realty Brokers Inc. acted as Landlord\\'s broker in connection with this Lease. Landlord shall be solely responsible for the payment of all brokerage commissions due to Ridgeview Realty Brokers Inc. pursuant to a separate written agreement between Landlord and such broker. Tenant represents and warrants that it has not engaged any broker or finder in connection with this Lease. Each party shall indemnify, defend, and hold harmless the other from and against any claims, losses, costs, and expenses (including reasonable attorneys\\' fees) arising from any breach of its representations under this Article.\n\n**[ARTICLE XXVI --- MISCELLANEOUS]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 26.01 --- Governing Law.** This Lease shall be governed by and construed in accordance with the laws of the State of Georgia, without regard to its conflict of laws principles.\n\n**Section 26.02 --- Entire Agreement.** This Lease, together with all Exhibits attached hereto and incorporated herein by reference, constitutes the entire agreement between the parties with respect to the subject matter hereof and supersedes all prior negotiations, proposals, representations, understandings, and agreements, whether oral or written.\n\n**Section 26.03 --- Amendments.** This Lease may not be modified, amended, or supplemented except by a written instrument executed by both Landlord and Tenant.\n\n**Section 26.04 --- Severability.** If any provision of this Lease is held to be invalid, illegal, or unenforceable by a court of competent jurisdiction, the validity, legality, and enforceability of the remaining provisions shall not in any way be affected or impaired thereby.\n\n**Section 26.05 --- Waiver.** No waiver by either party of any breach of any term, covenant, or condition of this Lease shall be deemed a waiver of any subsequent breach of the same or any other term, covenant, or condition. No waiver shall be effective unless in writing signed by the waiving party.\n\n**Section 26.06 --- Successors and Assigns.** This Lease shall be binding upon and inure to the benefit of the parties hereto and their respective heirs, executors, administrators, legal representatives, successors, and permitted assigns.\n\n**Section 26.07 --- No Recording.** Tenant shall not record this Lease or any memorandum hereof in any public records without Landlord\\'s prior written consent; provided, however, that upon Tenant\\'s request, the parties shall execute and record a memorandum of lease in a form mutually agreed upon by the parties, which memorandum shall set forth the parties, the Premises, the Lease Term, and such other provisions as the parties may agree.\n\n**Section 26.08 --- Force Majeure.** Neither party shall be liable for any delay or failure in performance of any obligation under this Lease (other than Tenant\\'s obligation to pay rent and other monetary sums) to the extent caused by events beyond such party\\'s reasonable control, including but not limited to acts of God, fire, flood, earthquake, epidemic, pandemic, war, terrorism, civil unrest, strikes, labor disputes, governmental restrictions, or failure of utilities.\n\n**Section 26.09 --- Authority.** Each individual executing this Lease on behalf of a party represents and warrants that he or she is duly authorized to execute and deliver this Lease on behalf of such party and that this Lease is binding upon such party in accordance with its terms.\n\n**Section 26.10 --- Counterparts.** This Lease may be executed in two or more counterparts, each of which shall be deemed an original but all of which together shall constitute one and the same instrument. Signatures transmitted by electronic means (including PDF) shall be deemed original signatures for all purposes.\n\n**Section 26.11 --- Time of Essence.** Time is of the essence with respect to the performance of each and every obligation of the parties under this Lease.\n\n**Section 26.12 --- Attorneys\\' Fees.** In any action or proceeding arising out of or in connection with this Lease, the prevailing party shall be entitled to recover its reasonable attorneys\\' fees and costs from the non-prevailing party, in addition to any other relief to which such prevailing party may be entitled.\n\n**Section 26.13 --- Rules and Regulations.** Tenant agrees to observe and comply with the Building\\'s rules and regulations attached hereto as **Exhibit E**, as the same may be reasonably modified, amended, or supplemented by Landlord from time to time upon not less than thirty (30) days\\' prior written notice to Tenant, provided that such modifications are uniformly applied to all tenants of the Building and do not materially and adversely affect Tenant\\'s use and enjoyment of the Premises.\n\n**[ARTICLE XXVII --- LIMITATION OF LANDLORD\\'S LIABILITY]{.underline}**\n\nTenant agrees that it shall look solely to the interest of Landlord in the Building and the land upon which the Building is situated for satisfaction of any claim, judgment, or decree requiring the payment of money by Landlord arising out of or in connection with this Lease. No partner, member, manager, officer, director, shareholder, or beneficiary of Landlord shall have any personal liability for any obligation of Landlord under this Lease. In no event shall Tenant\\'s aggregate damages against Landlord exceed the value of Landlord\\'s equity interest in the Building and its proceeds.\n\n**[ARTICLE XXVIII --- EXHIBITS]{.underline}**\n\nThe following Exhibits are attached hereto and incorporated herein by this reference as though fully set forth in the body of this Lease:\n\n> • **Exhibit A** --- Floor Plan of Premises\n>\n> • **Exhibit B** --- Rent Schedule\n>\n> • **Exhibit C** --- Work Letter (Tenant Improvement Allowance)\n>\n> • **Exhibit D** --- Parking Addendum\n>\n> • **Exhibit E** --- Building Rules and Regulations\n\n*\\[Signature Page Follows\\]*\n\nIN WITNESS WHEREOF, the parties hereto have caused this Office Lease Agreement to be duly executed as of the Lease Date first written above.\n\n**LANDLORD:**\n\nTHORNFIELD REALTY HOLDINGS LP, a North Carolina limited partnership\n\nBy: Thornfield Management Corp.,\n\na North Carolina corporation, its General Partner\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: Renata Thornfield-Kiess\n\nTitle: President\n\nDate: February 28, 2022\n\n**TENANT:**\n\nVERDANA SOFTWARE SOLUTIONS INC., a Delaware corporation\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nTitle: Chief Executive Officer\n\nDate: February 28, 2022\n\n**[EXHIBIT A --- FLOOR PLAN OF PREMISES]{.underline}**\n\nAttached hereto is the floor plan of the 8th Floor of Concord Office Tower, 300 Concord Plaza Drive, Atlanta, Georgia 30309, depicting the Premises consisting of approximately 18,750 rentable square feet. The 8th Floor encompasses the entire floor plate of the Building. The Premises are outlined in bold on the attached plan.\n\n*\\[Floor plan to be attached.\\]*\n\n**[EXHIBIT B --- RENT SCHEDULE]{.underline}**\n\nThe following Rent Schedule sets forth the Base Rent payable by Tenant for each Lease Year during the Lease Term, based on a Year 1 rate of \\$34.00 per RSF per annum with three percent (3%) annual compounding escalation and 18,750 RSF:\n\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Lease Year**   **Dates**                       **\\$/RSF/Year**   **Annual Rent**   **Monthly Rent**\n  ---------------- ------------------------------- ----------------- ----------------- ------------------\n  Year 1           July 1, 2022 -- June 30, 2023   \\$34.00           \\$637,500.00      \\$53,125.00\n\n  Year 2           July 1, 2023 -- June 30, 2024   \\$35.02           \\$656,625.00      \\$54,718.75\n\n  Year 3           July 1, 2024 -- June 30, 2025   \\$36.07           \\$676,312.50      \\$56,359.38\n\n  Year 4           July 1, 2025 -- June 30, 2026   \\$37.15           \\$696,562.50      \\$58,046.88\n\n  Year 5           July 1, 2026 -- June 30, 2027   \\$38.26           \\$717,375.00      \\$59,781.25\n\n  Year 6           July 1, 2027 -- June 30, 2028   \\$39.41           \\$738,937.50      \\$61,578.13\n\n  Year 7           July 1, 2028 -- June 30, 2029   \\$40.59           \\$761,062.50      \\$63,421.88\n\n  Year 8           July 1, 2029 -- June 30, 2030   \\$41.81           \\$783,937.50      \\$65,328.13\n\n  Year 9           July 1, 2030 -- June 30, 2031   \\$43.06           \\$807,375.00      \\$67,281.25\n\n  Year 10          July 1, 2031 -- June 30, 2032   \\$44.35           \\$831,562.50      \\$69,296.88\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nAll amounts are rounded to the nearest cent. Annual Rent is calculated as the applicable \\$/RSF/Year multiplied by 18,750 RSF. Monthly Rent is one-twelfth (1/12) of Annual Rent.\n\n**[EXHIBIT C --- WORK LETTER (TENANT IMPROVEMENT ALLOWANCE)]{.underline}**\n\n**1. TI Allowance.** Landlord shall provide Tenant with a tenant improvement allowance in the amount of Fifty-Five and 00/100 Dollars (\\$55.00) per RSF of the Premises (18,750 RSF), for a total TI Allowance of One Million Thirty-One Thousand Two Hundred Fifty and 00/100 Dollars (\\$1,031,250.00).\n\n**2. Scope of Allowance.** The TI Allowance may be applied by Tenant toward the hard and soft costs of designing and constructing Tenant\\'s initial improvements to the Premises, including without limitation: (a) architectural and engineering design fees; (b) permit and inspection fees; (c) construction hard costs (demolition, framing, drywall, flooring, ceiling, millwork, painting, doors, and hardware); (d) mechanical, electrical, and plumbing work within the Premises; (e) data/telecommunications cabling and infrastructure; (f) fire protection and life safety modifications; and (g) construction management or project management fees, not to exceed three percent (3%) of total hard costs. The TI Allowance shall not be applied toward the cost of Tenant\\'s furniture, fixtures, equipment, or moving expenses.\n\n**3. Plans and Approval.** Tenant shall submit complete construction documents (including architectural, mechanical, electrical, and plumbing drawings and specifications) to Landlord for review and approval within sixty (60) days after the Lease Date. Landlord shall approve the construction documents or provide written comments within fifteen (15) business days after receipt. Landlord\\'s approval shall not be unreasonably withheld, conditioned, or delayed.\n\n**4. Disbursement.** The TI Allowance shall be disbursed to Tenant (or, at Landlord\\'s election, directly to Tenant\\'s general contractor) on a monthly basis, upon Tenant\\'s submission of written draw requests accompanied by (a) the general contractor\\'s application for payment (AIA G702/G703 format or equivalent); (b) unconditional lien waivers from the general contractor and all subcontractors for previously paid amounts; and (c) conditional lien waivers for the current draw amount. Landlord shall retain ten percent (10%) of each disbursement as retainage, which shall be released upon substantial completion of the Tenant Improvements and Tenant\\'s receipt of a certificate of occupancy (or its equivalent) from the applicable governmental authority.\n\n**5. Excess Costs.** Any costs of the Tenant Improvements in excess of the TI Allowance shall be the sole responsibility of Tenant and shall be paid by Tenant directly.\n\n**6. Unused Allowance.** Any unused portion of the TI Allowance remaining after completion of the Tenant Improvements and final disbursement shall not be credited to Tenant, applied against rent, or paid to Tenant in cash. Any such unused portion shall be deemed forfeited.\n\n**7. Amortization.** For purposes of calculating early termination or contraction recapture fees under Articles XIII and XIV of this Lease, the TI Allowance shall be amortized over the initial ten (10) year Lease Term at seven percent (7%) per annum interest, commencing on the Commencement Date, using a standard mortgage-style amortization schedule with one hundred twenty (120) equal monthly payments.\n\n**8. Ownership.** All Tenant Improvements constructed utilizing the TI Allowance shall, upon installation, become the property of Landlord and shall remain upon the Premises at the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease, subject to Landlord\\'s right to require removal of designated non-standard improvements as set forth in Article VII of this Lease.\n\n**[EXHIBIT D --- PARKING ADDENDUM]{.underline}**\n\n**1. Unreserved Parking Spaces.** Tenant shall be entitled to fifty-six (56) unreserved parking spaces in the Building\\'s parking garage throughout the Lease Term, provided at no additional charge. This allocation represents a ratio of approximately 2.99 spaces per 1,000 RSF (56 spaces ÷ 18.750 thousands of RSF). Unreserved spaces shall be available on a first-come, first-served basis in the areas of the parking garage designated by Landlord for unreserved tenant parking.\n\n**2. Reserved Executive Parking Spaces.** Tenant shall be entitled to eight (8) reserved parking spaces in the Building\\'s parking garage, located on Level P1, in locations to be designated by Landlord. The monthly charge for each reserved parking space shall be One Hundred Seventy-Five and 00/100 Dollars (\\$175.00) per space (\\$175.00 × 8 = \\$1,400.00 per month; \\$16,800.00 per year). Reserved parking charges shall be payable monthly as Additional Rent, due on the first day of each calendar month. Landlord may adjust the reserved parking rate annually, effective on each anniversary of the Commencement Date, to reflect prevailing market rates for the Building\\'s parking garage; provided, however, that such adjustment shall not exceed five percent (5%) per annum.\n\n**3. Access.** Tenant and its employees shall have twenty-four (24) hour, seven (7) day per week access to the parking garage. Landlord shall provide access cards or key fobs to Tenant\\'s designated parking users at no additional charge, subject to a reasonable replacement fee for lost or damaged cards.\n\n**4. Maintenance.** Landlord shall be responsible for maintaining the parking garage in a clean, safe, and well-lit condition, and shall provide reasonable security measures consistent with comparable Class A office buildings in the Atlanta midtown submarket.\n\n**5. Transfer.** Tenant\\'s parking rights under this Exhibit D are appurtenant to this Lease and may not be separately assigned, transferred, or conveyed, except in connection with a permitted assignment or subletting of the Premises under Article XI of this Lease.\n\n**6. Visitor Validation.** Landlord shall provide a reasonable visitor parking validation program, allowing Tenant\\'s visitors to park in designated visitor areas of the Building\\'s garage at no charge to Tenant or its visitors, subject to reasonable time limitations and Building policies.\n\n**[EXHIBIT E --- BUILDING RULES AND REGULATIONS]{.underline}**\n\nThe following rules and regulations are in effect for Concord Office Tower, 300 Concord Plaza Drive, Atlanta, Georgia 30309. Tenant, its employees, agents, and invitees shall comply with these rules and regulations at all times.\n\n**1. Building Hours.** Standard business hours are 8:00 AM to 6:00 PM, Monday through Friday, and 8:00 AM to 1:00 PM, Saturday, excluding the following Building holidays: New Year\\'s Day, Martin Lueger King Jr. Day, Memorial Day, Independence Day, Labor Day, Thanksgiving Day, the day after Thanksgiving, and Christmas Day. The Building shall be accessible to ...(truncated)"}
