{"turn": 10, "role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "call_00_PE2aMJR3XJL1FIcMlcvy6998", "tool_name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/workspace/documents/pint-platter-lease.docx\"}", "result": "**[LEASE AGREEMENT]{.underline}**\n\n**Haywood Village Shops --- Unit 4** **92 Haywood Street, Asheville, North Carolina 28801**\n\nThis Lease Agreement (this \\\"Lease\\\") is made and entered into as of the 15th day of May, 2020 (the \\\"Lease Date\\\"), by and between:\n\n**LANDLORD:** Thornfield Realty Holdings LP, a North Carolina limited partnership, whose general partner is Thornfield Management Corp., a North Carolina corporation, with its principal office located at 500 South Tryon Street, Suite 2200, Charlotte, North Carolina 28202 (hereinafter referred to as \\\"Landlord\\\");\n\nand\n\n**TENANT:** The Pint & Platter Restaurant Group LLC, a North Carolina limited liability company, whose sole member is Lance Whitford, an individual, with its principal office located at 92 Haywood Street, Unit 4, Asheville, North Carolina 28801 (hereinafter referred to as \\\"Tenant\\\").\n\n**RECITALS**\n\n**WHEREAS**, Landlord is the fee simple owner of that certain retail shopping center commonly known as Haywood Village Shops, located at 92 Haywood Street, Asheville, Buncombe County, North Carolina 28801, containing approximately 62,000 rentable square feet of leasable retail and commercial space, together with all improvements, common areas, and appurtenances thereto; and\n\n**WHEREAS**, Tenant desires to lease a portion of Haywood Village Shops for the operation of a full-service restaurant, bar, and related food-and-beverage establishment; and\n\n**WHEREAS**, Landlord is willing to lease such space to Tenant upon the terms and conditions set forth herein;\n\n**NOW, THEREFORE**, in consideration of the mutual covenants and agreements contained herein, and for other good and valuable consideration, the receipt and sufficiency of which are hereby acknowledged, Landlord and Tenant agree as follows:\n\n**[ARTICLE 1 --- DEFINITIONS]{.underline}**\n\nAs used in this Lease, the following terms shall have the meanings set forth below:\n\n**\\\"Additional Rent\\\"** shall mean all sums payable by Tenant to Landlord under this Lease other than Base Rent and Percentage Rent, including without limitation Tenant\\'s Pro Rata Share of Operating Expenses, real estate taxes, insurance premiums, and CAM Expenses, as well as any late charges, interest, and other amounts due hereunder.\n\n**\\\"Base Rent\\\"** shall mean the annual base rental amount payable by Tenant as set forth in the Rent Schedule attached hereto as Exhibit B and incorporated herein by this reference, subject to escalation as provided in Section 3.2 of this Lease.\n\n**\\\"Breakpoint\\\"** shall have the meaning set forth in Section 3.3 of this Lease.\n\n**\\\"Buildout Period\\\"** shall mean the period commencing on the Lease Date and ending on the day immediately preceding the Commencement Date, during which Tenant shall design, permit, and construct its leasehold improvements within the Premises in accordance with plans approved by Landlord.\n\n**\\\"Commencement Date\\\"** shall mean October 1, 2020.\n\n**\\\"Common Area Maintenance (CAM) Expenses\\\"** shall mean all costs and expenses incurred by Landlord in operating, maintaining, repairing, replacing, and managing the common areas of Haywood Village Shops, including but not limited to parking areas, sidewalks, driveways, access roads, landscaping, irrigation, exterior lighting, signage (excluding tenant-specific signage), stormwater management systems, utilities serving common areas, janitorial and cleaning services for common areas, snow and ice removal, security services, and a management fee not to exceed four percent (4%) of gross rental revenue of the Shopping Center.\n\n**\\\"Expiration Date\\\"** shall mean September 30, 2030, unless the Term is sooner terminated or extended as provided herein.\n\n**\\\"Gross Sales\\\"** shall mean all revenues from the sale of food, beverages (including alcoholic beverages), merchandise, and services of any kind made in, upon, or from the Premises, whether for cash, credit, or otherwise, including catering orders and delivery orders originating from the Premises, and revenues from online or mobile orders fulfilled at or from the Premises. The following items shall be excluded from Gross Sales: (i) tips and gratuities received by employees; (ii) sales tax, meals tax, or any other tax collected from customers and remitted to a governmental authority; (iii) the amount of any cash or credit refunds made to customers for goods or services previously included in Gross Sales; (iv) employee meals provided at cost; (v) proceeds from the sale of gift cards or gift certificates (provided, however, that the redemption of such gift cards or certificates shall be included in Gross Sales at the time of redemption); and (vi) proceeds from the sale of trade fixtures or equipment not in the ordinary course of business.\n\n**\\\"Haywood Village Shops\\\"** or **\\\"Shopping Center\\\"** shall mean the retail shopping center located at 92 Haywood Street, Asheville, North Carolina 28801, containing approximately 62,000 rentable square feet of leasable space, together with all improvements, common areas, parking areas, and appurtenances, as more particularly depicted on the site plan attached hereto as Exhibit A.\n\n**\\\"Lease Date\\\"** shall mean May 15, 2020.\n\n**\\\"Lease Term\\\"** or **\\\"Term\\\"** shall mean the period of ten (10) years commencing on the Commencement Date and expiring on the Expiration Date, unless sooner terminated or extended pursuant to the terms of this Lease, including any Renewal Term exercised by Tenant in accordance with Article 11.\n\n**\\\"Lease Year\\\"** shall mean each successive twelve (12) month period during the Term. Lease Year 1 shall commence on October 1, 2020 and expire on September 30, 2021. Lease Year 2 shall commence on October 1, 2021 and expire on September 30, 2022, and so forth for each successive Lease Year during the Term.\n\n**\\\"Natural Breakpoint\\\"** shall mean, with respect to Percentage Rent, the quotient obtained by dividing the annual Base Rent for the applicable Lease Year by the Percentage Rent Rate (i.e., Base Rent ÷ 0.06). The Natural Breakpoint shall be recalculated for each Lease Year to reflect changes in Base Rent resulting from CPI adjustments or other modifications. Gross Sales in excess of the Natural Breakpoint during any Lease Year shall be subject to Percentage Rent as set forth in Section 3.3 of this Lease.\n\n**\\\"Operating Expenses\\\"** shall mean the aggregate of all real estate taxes and assessments, property insurance premiums, and CAM Expenses.\n\n**\\\"Percentage Rent\\\"** shall mean an amount equal to six percent (6%) of Tenant\\'s annual Gross Sales in excess of the Breakpoint, as more particularly described in Section 3.3 of this Lease.\n\n**\\\"Percentage Rent Rate\\\"** shall mean six percent (6%).\n\n**\\\"Permitted Use\\\"** shall mean the operation of a full-service restaurant, bar, and related food-and-beverage service, together with such ancillary uses as are customarily associated therewith, including on-premises and off-premises catering, private event hosting, and delivery services.\n\n**\\\"Premises\\\"** shall mean approximately 4,400 rentable square feet (\\\"RSF\\\") of ground-floor retail space, including the patio area, designated as Unit 4, Haywood Village Shops, 92 Haywood Street, Asheville, North Carolina 28801, as more particularly depicted on the floor plan attached hereto as Exhibit A.\n\n**\\\"Pro Rata Share\\\"** shall mean Tenant\\'s proportionate share of Operating Expenses, calculated as the ratio of the RSF of the Premises (4,400 RSF) to the total leasable RSF of Haywood Village Shops (62,000 RSF), which is approximately 7.10%.\n\n**\\\"Security Deposit\\\"** shall mean the sum of Nineteen Thousand Eight Hundred Dollars (\\$19,800.00) deposited by Tenant with Landlord pursuant to Article 4 of this Lease.\n\n**[ARTICLE 2 --- PREMISES, TERM, AND DELIVERY]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 2.1 --- Premises**\n\nLandlord hereby leases to Tenant, and Tenant hereby leases from Landlord, the Premises consisting of approximately 4,400 RSF of ground-floor retail space, together with the adjacent patio area, designated as Unit 4, Haywood Village Shops, 92 Haywood Street, Asheville, North Carolina 28801, as more particularly depicted on the floor plan attached hereto as Exhibit A and incorporated herein by this reference. The Premises shall include all improvements, fixtures, and appurtenances constructed or installed therein by Tenant in accordance with this Lease.\n\n**Section 2.2 --- Term**\n\nThe Lease Term shall be ten (10) years, commencing on October 1, 2020 (the \\\"Commencement Date\\\") and expiring on September 30, 2030 (the \\\"Expiration Date\\\"), unless sooner terminated or extended as provided herein. The Buildout Period shall run from May 15, 2020 through September 30, 2020 (approximately four and one-half (4.5) months), during which Tenant shall design, permit, and construct its leasehold improvements in accordance with plans and specifications approved by Landlord. No Base Rent or Percentage Rent shall accrue or be due during the Buildout Period; provided, however, that Tenant shall be responsible for all utility charges, insurance premiums, and any costs associated with the construction of its improvements during such period.\n\n**Section 2.3 --- Delivery and Condition**\n\nLandlord shall deliver the Premises to Tenant in broom-clean, shell condition on or before the Lease Date. Tenant acknowledges that it has inspected the Premises and, subject to Landlord\\'s obligation to deliver all base building systems (including electrical, plumbing, HVAC, and fire/life safety systems) in good working order, Tenant accepts the Premises in its \\\"as-is\\\" condition. Landlord makes no representations or warranties concerning the condition of the Premises except as expressly set forth in this Lease.\n\n**Section 2.4 --- Holdover**\n\nIf Tenant holds over and remains in possession of the Premises after the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease without Landlord\\'s prior written consent, Tenant shall be deemed a tenant-at-sufferance and shall pay holdover rent equal to one hundred fifty percent (150%) of the Base Rent in effect during the last month of the Term, plus all Additional Rent and other charges due hereunder. Such holdover tenancy shall not constitute a renewal or extension of this Lease, and Landlord shall have the right to terminate such holdover tenancy at any time upon thirty (30) days\\' written notice.\n\n**[ARTICLE 3 --- RENT]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 3.1 --- Base Rent**\n\nCommencing on the Commencement Date, Tenant shall pay to Landlord Base Rent in equal monthly installments, in advance, on the first (1st) day of each calendar month during the Term. Base Rent for Lease Year 1 shall be Eighteen Dollars (\\$18.00) per RSF per annum, which, based on 4,400 RSF, equals Seventy-Nine Thousand Two Hundred Dollars (\\$79,200.00) per annum, or Six Thousand Six Hundred Dollars (\\$6,600.00) per month. All Base Rent shall be due and payable without notice, demand, deduction, setoff, or abatement, except as expressly provided in this Lease. If the Commencement Date or Expiration Date falls on a date other than the first or last day of a calendar month, Base Rent for such partial month shall be prorated on a per diem basis.\n\n**Section 3.2 --- Rent Escalation (CPI Adjustment)**\n\nCommencing on October 1, 2021 (the first day of Lease Year 2) and on each anniversary thereof during the Term (each, an \\\"Adjustment Date\\\"), the Base Rent shall be adjusted based on the percentage increase in the Consumer Price Index --- All Urban Consumers (CPI-U), U.S. City Average, All Items (1982--84 = 100), as published by the Bureau of Labor Statistics of the United States Department of Labor. The CPI adjustment shall be calculated by comparing the CPI-U index published for the month that is three (3) months prior to the applicable Adjustment Date (the \\\"Current Index\\\") to the CPI-U index for the corresponding month of the immediately preceding year (the \\\"Base Index\\\"), and the percentage increase shall equal (Current Index − Base Index) ÷ Base Index × 100. Notwithstanding the foregoing, the annual CPI adjustment shall be subject to (a) a minimum floor adjustment of two percent (2%) per annum, and (b) a maximum cap adjustment of four percent (4%) per annum. If the CPI-U is discontinued or substantially modified, Landlord shall select a comparable index.\n\nThe Base Rent for the first five (5) Lease Years, as adjusted in accordance with this Section 3.2, is as follows:\n\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Lease Year**   **Period**                    **Base Rent (\\$/RSF)**   **Annual Base Rent**   **Monthly Base Rent**\n  ---------------- ----------------------------- ------------------------ ---------------------- -----------------------\n  1                Oct 1, 2020 -- Sep 30, 2021   \\$18.000                 \\$79,200.00            \\$6,600.00\n\n  2                Oct 1, 2021 -- Sep 30, 2022   \\$18.684                 \\$82,209.60            \\$6,850.80\n\n  3                Oct 1, 2022 -- Sep 30, 2023   \\$19.431                 \\$85,496.40            \\$7,124.70\n\n  4                Oct 1, 2023 -- Sep 30, 2024   \\$19.917                 \\$87,634.80            \\$7,302.90\n\n  5                Oct 1, 2024 -- Sep 30, 2025   \\$20.315                 \\$89,386.00            \\$7,448.83\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nFor Lease Years 6 through 10, Base Rent shall be determined annually in accordance with this Section 3.2, applying the applicable CPI adjustment with the two percent (2%) floor and four percent (4%) cap. Landlord shall provide written notice to Tenant of the adjusted Base Rent not less than thirty (30) days prior to the applicable Adjustment Date. In the event Landlord fails to provide timely notice, Tenant shall continue to pay the prior Lease Year\\'s Base Rent until notice is given, and Tenant shall pay any resulting deficiency within thirty (30) days of receipt of such notice.\n\n**Section 3.3 --- Percentage Rent**\n\nIn addition to Base Rent, commencing in Lease Year 2, Tenant shall pay to Landlord Percentage Rent equal to six percent (6%) of Tenant\\'s annual Gross Sales in excess of the Breakpoint. For purposes of calculating Percentage Rent, the Breakpoint shall be One Million Three Hundred Fifty Thousand Dollars (\\$1,350,000) for each Lease Year during the Term. For clarity, Percentage Rent for any Lease Year shall be calculated as follows: Percentage Rent = (Gross Sales − \\$1,350,000) × 0.06, provided that if Gross Sales for such Lease Year do not exceed \\$1,350,000, no Percentage Rent shall be due for such Lease Year.\n\nPercentage Rent shall be calculated and paid annually within sixty (60) days after the end of each Lease Year. Tenant shall submit to Landlord a certified statement of Gross Sales for the preceding Lease Year, prepared in accordance with generally accepted accounting principles, within thirty (30) days after the end of such Lease Year.\n\nLandlord shall have the right, at Landlord\\'s expense (except as provided below), to audit Tenant\\'s books and records relating to Gross Sales during normal business hours upon not less than fifteen (15) days\\' prior written notice. Such audit right may be exercised no more than once per Lease Year and shall cover the three (3) most recent completed Lease Years. If any such audit reveals an understatement of Gross Sales by more than three percent (3%) for any audited Lease Year, Tenant shall (a) pay the cost of such audit, (b) pay the resulting deficiency in Percentage Rent, and (c) pay interest on the deficiency at the rate of one and one-half percent (1.5%) per month from the date such Percentage Rent was originally due until the date of payment.\n\n**Section 3.4 --- Reporting of Gross Sales**\n\nTenant shall maintain complete and accurate books and records of all Gross Sales in accordance with generally accepted accounting principles, consistently applied. Tenant shall furnish to Landlord monthly sales reports, in a form reasonably satisfactory to Landlord, within fifteen (15) days after the end of each calendar month, reflecting the Gross Sales for such month. In addition, Tenant shall furnish an annual certified statement of Gross Sales for each Lease Year within thirty (30) days after the end of such Lease Year, which statement shall be certified by an authorized officer of Tenant. Landlord\\'s audit rights with respect to Gross Sales records shall be as set forth in Section 3.3 above.\n\n**Section 3.5 --- Additional Rent / NNN Charges**\n\nIn addition to Base Rent and Percentage Rent, Tenant shall pay as Additional Rent its Pro Rata Share of the following Operating Expenses: (a) all real estate taxes, assessments, and governmental charges levied against the Shopping Center (including Landlord\\'s reasonable costs of contesting such taxes); (b) all premiums for property and liability insurance maintained by Landlord for the Shopping Center; and (c) all CAM Expenses as defined in Article 1 hereof. Landlord shall provide Tenant with an annual estimate of Additional Rent prior to or at the beginning of each calendar year, and Tenant shall pay one-twelfth (1/12) of the estimated annual amount on the first day of each calendar month. Within ninety (90) days after the end of each calendar year, Landlord shall provide Tenant with a reconciliation statement showing actual Operating Expenses for the preceding year. Any overpayment by Tenant shall be credited against the next monthly installment(s) of Additional Rent; any underpayment shall be due and payable by Tenant within thirty (30) days of receipt of the reconciliation statement.\n\n**Section 3.6 --- Late Charge and Interest**\n\nAny payment of Base Rent, Percentage Rent, Additional Rent, or any other sum due under this Lease that is not received by Landlord within five (5) days after the date when due shall bear a late charge equal to five percent (5%) of the overdue amount. In addition, all overdue amounts shall accrue interest at the rate of one and one-half percent (1.5%) per month (eighteen percent (18%) per annum), or the maximum rate permitted by applicable North Carolina law, whichever is less, from the due date until paid in full.\n\n**[ARTICLE 4 --- SECURITY DEPOSIT]{.underline}**\n\nTenant has deposited with Landlord the sum of Nineteen Thousand Eight Hundred Dollars (\\$19,800.00) as a security deposit (the \\\"Security Deposit\\\"), which amount is equal to three (3) months of Lease Year 1 Base Rent. The Security Deposit shall be held by Landlord as security for the faithful performance by Tenant of all terms, covenants, and conditions of this Lease. Landlord shall not be required to hold the Security Deposit in a separate account or to pay interest thereon. In the event of any default by Tenant hereunder, Landlord may, without prejudice to any other remedy, apply all or any portion of the Security Deposit to cure such default or to compensate Landlord for any loss or damage resulting therefrom. Tenant shall replenish any amounts so applied within ten (10) days of written notice from Landlord. Provided Tenant is not then in default, the Security Deposit (less any amounts properly applied) shall be returned to Tenant within thirty (30) days following the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease and Tenant\\'s delivery of the Premises in accordance with Article 16 hereof.\n\n**[ARTICLE 5 --- USE OF PREMISES]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 5.1 --- Permitted Use**\n\nTenant shall use and occupy the Premises solely for the Permitted Use, which is the operation of a full-service restaurant, bar, and related food-and-beverage service (including dine-in, takeout, delivery, and catering services), and for no other purpose without Landlord\\'s prior written consent, which consent may be withheld in Landlord\\'s sole and absolute discretion. Tenant shall operate its business in the Premises during customary operating hours for the Shopping Center and shall maintain a level of staffing, inventory, and service consistent with the operation of a first-class full-service restaurant.\n\n**Section 5.2 --- Exclusive Use**\n\nLandlord hereby grants to Tenant the exclusive right to operate a full-service restaurant within Haywood Village Shops during the Term (the \\\"Exclusive Use Right\\\"). For purposes of this Section 5.2, \\\"full-service restaurant\\\" shall mean an establishment primarily engaged in the sale of food and beverages for on-premises consumption, offering table service by waitstaff, and maintaining a menu of entrées, appetizers, and desserts. Notwithstanding the foregoing, the Exclusive Use Right shall not restrict Landlord from leasing space within the Shopping Center to quick-service or fast-casual restaurant operators, coffee shops, bakeries, ice cream or frozen dessert shops, or specialty food retailers, provided that any such quick-service or fast-casual restaurant operator occupies premises of less than 2,000 RSF within the Shopping Center. In the event Landlord violates the Exclusive Use Right by leasing to or permitting the operation of a competing full-service restaurant within the Shopping Center, Tenant shall be entitled to (i) specific performance and injunctive relief to enforce the Exclusive Use Right, and (ii) abatement of Base Rent for the period during which such violation continues.\n\n**Section 5.3 --- Radius Restriction**\n\nDuring the Term and for a period of twelve (12) months following the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease, Tenant shall not, directly or indirectly, whether as owner, partner, member, shareholder, officer, director, employee, consultant, or otherwise, open, operate, manage, or have any ownership interest in any restaurant or food-and-beverage establishment within a three (3) mile radius of the Premises (the \\\"Restricted Area\\\"). The foregoing restriction shall not apply to any restaurant that is open and operating as of the Lease Date. In the event Tenant violates this radius restriction, Landlord shall be entitled, in addition to any other remedies available at law or in equity, to include in Gross Sales hereunder all revenues from any such competing restaurant operated by Tenant or its affiliates within the Restricted Area, for purposes of calculating Percentage Rent under Section 3.3.\n\n**Section 5.4 --- Compliance with Laws; Licenses and Permits**\n\nTenant shall, at its sole cost and expense, comply with all applicable federal, state, and local laws, ordinances, rules, regulations, and orders in connection with its use and occupancy of the Premises. Tenant shall obtain and maintain at its sole cost all licenses, permits, and approvals required for the operation of the Premises, including without limitation health department permits, fire and safety permits, building permits, certificates of occupancy, and ABC permits for the sale and service of alcoholic beverages.\n\n**Section 5.5 --- Liquor License / ABC Permits**\n\nLandlord covenants to cooperate reasonably with Tenant\\'s application for and maintenance of all Alcoholic Beverage Control (\\\"ABC\\\") permits and licenses necessary for Tenant\\'s operation, including executing such landlord consents and certifications as may be required by the North Carolina ABC Commission. The parties acknowledge that the execution of this Lease was contingent upon Tenant obtaining an ABC permit within one hundred twenty (120) days of the Commencement Date, and the parties further acknowledge that such condition has been satisfied as of the date hereof.\n\n**[ARTICLE 6 --- OPERATING EXPENSES AND TAXES]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 6.1 --- Triple Net Structure**\n\nThis Lease is intended to be a \\\"triple net\\\" (NNN) lease, and Tenant acknowledges that the Base Rent set forth herein is a net amount to Landlord. Accordingly, in addition to Base Rent and Percentage Rent, Tenant shall pay its Pro Rata Share of (a) all real estate taxes and assessments levied against the Shopping Center, (b) all premiums for property and liability insurance maintained by Landlord for the Shopping Center, and (c) all CAM Expenses, all as more particularly set forth in Section 3.5 above. In no event shall Operating Expenses include costs attributable to Landlord\\'s financing, depreciation, capital improvements (except to the extent amortized over their useful life as required to comply with applicable law or to reduce Operating Expenses), or leasing commissions.\n\n**Section 6.2 --- Estimates and Reconciliation**\n\nLandlord shall furnish Tenant with a written estimate of Tenant\\'s Pro Rata Share of Operating Expenses for each calendar year prior to or at the beginning of such year. Tenant shall pay one-twelfth (1/12) of the estimated amount on the first day of each calendar month. Within ninety (90) days following the end of each calendar year, Landlord shall provide a reconciliation statement showing actual Operating Expenses, and any overpayment or underpayment shall be adjusted as set forth in Section 3.5 above. Tenant shall have the right, upon not less than thirty (30) days\\' prior written notice and at Tenant\\'s expense, to audit Landlord\\'s books and records relating to Operating Expenses for the preceding calendar year.\n\n**[ARTICLE 7 --- MAINTENANCE AND REPAIRS]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 7.1 --- Tenant\\'s Obligations**\n\nTenant shall, at its sole cost and expense, maintain the interior of the Premises in good order, condition, and repair, including without limitation the HVAC systems exclusively serving the Premises, plumbing fixtures and connections, grease traps and interceptors, kitchen exhaust and ventilation systems, interior walls, ceilings, and floor coverings, electrical systems within the Premises, patio area (including furniture, railings, and surfaces), and all trade fixtures and equipment. Tenant shall enter into and maintain a preventive maintenance contract for the HVAC systems serving the Premises with a qualified contractor reasonably approved by Landlord.\n\n**Section 7.2 --- Landlord\\'s Obligations**\n\nLandlord shall maintain and repair the structural elements of the building in which the Premises are located, including the foundation, exterior load-bearing walls, and roof structure, as well as the common areas of the Shopping Center (including parking areas, sidewalks, landscaping, and exterior lighting) and shared building systems (such as main utility lines and fire suppression systems) to the extent such systems are not exclusively serving the Premises. The cost of such maintenance and repair to the extent constituting CAM Expenses shall be included in Operating Expenses and allocated to tenants in accordance with their Pro Rata Shares.\n\n**[ARTICLE 8 --- INSURANCE]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 8.1 --- Tenant\\'s Insurance**\n\nTenant shall obtain and maintain at its sole cost and expense throughout the Term the following insurance coverage: (a) Commercial General Liability insurance, including coverage for bodily injury, property damage, personal injury, and products/completed operations liability, with limits of not less than One Million Dollars (\\$1,000,000) per occurrence and Two Million Dollars (\\$2,000,000) in the aggregate; (b) Liquor Liability insurance with limits of not less than One Million Dollars (\\$1,000,000) per occurrence; (c) Property insurance covering Tenant\\'s trade fixtures, equipment, inventory, and leasehold improvements at full replacement cost; (d) Workers\\' Compensation insurance as required by the laws of the State of North Carolina, and Employer\\'s Liability insurance with limits of not less than Five Hundred Thousand Dollars (\\$500,000); and (e) Business Interruption insurance covering not less than twelve (12) months of rent obligations under this Lease. All policies shall name Landlord and Landlord\\'s mortgagee(s) as additional insureds and shall provide for not less than thirty (30) days\\' prior written notice to Landlord of cancellation or material modification.\n\n**Section 8.2 --- Landlord\\'s Insurance**\n\nLandlord shall maintain property insurance on the Shopping Center (building shell and common areas) on a replacement cost basis and Commercial General Liability insurance with commercially reasonable limits. The cost of such insurance shall be included in Operating Expenses and allocated to tenants in accordance with their respective Pro Rata Shares.\n\n**Section 8.3 --- Waiver of Subrogation**\n\nLandlord and Tenant each hereby waive all rights of recovery against the other party for any loss or damage to the extent such loss or damage is covered by the waiving party\\'s insurance policies (or would have been covered had the waiving party maintained the insurance required under this Lease). Each party shall cause its respective insurance carriers to issue appropriate waiver of subrogation endorsements in favor of the other party.\n\n**[ARTICLE 9 --- ASSIGNMENT AND SUBLETTING]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 9.1 --- Restriction**\n\nTenant shall not assign, transfer, mortgage, pledge, or encumber this Lease, or sublet all or any portion of the Premises, without the prior written consent of Landlord, which consent shall not be unreasonably withheld, conditioned, or delayed. Any attempted assignment or subletting without Landlord\\'s consent shall be void and of no force or effect and shall constitute an Event of Default under this Lease.\n\n**Section 9.2 --- Conditions to Consent**\n\nIn evaluating any proposed assignment or subletting, Landlord may consider the proposed assignee\\'s or subtenant\\'s (a) financial condition and creditworthiness, (b) business reputation and character, (c) proposed use of the Premises (which must conform to the Permitted Use), and (d) restaurant operating experience. Landlord may also require that Tenant remain liable for all obligations under this Lease and that the assignee or subtenant execute an assumption agreement in form satisfactory to Landlord.\n\n**Section 9.3 --- No Release**\n\nNo assignment or subletting, whether or not consented to by Landlord, shall release Tenant from its obligations under this Lease. The assignee or subtenant shall assume in writing all obligations of Tenant under this Lease for the benefit of Landlord, and a copy of such assumption agreement shall be delivered to Landlord prior to the effective date of the assignment or subletting.\n\n**[ARTICLE 10 --- CO-TENANCY]{.underline}**\n\nIf the aggregate occupancy of Haywood Village Shops falls below seventy percent (70%) of the total leasable area of the Shopping Center for more than one hundred eighty (180) consecutive days (a \\\"Co-Tenancy Failure\\\"), Tenant shall have the right, upon thirty (30) days\\' prior written notice to Landlord, to elect either of the following remedies: (a) **Reduced Rent**: Tenant may pay Base Rent equal to seventy-five percent (75%) of the then-applicable Base Rent, plus Percentage Rent (without adjustment to the Breakpoint), until such time as occupancy of the Shopping Center is restored to seventy percent (70%) or above, at which point Base Rent shall revert to the full amount then in effect; or (b) **Termination**: Tenant may terminate this Lease upon sixty (60) days\\' prior written notice to Landlord, provided that such Co-Tenancy Failure is then continuing and has not been cured as of the date Tenant delivers such termination notice. If the occupancy of the Shopping Center is restored to seventy percent (70%) or above prior to the effective date of any termination notice, the termination notice shall be deemed withdrawn and of no further effect. The Co-Tenancy Failure must be continuous; if occupancy is restored to seventy percent (70%) or above before Tenant exercises either remedy, the right shall lapse until a new Co-Tenancy Failure occurs.\n\n**[ARTICLE 11 --- RENEWAL OPTIONS]{.underline}**\n\nTenant shall have two (2) options to renew this Lease, each for an additional term of five (5) years (each, a \\\"Renewal Term\\\"), upon the following terms and conditions: (a) Tenant shall deliver irrevocable written notice of exercise to Landlord not less than nine (9) months prior to the then-current Expiration Date (or the expiration of the first Renewal Term, as applicable); (b) the Base Rent during each Renewal Term shall be the then-prevailing fair market rent for comparable restaurant space in the Asheville, North Carolina metropolitan area, as mutually determined by Landlord and Tenant in good faith; provided, however, that in no event shall the Base Rent during any Renewal Term be less than the Base Rent payable during the final Lease Year of the immediately preceding Term or Renewal Term; (c) all other terms and conditions of this Lease shall remain in full force and effect during each Renewal Term unless otherwise mutually agreed in writing; and (d) Tenant shall not be in default under this Lease, and no Event of Default shall be continuing, both at the time Tenant delivers its exercise notice and at the commencement of the applicable Renewal Term. If the parties are unable to agree upon fair market rent within sixty (60) days after Tenant\\'s exercise notice, the determination shall be made by a single MAI-certified appraiser mutually selected by the parties, whose determination shall be binding.\n\n**[ARTICLE 12 --- SUBORDINATION, NON-DISTURBANCE, AND ATTORNMENT]{.underline}**\n\nThis Lease shall be subject and subordinate to any mortgage, deed of trust, or other security instrument now or hereafter placed upon the Shopping Center or any portion thereof, and to all renewals, modifications, consolidations, replacements, and extensions thereof. Notwithstanding the foregoing, Landlord shall use commercially reasonable efforts to obtain from each existing and future mortgagee or beneficiary a subordination, non-disturbance, and attornment agreement (\\\"SNDA\\\") in commercially reasonable form, which shall provide that, so long as Tenant is not in default under this Lease, Tenant\\'s possession and rights under this Lease shall not be disturbed by the mortgagee or any successor in a foreclosure proceeding. Tenant agrees to attorn to any successor landlord, whether by foreclosure, deed in lieu of foreclosure, or otherwise, and to recognize such successor as Landlord under this Lease.\n\n**[ARTICLE 13 --- INDEMNIFICATION AND LIABILITY]{.underline}**\n\nTenant shall indemnify, defend, and hold harmless Landlord and its partners, officers, directors, employees, agents, and affiliates from and against all claims, actions, damages, losses, liabilities, costs, and expenses (including reasonable attorneys\\' fees) arising out of or in connection with (a) Tenant\\'s use and occupancy of the Premises, (b) any act or omission of Tenant or its employees, agents, contractors, or invitees, (c) any breach by Tenant of its obligations under this Lease, or (d) any injury to or death of any person or damage to any property occurring in, on, or about the Premises. Landlord shall indemnify, defend, and hold harmless Tenant from and against all claims, actions, damages, losses, liabilities, costs, and expenses (including reasonable attorneys\\' fees) arising out of or in connection with Landlord\\'s negligence in maintaining the common areas and structural elements of the Shopping Center. Notwithstanding anything to the contrary herein, neither party shall be liable to the other for any consequential, incidental, indirect, special, or punitive damages arising under or in connection with this Lease.\n\n**[ARTICLE 14 --- DEFAULT AND REMEDIES]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 14.1 --- Events of Default**\n\nEach of the following shall constitute an \\\"Event of Default\\\" under this Lease: (a) Tenant\\'s failure to pay Base Rent, Percentage Rent, Additional Rent, or any other monetary obligation due hereunder within ten (10) days after written notice from Landlord that such payment is past due; (b) Tenant\\'s failure to perform or observe any non-monetary covenant, condition, or obligation of this Lease within thirty (30) days after written notice from Landlord specifying the nature of such failure, provided that if such failure is of a nature that cannot reasonably be cured within thirty (30) days, Tenant shall not be in default if Tenant commences to cure within such thirty (30) day period and thereafter diligently prosecutes such cure to completion; (c) Tenant\\'s filing of a petition in bankruptcy, making an assignment for the benefit of creditors, or the appointment of a receiver or trustee for all or substantially all of Tenant\\'s assets; or (d) Tenant\\'s abandonment of the Premises for more than thirty (30) consecutive days.\n\n**Section 14.2 --- Landlord\\'s Remedies**\n\nUpon the occurrence and during the continuance of an Event of Default, Landlord may, at its option, exercise any one or more of the following remedies: (a) terminate this Lease by written notice to Tenant and recover from Tenant all damages incurred by Landlord, including unamortized tenant improvement allowances, the present value (discounted at six percent (6%) per annum) of the excess of rent due for the remainder of the Term over the fair market rental value of the Premises for such period, and reasonable attorneys\\' fees; (b) re-enter the Premises and relet all or any portion thereof on such terms and conditions as Landlord deems advisable, with Tenant remaining liable for the deficiency; or (c) pursue any other remedy available at law or in equity. All remedies of Landlord are cumulative and non-exclusive, and the exercise of any remedy shall not preclude the exercise of any other remedy.\n\n**[ARTICLE 15 --- DAMAGE AND DESTRUCTION; CONDEMNATION]{.underline}**\n\nIn the event the Premises are damaged by fire, casualty, or other cause, Landlord shall promptly notify Tenant of the estimated time to repair. If the damage renders more than fifty percent (50%) of the RSF of the Premises untenantable, either party may terminate this Lease upon sixty (60) days\\' written notice to the other, delivered within ninety (90) days of the date of damage. If the damage renders fifty percent (50%) or less of the RSF untenantable, Landlord shall repair the Premises to substantially its condition prior to the casualty (excluding Tenant\\'s trade fixtures, equipment, and personal property) within one hundred eighty (180) days from the date of damage. Base Rent and Additional Rent shall be equitably abated during the period of repair in proportion to the area rendered untenantable. In the event of a taking of all or substantially all of the Premises by eminent domain or condemnation, this Lease shall terminate as of the date of such taking. In the event of a partial taking that does not render the Premises unsuitable for Tenant\\'s use, the Lease shall continue and Base Rent shall be equitably reduced in proportion to the area taken. All condemnation awards shall belong to Landlord, except that Tenant shall be entitled to make a separate claim for the value of Tenant\\'s trade fixtures and relocation expenses.\n\n**[ARTICLE 16 --- SURRENDER AND RESTORATION]{.underline}**\n\nUpon the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease, Tenant shall peaceably surrender and deliver the Premises to Landlord in good condition, ordinary wear and tear excepted, and shall remove all of Tenant\\'s trade fixtures, equipment, signage, and personal property. Tenant shall repair any damage to the Premises caused by such removal. Any property not removed by Tenant within ten (10) days following the expiration or termination date shall be deemed abandoned and may be retained, disposed of, or removed by Landlord at Tenant\\'s expense.\n\n**[ARTICLE 17 --- NOTICES]{.underline}**\n\nAll notices, demands, consents, approvals, and other communications required or permitted under this Lease shall be in writing and shall be deemed duly given when (a) delivered personally, (b) sent by a nationally recognized overnight courier service with tracking capability, or (c) sent by certified United States mail, return receipt requested, postage prepaid, addressed as follows:\n\n**If to Landlord:**\n\n> Thornfield Realty Holdings LP 500 South Tryon Street, Suite 2200 Charlotte, North Carolina 28202\n>\n> Attention: Renata Thornfield-Kiess, President\n>\n> With a copy to: Karen Osgood, Property Manager Thornfield Realty Holdings LP 500 South Tryon Street, Suite 2200 Charlotte, North Carolina 28202\n\n**If to Tenant:**\n\n> The Pint & Platter Restaurant Group LLC 92 Haywood Street, Unit 4 Asheville, North Carolina 28801\n>\n> Attention: Lance Whitford\n\nEither party may change its notice address by written notice to the other party in accordance with this Article 17. Notice shall be deemed received on the date of personal delivery, the next business day following deposit with an overnight courier, or three (3) business days after deposit in the United States mail as provided above.\n\n**[ARTICLE 18 --- MISCELLANEOUS]{.underline}**\n\n**Section 18.1 --- Entire Agreement**\n\nThis Lease, together with all Exhibits attached hereto (Exhibits A, B, C, and D), constitutes the entire agreement between Landlord and Tenant with respect to the subject matter hereof, and supersedes all prior and contemporaneous negotiations, representations, warranties, understandings, and agreements, whether written or oral, between the parties relating to the Premises and the transactions contemplated hereby.\n\n**Section 18.2 --- Amendments**\n\nThis Lease may not be modified, amended, or supplemented except by a written instrument executed by both Landlord and Tenant.\n\n**Section 18.3 --- Governing Law; Venue**\n\nThis Lease shall be governed by and construed in accordance with the laws of the State of North Carolina, without regard to its conflict-of-laws principles. Any action or proceeding arising out of or related to this Lease shall be brought exclusively in the state or federal courts located in Buncombe County, North Carolina, and the parties hereby consent to the exclusive jurisdiction and venue of such courts.\n\n**Section 18.4 --- Severability**\n\nIf any term, provision, covenant, or condition of this Lease is held to be invalid, void, or unenforceable by a court of competent jurisdiction, the remaining provisions shall continue in full force and effect and shall in no way be affected, impaired, or invalidated thereby.\n\n**Section 18.5 --- Waiver**\n\nThe failure of either party to insist upon strict performance of any provision of this Lease, or the failure to exercise any right or remedy upon a breach thereof, shall not constitute a waiver of such provision, right, or remedy with respect to any subsequent breach, nor shall any single or partial exercise of any right or remedy preclude any other or further exercise thereof.\n\n**Section 18.6 --- Successors and Assigns**\n\nThis Lease shall be binding upon and shall inure to the benefit of the parties hereto and their respective heirs, executors, administrators, legal representatives, successors, and permitted assigns.\n\n**Section 18.7 --- Brokers**\n\nLandlord represents and warrants that Ridgeview Realty Brokers Inc. acted as Landlord\\'s broker in connection with this Lease, and Landlord shall be solely responsible for the payment of any commission due to such broker pursuant to a separate agreement. Tenant represents and warrants that no broker, finder, or agent acted on behalf of Tenant in connection with this Lease. Each party agrees to indemnify, defend, and hold harmless the other party from and against any claims for brokerage commissions or finder\\'s fees arising from the indemnifying party\\'s breach of the foregoing representations.\n\n**Section 18.8 --- Counterparts**\n\nThis Lease may be executed in any number of counterparts, each of which shall be deemed an original and all of which together shall constitute one and the same instrument. Execution and delivery by facsimile or electronic transmission (including PDF) shall be deemed effective for all purposes.\n\n**Section 18.9 --- Recording**\n\nNeither party shall record this Lease or any memorandum thereof in any public records without the prior written consent of the other party; provided, however, that upon the mutual written agreement of both Landlord and Tenant, the parties may execute and record a short-form memorandum of this Lease in the Buncombe County Register of Deeds.\n\n**IN WITNESS WHEREOF**, the parties hereto have executed this Lease Agreement as of the date first above written.\n\n**LANDLORD:**\n\nTHORNFIELD REALTY HOLDINGS LP, a North Carolina limited partnership\n\nBy: Thornfield Management Corp.,\n\na North Carolina corporation, its General Partner\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: Renata Thornfield-Kiess\n\nTitle: President\n\nDate: May 15, 2020\n\n**TENANT:**\n\nTHE PINT & PLATTER RESTAURANT GROUP LLC, a North Carolina limited liability company\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: Lance Whitford\n\nTitle: Sole Member and Manager\n\nDate: May 15, 2020\n\n**[EXHIBIT A]{.underline}**\n\n**[FLOOR PLAN / SITE PLAN]{.underline}**\n\nThe following is a depiction of the Premises, designated as Unit 4, Haywood Village Shops, 92 Haywood Street, Asheville, North Carolina 28801. The Premises consists of approximately 4,400 rentable square feet of ground-floor retail space, including the adjacent patio area, as outlined and cross-hatched on the site plan below.\n\n*\\[See attached floor plan depicting Unit 4, including interior dining area, kitchen and back-of-house area, restrooms, storage, and patio area, totaling approximately 4,400 RSF. The Premises are shown in relation to the overall site plan of Haywood Village Shops, including common areas, parking, and adjacent tenant spaces.\\]*\n\nThis Exhibit A is attached to and made a part of that certain Lease Agreement dated May 15, 2020, between Thornfield Realty Holdings LP, as Landlord, and The Pint & Platter Restaurant Group LLC, as Tenant.\n\n**[EXHIBIT B]{.underline}**\n\n**[RENT SCHEDULE]{.underline}**\n\nThe following Rent Schedule sets forth the Base Rent and Percentage Rent Breakpoint applicable to the Lease between Thornfield Realty Holdings LP (\\\"Landlord\\\") and The Pint & Platter Restaurant Group LLC (\\\"Tenant\\\") for Unit 4, Haywood Village Shops.\n\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Lease Year**   **Period**                         **Base Rent (\\$/RSF/Year)**   **Annual Base Rent**   **Monthly Base Rent**   **Percentage Rent Breakpoint**\n  ---------------- ---------------------------------- ----------------------------- ---------------------- ----------------------- --------------------------------\n  1                Oct 1, 2020 -- Sep 30, 2021        \\$18.000                      \\$79,200.00            \\$6,600.00              \\$1,350,000\n\n  2                Oct 1, 2021 -- Sep 30, 2022        \\$18.684                      \\$82,209.60            \\$6,850.80              \\$1,350,000\n\n  3                Oct 1, 2022 -- Sep 30, 2023        \\$19.431                      \\$85,496.40            \\$7,124.70              \\$1,350,000\n\n  4                Oct 1, 2023 -- Sep 30, 2024        \\$19.917                      \\$87,634.80            \\$7,302.90              \\$1,350,000\n\n  5                Oct 1, 2024 -- Sep 30, 2025        \\$20.315                      \\$89,386.00            \\$7,448.83              \\$1,350,000\n\n  6 -- 10          Per CPI Adjustment (Section 3.2)   TBD                           TBD                    TBD                     \\$1,350,000\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Notes:**\n\n1\\. Base Rent for Lease Years 6 through 10 shall be determined annually in accordance with Section 3.2 of the Lease, applying the CPI-U adjustment with a minimum (floor) of 2% and a maximum (cap) of 4% per annum.\n\n2\\. Percentage Rent Rate: 6.0% of Gross Sales in excess of the Breakpoint set forth above.\n\n3\\. Percentage Rent is payable commencing in Lease Year 2 in accordance with Section 3.3 of the Lease.\n\n4\\. All amounts are based on a Premises size of 4,400 rentable square feet.\n\nThis Exhibit B is attached to and made a part of that certain Lease Agreement dated May 15, 2020, between Thornfield Realty Holdings LP, as Landlord, and The Pint & Platter Restaurant Group LLC, as Tenant.\n\n**[EXHIBIT C]{.underline}**\n\n**[RULES AND REGULATIONS]{.underline}**\n\n**[HAYWOOD VILLAGE SHOPS]{.underline}**\n\nThe following Rules and Regulations shall apply to all tenants, their employees, agents, contractors, and invitees at Haywood Village Shops. These Rules and Regulations are supplemental to the Lease and are incorporated therein by reference.\n\n1\\. **Hours of Operation.** Tenant shall maintain regular business hours consistent with the Shopping Center\\'s customary operating hours, which are currently 7:00 a.m. to 11:00 p.m. daily. Tenant may request extended hours for special events by submitting a written request to Landlord not less than seven (7) days in advance.\n\n2\\. **Trash and Dumpster Requirements.** All refuse, garbage, and waste shall be deposited in the designated dumpster enclosures located in the service area behind the Shopping Center. Tenant shall not permit the accumulation of trash, debris, or refuse outside the Premises or in any common area. Tenant shall comply with all recycling and waste separation requirements imposed by Landlord or applicable governmental authorities.\n\n3\\. **Grease Trap Maintenance.** Tenant shall maintain, clean, and service all grease traps, grease interceptors, and related equipment serving the Premises at least quarterly, or more frequently as required by applicable health codes, and shall maintain records of all such service available for inspection by Landlord.\n\n4\\. **Signage.** All exterior signage, awnings, and displays must be approved in writing by Landlord prior to installation and must conform to the signage standards established for Haywood Village Shops. No signage shall be affixed to windows, doors, or the exterior of the building without prior written approval.\n\n5\\. **Exterior Appearance.** Tenant shall maintain the storefront, windows, and patio area in a clean, attractive, and well-maintained condition at all times. Seasonal decorations and promotional displays visible from the exterior must be tasteful and consistent with the character of the Shopping Center.\n\n6\\. **Loading and Deliveries.** All deliveries, loading, and unloading shall be conducted via the designated service area at the rear of the building and shall be completed between the hours of 6:00 a.m. and 10:00 a.m. daily, unless otherwise approved by Landlord. Delivery vehicles shall not park in customer parking areas.\n\n7\\. **Noise and Nuisance.** Tenant shall not conduct any activity that constitutes a nuisance or that unreasonably disturbs other tenants or their customers. All amplified music and entertainment shall comply with applicable Asheville noise ordinances and shall not be audible beyond the boundaries of the Premises after 10:00 p.m.\n\n8\\. **Patio Area.** The patio area shall be maintained in a clean and orderly condition. Furniture, planters, and fixtures on the patio shall not obstruct pedestrian pathways or fire egress routes. Patio umbrellas and heaters must be secured and maintained in good condition.\n\n9\\. **Parking.** Tenant, its employees, and its agents shall park only in areas designated by Landlord for employee parking, which are located in the rear portion of the parking lot. Customer parking areas nearest to the storefronts shall be reserved for Shopping Center patrons.\n\n10\\. **Hazardous Materials.** Tenant shall not bring onto the Premises or the Shopping Center any hazardous materials or substances except those customarily used in the operation of a restaurant (such as cleaning supplies and cooking fuels), and all such materials shall be stored, used, and disposed of in compliance with applicable environmental laws and regulations.\n\nThis Exhibit C is attached to and made a part of that certain Lease Agreement dated May 15, 2020, between Thornfield Realty Holdings LP, as Landlord, and The Pint & Platter Restaurant Group LLC, as Tenant.\n\n**[EXHIBIT D]{.underline}**\n\n**[PERSONAL GUARANTY OF LEASE]{.underline}**\n\nDate: May 15, 2020\n\n**PERSONAL GUARANTY OF LEASE**\n\nThis Personal Guaranty of Lease (this \\\"Guaranty\\\") is made as of the 15th day of May, 2020, by Lance Whitford, an individual residing at 47 Montford Avenue, Asheville, North Carolina 28801 (\\\"Guarantor\\\"), in favor of Thornfield Realty Holdings LP, a North Carolina limited partnership (\\\"Landlord\\\").\n\n**RECITALS**\n\n**WHEREAS**, Guarantor is the sole member and manager of The Pint & Platter Restaurant Group LLC, a North Carolina limited liability company (\\\"Tenant\\\"); and\n\n**WHEREAS**, Tenant has entered into that certain Lease Agreement dated May 15, 2020 (the \\\"Lease\\\") with Landlord for the premises designated as Unit 4, Haywood Village Shops, 92 Haywood Street, Asheville, North Carolina 28801; and\n\n**WHEREAS**, as a material inducement for Landlord to enter into the Lease with Tenant, Guarantor has agreed to personally guarantee the payment and performance of Tenant\\'s obligations under the Lease upon the terms and conditions set forth herein;\n\n**NOW, THEREFORE**, in consideration of Landlord\\'s execution and delivery of the Lease, and for other good and valuable consideration, the receipt and sufficiency of which are hereby acknowledged, Guarantor agrees as follows:\n\n**Section 1 --- Guaranty.** Guarantor hereby unconditionally and irrevocably guarantees to Landlord the full, faithful, and timely payment and performance of all of Tenant\\'s obligations under the Lease, including but not limited to the payment of Base Rent, Percentage Rent, Additional Rent, and all other monetary obligations, as well as the performance of all non-monetary obligations of Tenant under the Lease. This Guaranty is a guaranty of payment and performance and not merely of collection.\n\n**Section 2 --- Guaranty Term and Burn-Off.** The obligations of Guarantor under this Guaranty shall expire and be of no further force or effect upon the earliest to occur of the following:\n\n> \\(a\\) September 30, 2025 (i.e., the last day of Lease Year 5 under the Lease); or\n>\n> \\(b\\) the date upon which Tenant achieves trailing-twelve-month Gross Sales (as defined in the Lease) of Two Million Dollars (\\$2,000,000.00) or more, as evidenced by a certified sales report delivered to Landlord in accordance with Section 3.4 of the Lease.\n\nUpon the occurrence of either (a) or (b) above, Landlord shall, upon Guarantor\\'s written request, execute and deliver to Guarantor a written release of this Guaranty in recordable form within thirty (30) days following such request. Notwithstanding the foregoing, the expiration of this Guaranty shall not release Guarantor from liability for any obligations of Tenant that accrued prior to the expiration date and remain unpaid or unperformed as of such date.\n\n**Section 3 --- Waivers.** Guarantor hereby waives, to the fullest extent permitted by applicable law: (a) notice of acceptance of this Guaranty; (b) presentment, demand, and protest with respect to any obligation of Tenant guaranteed hereby; (c) notice of any modification, amendment, extension, or renewal of the Lease, whether or not Guarantor consents thereto; (d) any defense arising from the discharge or release of Tenant in any bankruptcy, insolvency, or similar proceeding; (e) any right to require Landlord to proceed first against Tenant, to exhaust any security held by Landlord, or to pursue any other remedy before enforcing this Guaranty against Guarantor; and (f) any right to assert any statute of limitations as a defense to any obligation hereunder.\n\n**Section 4 --- Remedies.** Landlord may proceed directly against Guarantor under this Guaranty without first pursuing any remedy against Tenant, any other guarantor, or any collateral. Guarantor\\'s liability hereunder is primary, direct, and immediate, and not secondary or contingent. Landlord\\'s rights under this Guaranty are cumulative and not exclusive of any other rights or remedies that Landlord may have against Tenant or Guarantor at law or in equity.\n\n**Section 5 --- Miscellaneous.** This Guaranty shall be governed by and construed in accordance with the laws of the State of North Carolina. Any action or proceeding arising out of or related to this Guaranty shall be brought exclusively in the state or federal courts located in Buncombe County, North Carolina. This Guaranty shall be binding upon Guarantor and Guarantor\\'s heirs, executors, administrators, personal representatives, successors, and assigns. This Guaranty may not be modified, amended, or terminated except by a written instrument signed by both Landlord and Guarantor. This Guaranty may be executed in counterparts, each of which shall be deemed an original. If any provision of this Guaranty is held to be invalid or unenforceable, the remaining provisions shall remain in full force and effect.\n\n**IN WITNESS WHEREOF**, Guarantor has executed this Personal Guaranty of Lease as of the date first above written.\n\n**GUARANTOR:**\n\n\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\n\nLance Whitford, individually\n\nDate: May 15, 2020\n\n**ACCEPTED AND AGREED:**\n\n**LANDLORD:**\n\nTHORNFIELD REALTY HOLDINGS LP, a North Carolina limited partnership\n\nBy: Thornfield Management Corp.,\n\na North Carolina corporation, its General Partner\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: Renata Thornfield-Kiess\n\nTitle: President\n\nDate: May 15, 2020\n"}
