{"turn": 11, "role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "call_00_TSHbV2F8YoCniHyAz4IH2118", "tool_name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/workspace/documents/gsa-lease.docx\"}", "result": "**GENERAL SERVICES ADMINISTRATION** **PUBLIC BUILDINGS SERVICE**\n\n**LEASE FOR REAL PROPERTY**\n\n**GSA FORM L201D**\n\n**LEASE NO. GS-04B-15678**\n\nThis Lease is entered into as of September 30, 2020, by and between:\n\n**LESSOR:** Thornfield Realty Holdings LP, a North Carolina limited partnership, acting through its General Partner, Thornfield Management Corp., a North Carolina corporation, 500 South Tryon Street, Suite 2200, Charlotte, NC 28202\n\nand\n\n**LESSEE / TENANT / GOVERNMENT:** The United States of America, acting by and through the General Services Administration, Region 4 (Southeast Sunbelt) Contracting Officer: James Hua\n\n**PROPERTY:** Concord Office Tower 300 Concord Plaza Drive, 5th Floor Atlanta, GA 30309\n\n**DATE OF EXECUTION:** September 30, 2020\n\n**[SECTION 1 --- LEASE INFORMATION SUMMARY]{.underline}**\n\nThe following table sets forth the principal terms and data of this Lease. In the event of any conflict between the information contained in this Section 1 and the detailed provisions of this Lease, the detailed provisions shall control.\n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Item**                                  **Description**\n  ----------------------------------------- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Lease Number**                          GS-04B-15678\n\n  **Lessor**                                Thornfield Realty Holdings LP, 500 South Tryon Street, Suite 2200, Charlotte, NC 28202\n\n  **Lessor\\'s Authorized Representative**   Renata Thornfield-Kiess, President, Thornfield Management Corp. (General Partner)\n\n  **Lessor\\'s Property Manager**            Karen Osgood, Property Manager, Thornfield Realty Holdings LP\n\n  **Government\\'s Contracting Officer**     James Hua, Contracting Officer, General Services Administration, Region 4 (Southeast Sunbelt)\n\n  **Building Name and Address**             Concord Office Tower, 300 Concord Plaza Drive, Atlanta, GA 30309\n\n  **Space Description**                     Entire 5th Floor\n\n  **Rentable Square Feet (RSF)**            15,000 RSF\n\n  **Lease Execution Date**                  September 30, 2020\n\n  **Lease Occupancy Date**                  January 1, 2021\n\n  **Firm Term**                             Five (5) years, commencing January 1, 2021, and expiring December 31, 2025\n\n  **Total Lease Term**                      Ten (10) years, commencing January 1, 2021, and expiring December 31, 2030\n\n  **Shell Rent (Firm Term)**                \\$31.50/RSF/year (\\$472,500.00/year; \\$39,375.00/month)\n\n  **Escalation After Firm Term**            2.5% per annum, compounding annually, applied to shell rent for each year following the Firm Term\n\n  **Government Termination Right**          The Government may terminate this Lease at any time after the Firm Term Expiration Date (December 31, 2025) upon not less than one hundred twenty (120) calendar days\\' prior written notice to Lessor. No termination fee or penalty shall be payable by the Government.\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe Firm Term of this Lease expires on December 31, 2025. Following the Firm Term Expiration Date, the Lease continues for an additional five (5) years of Soft Term through December 31, 2030, during which the Government possesses the unilateral right to terminate upon one hundred twenty (120) calendar days\\' prior written notice to the Lessor. No fee, penalty, or other consideration is due to the Lessor upon the Government\\'s exercise of its termination right during the Soft Term. The Soft Term rental income set forth in Exhibit B is therefore contingent upon the Government\\'s continued occupancy and is not guaranteed beyond the Firm Term Expiration Date.\n\n**[SECTION 2 --- PREMISES]{.underline}**\n\nThe Lessor hereby leases to the Government, and the Government hereby leases from the Lessor, the following described space (the \\\"Premises\\\"): the entire 5th floor, consisting of approximately fifteen thousand (15,000) rentable square feet (\\\"RSF\\\"), in the building commonly known as Concord Office Tower, located at 300 Concord Plaza Drive, Atlanta, GA 30309 (the \\\"Building\\\").\n\nThe Premises shall be measured in accordance with the American National Standard Method for Measuring Floor Area in Office Buildings (ANSI/BOMA Z65.1--2017), as may be amended from time to time. In the event of any dispute regarding the measurement of the Premises, the Government\\'s Contracting Officer shall make a final determination in accordance with the applicable ANSI/BOMA standard, and such determination shall be binding upon the parties.\n\nA floor plan of the Premises is attached hereto as **Exhibit A** and is incorporated herein by reference. Exhibit A depicts the general configuration and boundaries of the Premises as of the Lease Execution Date.\n\nThe Government shall have non-exclusive access to all common areas of the Building, including but not limited to lobbies, corridors, elevators, stairwells, restrooms, loading docks, and parking areas, together with all rights of ingress and egress appurtenant to the Premises. The Government\\'s use of common areas shall be subject to reasonable rules and regulations established by the Lessor, provided that such rules and regulations do not materially interfere with the Government\\'s use and occupancy of the Premises or with the Government\\'s security requirements.\n\n**[SECTION 3 --- TERM OF LEASE]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 3.1 --- Firm Term]{.underline}**\n\nThe firm term of this Lease shall be five (5) years, commencing on January 1, 2021 (the \\\"Occupancy Date\\\"), and expiring on December 31, 2025 (the \\\"Firm Term Expiration Date\\\"). During the Firm Term, the Government\\'s obligation to pay rent and occupy the Premises is firm and non-cancelable, subject to the availability of appropriated funds as provided in Section 4.3. The Government may not terminate this Lease during the Firm Term except in the case of casualty or condemnation as provided in Section 13 or default by the Lessor as provided in Section 14.\n\n**[Section 3.2 --- Soft Term and Government Termination Right]{.underline}**\n\nFollowing the Firm Term Expiration Date, this Lease shall continue for an additional five (5) years (the \\\"Soft Term\\\"), commencing on January 1, 2026, and expiring on December 31, 2030, unless earlier terminated by the Government as provided herein. The Firm Term and the Soft Term, taken together, constitute the total Lease term of ten (10) years.\n\nThe Government may terminate this Lease at any time during the Soft Term by providing the Lessor with not less than one hundred twenty (120) calendar days\\' prior written notice of its intent to vacate the Premises. Such notice shall be delivered to the Lessor at the address set forth in Section 1, or such other address as the Lessor may designate in writing in accordance with Section 18. The termination notice shall specify the effective date of termination, which date shall be not earlier than one hundred twenty (120) calendar days following the date of such notice.\n\nUpon the effective date of such termination, the Government shall vacate the Premises (subject to the Government\\'s rights under Section 7.2 regarding removal of Government Improvements and Section 3.3 regarding holdover), and the Government\\'s obligation to pay rent shall cease as of the effective date of termination.\n\nNo termination fee, penalty, liquidated damages, or other consideration of any kind shall be payable by the Government to the Lessor in connection with the Government\\'s exercise of its termination right under this Section 3.2. The Government\\'s right to terminate during the Soft Term is absolute and unilateral; no cause, justification, or explanation is required.\n\n**[Section 3.3 --- Holding Over]{.underline}**\n\nIf the Government fails to vacate the Premises upon the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease, the Government shall be deemed a tenant at sufferance. During any holdover period, the Government shall continue to pay rent at the rate in effect immediately prior to the expiration or termination date, payable monthly in arrears, until such time as the Government vacates the Premises and delivers possession to the Lessor. The Lessor hereby waives any claim for additional holdover rent, premium rent, or damages arising from the Government\\'s holdover occupancy of the Premises.\n\n**[SECTION 4 --- RENT]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 4.1 --- Shell Rent During Firm Term]{.underline}**\n\nThe Government shall pay the Lessor annual shell rent of Thirty-One Dollars and Fifty Cents (\\$31.50) per RSF during the Firm Term, calculated as follows:\n\n> Annual Rent: \\$31.50 × 15,000 RSF = \\$472,500.00\n>\n> Monthly Rent: \\$472,500.00 ÷ 12 = \\$39,375.00\n\nShell rent is payable monthly in arrears. Payments shall be made by electronic funds transfer (\\\"EFT\\\") in accordance with GSA payment procedures and applicable provisions of the Prompt Payment Act (31 U.S.C. §§ 3901--3907). Rent for any partial month at the commencement or expiration of the Lease shall be prorated on a per diem basis.\n\nShell rent is fixed for the entirety of the Firm Term (Lease Years 1 through 5: January 1, 2021, through December 31, 2025) and shall not be subject to adjustment, escalation, or modification during the Firm Term, except as otherwise provided in Section 5 (Operating Expenses and Taxes) with respect to operating expense escalations above the Base Year.\n\nThe following rent schedule shall apply during the Firm Term:\n\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Lease Year**   **Period**                    **Shell Rent (\\$/RSF/Year)**   **Annual Rent**   **Monthly Rent**\n  ---------------- ----------------------------- ------------------------------ ----------------- ------------------\n  Year 1           Jan 1, 2021 -- Dec 31, 2021   \\$31.50                        \\$472,500.00      \\$39,375.00\n\n  Year 2           Jan 1, 2022 -- Dec 31, 2022   \\$31.50                        \\$472,500.00      \\$39,375.00\n\n  Year 3           Jan 1, 2023 -- Dec 31, 2023   \\$31.50                        \\$472,500.00      \\$39,375.00\n\n  Year 4           Jan 1, 2024 -- Dec 31, 2024   \\$31.50                        \\$472,500.00      \\$39,375.00\n\n  Year 5           Jan 1, 2025 -- Dec 31, 2025   \\$31.50                        \\$472,500.00      \\$39,375.00\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**[Section 4.2 --- Rent During Soft Term (Escalation)]{.underline}**\n\nDuring the Soft Term, shell rent shall escalate at a rate of two and one-half percent (2.5%) per annum, compounding annually, applied to the Firm Term shell rent rate of \\$31.50 per RSF per year. The escalation formula for each Soft Term year shall be calculated as follows:\n\n> Soft Term Shell Rent for Year *n* = \\$31.50 × (1.025)\\^(*n* − 5)\n>\n> where *n* represents the applicable Lease Year (Year 6 through Year 10).\n\nThe following rent schedule shall apply during the Soft Term:\n\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Lease Year**   **Period**                    **Shell Rent (\\$/RSF/Year)**   **Annual Rent**   **Monthly Rent**\n  ---------------- ----------------------------- ------------------------------ ----------------- ------------------\n  Year 6           Jan 1, 2026 -- Dec 31, 2026   \\$32.29                        \\$484,350.00      \\$40,362.50\n\n  Year 7           Jan 1, 2027 -- Dec 31, 2027   \\$33.10                        \\$496,500.00      \\$41,375.00\n\n  Year 8           Jan 1, 2028 -- Dec 31, 2028   \\$33.93                        \\$508,950.00      \\$42,412.50\n\n  Year 9           Jan 1, 2029 -- Dec 31, 2029   \\$34.78                        \\$521,700.00      \\$43,475.00\n\n  Year 10          Jan 1, 2030 -- Dec 31, 2030   \\$35.64                        \\$534,600.00      \\$44,550.00\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nShell rent rates in the foregoing table have been rounded to the nearest cent per RSF. Annual and monthly rent figures have been rounded to the nearest dollar and cent, respectively.\n\nSoft Term rent is payable only if and to the extent the Government does not exercise its termination right under Section 3.2 of this Lease. In the event the Government terminates this Lease effective as of a date other than December 31 of a given year, rent for the partial year in which such termination occurs shall be prorated on a per diem basis.\n\nThe complete rent schedule for the full ten (10) year Lease term, including both the Firm Term and the Soft Term, is set forth in **Exhibit B**, which is attached hereto and incorporated herein by reference.\n\n**[Section 4.3 --- Availability of Appropriations]{.underline}**\n\nThe Government\\'s obligation to make rental payments under this Lease is subject to the availability of appropriated funds. Nothing in this Lease shall be interpreted to require obligations or payments in violation of the Anti-Deficiency Act (31 U.S.C. § 1341). In the event funds are not appropriated or otherwise available for the continued performance of this Lease, the Government shall provide the Lessor with written notice of such non-availability as soon as reasonably practicable.\n\n**[SECTION 5 --- OPERATING EXPENSES AND TAXES]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 5.1 --- Base Year Operating Expenses]{.underline}**\n\nThe shell rent set forth in Section 4 includes the Lessor\\'s operating expenses for the Building for the calendar year 2021 (the \\\"Base Year\\\"). Operating expenses for purposes of this Lease include, without limitation: common area maintenance, utilities (including electricity, natural gas, water, and sewer), janitorial services, landscaping, pest control, property management fees, insurance premiums (including property and general liability insurance), and real estate taxes and assessments attributable to the Building and associated land. The Lessor shall provide the Government with a certified statement of actual operating expenses for the Base Year as set forth in Exhibit C.\n\n**[Section 5.2 --- Operating Expense Escalations]{.underline}**\n\nFor each calendar year following the Base Year, the Government shall pay its pro rata share of increases in actual operating expenses above the Base Year amount. The Government\\'s pro rata share shall be calculated as follows:\n\n> Government\\'s Pro Rata Share = (15,000 RSF ÷ Total Building RSF) × (Actual Operating Expenses for Current Year − Base Year Operating Expenses)\n\nOperating expense escalation charges shall be payable in addition to the shell rent set forth in Section 4. The Lessor shall provide the Government with an annual statement of actual operating expenses within ninety (90) days following the end of each calendar year. Such statement shall set forth in reasonable detail the components of operating expenses, including an itemization of real estate taxes, insurance, utilities, janitorial, management fees, and other material categories. The Government shall pay any underpayment within thirty (30) days of receipt of such statement, and the Lessor shall credit any overpayment against the next succeeding monthly rental payment.\n\nThe Government reserves the right to audit the Lessor\\'s books, records, and accounts relating to operating expenses at any time during the Lease term and for three (3) years following the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease. The Lessor shall make such records available for inspection at a mutually agreeable location within fifteen (15) business days of receiving the Government\\'s written request for an audit.\n\n**[Section 5.3 --- Real Estate Taxes]{.underline}**\n\nReal estate taxes, including ad valorem taxes, special assessments, and any other taxes or charges levied by any governmental authority upon the Building and associated land, are included as a component of operating expenses under this Section. To the extent real estate taxes increase above the Base Year amount, the Government shall pay its pro rata share of such increase as part of the operating expense escalation described in Section 5.2. The Lessor shall use commercially reasonable efforts to contest any real estate tax assessment that the Lessor believes to be excessive or erroneous.\n\n**[SECTION 6 --- SERVICES AND UTILITIES]{.underline}**\n\nThe Lessor shall provide the following services and utilities as part of the shell rent during normal business hours (7:00 AM to 6:00 PM, Monday through Friday, excluding Federal holidays as designated by the Office of Personnel Management):\n\n> \\(a\\) Heating, ventilation, and air conditioning (\\\"HVAC\\\") sufficient to maintain comfortable temperatures and humidity levels within the Premises, consistent with ASHRAE Standard 55 and applicable GSA design standards;\n>\n> \\(b\\) Electrical service to the Premises, including 120/208V, 3-phase power, in quantities sufficient for the Government\\'s office operations, including computer and telecommunications equipment;\n>\n> \\(c\\) Domestic water and sewer service;\n>\n> \\(d\\) Janitorial services, including nightly cleaning of the Premises five (5) days per week (Monday through Friday), including vacuuming, dusting, trash removal, and restroom sanitation;\n>\n> \\(e\\) Trash removal and recycling services;\n>\n> \\(f\\) Passenger and freight elevator service;\n>\n> \\(g\\) Lobby and common area maintenance, including cleaning, lighting, and general upkeep; and\n>\n> \\(h\\) Snow and ice removal from Building entrances, sidewalks, and parking areas, as applicable.\n\nAfter-hours HVAC service shall be provided at the Lessor\\'s then-prevailing rates upon reasonable advance notice from the Government\\'s authorized representative. The Lessor shall provide the Government\\'s Contracting Officer with a schedule of after-hours HVAC rates at the commencement of each calendar year.\n\nThe Lessor shall maintain the Building and all common areas in first-class condition, consistent with the standards of comparable Class A office buildings in the Atlanta, Georgia metropolitan area. The Lessor shall promptly address any deficiency in services or utilities upon receipt of written notice from the Government\\'s Contracting Officer or the Government\\'s authorized representative on-site.\n\n**[SECTION 7 --- TENANT IMPROVEMENTS]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 7.1 --- Government-Funded Improvements]{.underline}**\n\nAll tenant improvements, alterations, and installations made to the Premises by or on behalf of the Government (collectively, \\\"Government Improvements\\\") shall be funded by the Government at the Government\\'s sole cost and expense. The Lessor shall not be required to provide any tenant improvement allowance, contribution, or reimbursement in connection with the Government\\'s build-out of the Premises.\n\nGovernment Improvements include, without limitation, interior build-out, partitioning, wall finishes, flooring, ceiling modifications, cabling, telecommunications infrastructure, information technology systems, security systems (including access control, intrusion detection, and surveillance systems), and all fixtures, furnishings, and equipment installed by the Government.\n\nThe Lessor acknowledges that certain Government Improvements may include specialized security infrastructure, including improvements necessary to create and maintain a Sensitive Compartmented Information Facility (\\\"SCIF\\\") or SCIF-capable space within the Premises. The specific nature, design, configuration, and location of any SCIF improvements are classified and are not detailed in this Lease or any exhibit or attachment hereto. The Lessor agrees not to access, inspect, photograph, or otherwise interfere with any SCIF space or SCIF improvements without prior written authorization from the Government\\'s Contracting Officer or the Government\\'s designated Facility Security Officer. Lessor further acknowledges that it has no right to review or approve the plans, specifications, or construction methods for any SCIF improvements.\n\n**[Section 7.2 --- Ownership of Government Improvements]{.underline}**\n\n**All Government Improvements shall remain the property of the Government at all times during and after the term of this Lease.** Government Improvements shall not be deemed fixtures or part of the realty, regardless of the manner in which they are affixed to or integrated with the Premises, and shall not become the property of the Lessor by reason of their attachment to the Building.\n\nThe Government shall have the right, but not the obligation, to remove any or all Government Improvements upon the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease. Such removal shall be completed within sixty (60) calendar days following the effective date of expiration or termination, during which period the Government shall have continued access to the Premises for the purpose of removal activities. The Government shall provide the Lessor with reasonable advance notice of its intent to remove Government Improvements and shall coordinate access with the Lessor\\'s property manager.\n\n**The Government shall not be required to restore the Premises to their original or pre-improvement condition upon removal of Government Improvements**, except that the Government shall repair any damage to the Building\\'s structural elements (including but not limited to load-bearing walls, floor slabs, roof deck, and exterior envelope) caused directly by such removal. For the avoidance of doubt, the Government shall have no obligation to repair or restore cosmetic finishes, non-structural partitions, ceiling systems, floor coverings, or any other non-structural elements of the Premises affected by the removal of Government Improvements.\n\nIf the Government elects not to remove certain Government Improvements, such improvements shall be deemed abandoned by the Government and shall become the property of the Lessor at no cost to the Lessor and without any obligation on the part of the Government. The Lessor acknowledges and agrees that any Government Improvements left in place may not be in a condition suitable for use by the Lessor or any subsequent tenant and that the Lessor assumes all risk associated with the condition and utility of such abandoned improvements.\n\n**[Section 7.3 --- Lessor\\'s Base Building Obligations]{.underline}**\n\nThe Lessor shall deliver the Premises in shell condition, which shall include the following base building improvements at the Lessor\\'s sole cost and expense:\n\n> \\(a\\) Finished main lobby and elevator lobby on the 5th floor;\n>\n> \\(b\\) Passenger elevator service from the ground floor to the 5th floor;\n>\n> \\(c\\) Base building HVAC infrastructure, including main air handling units, ductwork to the floor, and thermostatic controls;\n>\n> \\(d\\) Restrooms constructed in compliance with applicable building codes and the Americans with Disabilities Act;\n>\n> \\(e\\) Fire and life safety systems in compliance with applicable codes, including sprinklers, fire alarms, and emergency lighting;\n>\n> \\(f\\) Core and shell finishes, including concrete floor slabs, drywall to deck at the perimeter, and basic suspended ceiling grid in common areas; and\n>\n> \\(g\\) Base building electrical distribution to the floor, including a main electrical panel.\n\nThe Lessor is responsible for all structural repairs and maintenance of the Building, including the roof, exterior walls, foundation, and Building systems (mechanical, electrical, and plumbing) to the extent such systems are not part of Government Improvements.\n\n**[SECTION 8 --- ASSIGNMENT AND SUBLETTING]{.underline}**\n\n**Government\\'s Rights.** The Government may assign this Lease or sublet all or any portion of the Premises to any agency or instrumentality of the United States Government without the prior consent of the Lessor, in accordance with applicable provisions of the Federal Acquisition Regulation (FAR) and GSA policy. The Lessor shall recognize any such assignee or subtenant as a tenant under this Lease upon receipt of written notice from the Government\\'s Contracting Officer. No assignment or subletting by the Government shall relieve the Government of its obligations under this Lease unless the Government expressly releases itself in writing.\n\n**Lessor\\'s Rights.** The Lessor shall not assign this Lease or any interest therein, nor shall the Lessor delegate any of its obligations hereunder, without the prior written consent of the Government\\'s Contracting Officer. Any purported assignment by the Lessor without such consent shall be void and of no force or effect. Any permitted assignment by the Lessor shall be subject to and shall not alter, modify, or diminish the Government\\'s rights under this Lease, and the assignee shall assume in writing all of the Lessor\\'s obligations under this Lease.\n\n**Successors and Assigns.** This Lease shall be binding upon and inure to the benefit of the parties hereto and their respective successors and assigns, subject to the restrictions set forth in this Section 8. In the event of a sale, transfer, or other conveyance of the Building or any portion thereof that includes the Premises, the Lessor shall provide the Government\\'s Contracting Officer with written notice of the identity and contact information of the new owner or transferee not less than thirty (30) days prior to the effective date of such sale or transfer. The new owner or transferee shall automatically assume all obligations of the Lessor under this Lease from and after the date of such transfer, and the Lessor shall cause the new owner or transferee to execute such documents as the Government\\'s Contracting Officer may reasonably request to evidence such assumption.\n\n**[SECTION 9 --- INSURANCE]{.underline}**\n\n**Government Self-Insurance.** The Government is self-insured under the Federal Tort Claims Act (28 U.S.C. §§ 2671--2680) and other applicable Federal statutes. The Government shall not be required to carry or maintain any insurance policies with respect to the Premises or its occupancy thereof, including but not limited to commercial general liability insurance, property insurance, business interruption insurance, or workers\\' compensation insurance specific to this Lease.\n\n**Lessor\\'s Insurance Obligations.** The Lessor shall carry and maintain at the Lessor\\'s sole expense the following insurance coverage throughout the term of this Lease, including any holdover period:\n\n> \\(a\\) **Property Insurance** covering the Building and all improvements thereto (exclusive of Government Improvements) against loss or damage by fire, windstorm, and other perils customarily covered under an \\\"all risk\\\" or \\\"special form\\\" property insurance policy, in an amount not less than the full replacement cost of the Building;\n>\n> \\(b\\) **Commercial General Liability Insurance** with limits of not less than One Million Dollars (\\$1,000,000) per occurrence and Three Million Dollars (\\$3,000,000) in the annual aggregate, covering bodily injury, property damage, and personal injury arising out of the Lessor\\'s ownership, operation, and maintenance of the Building and common areas; and\n>\n> \\(c\\) **Workers\\' Compensation Insurance** as required by the laws of the State of Georgia, covering all of the Lessor\\'s employees performing work at or in connection with the Building.\n\nThe Lessor shall provide the Government\\'s Contracting Officer with certificates of insurance evidencing the foregoing coverage upon request and upon each renewal thereof. All insurance policies maintained by the Lessor shall be issued by insurers rated at least \\\"A-\\\" VII by A.M. Best Company or equivalent.\n\nThe Government shall not be liable for any loss or damage to the Building or the Lessor\\'s property arising out of fire, flood, or other casualty, except to the extent caused by the willful misconduct of the Government\\'s employees or agents acting within the scope of their employment.\n\n**[SECTION 10 --- SECURITY AND ACCESS]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 10.1 --- Building Access]{.underline}**\n\nThe Government shall have access to the Premises twenty-four (24) hours per day, seven (7) days per week, three hundred sixty-five (365) days per year, including Federal holidays and weekends. The Lessor shall provide the Government with keys, access cards, security codes, and/or other credentials necessary for such unrestricted access. There shall be no additional charge for after-hours access to the Premises.\n\nThe Lessor shall provide staffed lobby security during normal business hours (7:00 AM to 6:00 PM, Monday through Friday, excluding Federal holidays) and electronic access control during non-business hours, at the Lessor\\'s expense as part of operating expenses included in the shell rent. The Government may supplement Building security with its own security personnel and systems at the Government\\'s expense.\n\n**[Section 10.2 --- SCIF and Classified Space]{.underline}**\n\nThe Lessor acknowledges that portions of the Premises may be designated as a Sensitive Compartmented Information Facility (\\\"SCIF\\\") or may contain classified national security information and materials. The Lessor and its agents, employees, contractors, and subcontractors shall not enter any SCIF-designated space or any other area of the Premises designated as a restricted or secure area without prior written authorization from the Government\\'s Contracting Officer or the Government\\'s designated Facility Security Officer (\\\"FSO\\\").\n\nThe Lessor shall cooperate with the Government in facilitating any security clearances or background investigations required for the Lessor\\'s personnel who may need access to non-SCIF portions of the Premises for maintenance, repair, and Building systems servicing purposes. The Government shall provide reasonable advance notice to the Lessor of any security clearance requirements applicable to the Lessor\\'s personnel.\n\nThe Government may install additional security measures within the Premises and on the Building exterior as required for the protection of classified information and materials, including but not limited to reinforced walls and barriers, intrusion detection systems, electronic and radio frequency shielding (including TEMPEST countermeasures), sound attenuation systems, dedicated HVAC systems with isolated air handling, and supplemental power systems. All such security installations shall be treated as Government Improvements under Section 7 of this Lease and shall be subject to the ownership and removal provisions set forth therein.\n\n**[SECTION 11 --- TELECOMMUNICATIONS]{.underline}**\n\nThe Government shall have the right, at no additional cost or charge, to install, maintain, operate, and remove telecommunications equipment on the roof of the Building and in risers, conduits, and other infrastructure pathways within the Building, for the exclusive use of the Government. Telecommunications equipment includes, without limitation, satellite dishes, microwave antennas, radio frequency antennas, cables (including fiber optic cables), junction boxes, amplifiers, and related infrastructure and support structures.\n\nThe Lessor shall provide the Government with reasonable access to the roof and to riser, chase, and conduit systems within the Building for the installation, maintenance, repair, and removal of telecommunications equipment. The Government shall coordinate roof access with the Lessor\\'s property manager and shall exercise reasonable care to avoid damage to the Building\\'s roofing membrane, structural components, or other Building systems during installation, maintenance, or removal activities.\n\nAll telecommunications equipment installed by the Government on the roof or within the Building shall be deemed Government Improvements under Section 7 of this Lease and shall be subject to the ownership and removal provisions set forth in Section 7.2. The Government shall have the right, but not the obligation, to remove all telecommunications equipment upon the expiration or earlier termination of this Lease.\n\nThe Lessor shall not charge the Government any fee, rental, license fee, or other consideration for the use of roof space, riser space, or Building infrastructure for telecommunications purposes. The Government\\'s telecommunications installations shall not be counted against the Premises RSF or result in any additional rent or operating expense charge.\n\n**[SECTION 12 --- ALTERATIONS AND MODIFICATIONS BY LESSOR]{.underline}**\n\nThe Lessor shall not make any alterations, modifications, or improvements to the Premises or to the Building systems serving the Premises without the prior written consent of the Government\\'s Contracting Officer. This restriction includes but is not limited to alterations to the HVAC system, electrical system, plumbing system, elevators, fire and life safety systems, security systems, structural elements, and common areas directly serving or adjacent to the Premises.\n\nAny proposed alterations to the Building that may affect the Government\\'s use and occupancy of the Premises, including changes to Building access, parking, common areas, or exterior signage, shall require prior consultation with the Government and written approval from the Government\\'s Contracting Officer before commencement of any such work. The Lessor shall provide the Government with at least thirty (30) days\\' prior written notice of any proposed alteration, together with a description of the scope and expected duration of the work.\n\n**[SECTION 13 --- CASUALTY AND CONDEMNATION]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 13.1 --- Damage or Destruction]{.underline}**\n\nIf the Premises or the Building are damaged by fire, flood, earthquake, hurricane, or other casualty to the extent that the Government is unable to use the Premises, in whole or in substantial part, for their intended purpose, the Government may, at its sole option:\n\n> \\(a\\) Terminate this Lease upon thirty (30) calendar days\\' written notice to the Lessor, effective as of the date specified in such notice; or\n>\n> \\(b\\) Require the Lessor to restore the Premises (exclusive of Government Improvements) to their pre-casualty condition within one hundred eighty (180) calendar days following the date of the casualty event. If the Lessor fails to complete such restoration within the 180-day period, the Government may terminate this Lease upon written notice to the Lessor.\n\nRent shall be abated proportionately during any period in which all or a portion of the Premises is unusable due to casualty damage, based on the ratio of unusable square footage to total RSF. The Lessor shall notify the Government promptly, and in any event within forty-eight (48) hours, of any casualty affecting the Premises or the Building.\n\n**[Section 13.2 --- Condemnation]{.underline}**\n\nIf all or a substantial portion of the Premises is taken by eminent domain or condemnation proceedings by any governmental authority, this Lease shall terminate as of the date of such taking, and rent shall be apportioned as of such date. If only a portion of the Premises is taken and such partial taking does not materially impair the Government\\'s use of the remaining Premises for their intended purpose, this Lease shall continue in full force and effect with respect to the remaining Premises, and rent shall be abated proportionally based on the ratio of the RSF taken to the total RSF of the Premises prior to such taking.\n\n**[SECTION 14 --- DEFAULT AND REMEDIES]{.underline}**\n\n**Lessor Default.** If the Lessor fails to perform any material obligation under this Lease and such failure continues for thirty (30) calendar days after written notice from the Government specifying the nature of the default (or such longer period as may be reasonably necessary to cure the default, provided the Lessor commences cure within such 30-day period and diligently prosecutes the same to completion), the Government may, at its sole election, exercise one or more of the following remedies:\n\n> \\(a\\) Cure the default and deduct the reasonable cost thereof from rent next becoming due, together with interest thereon at the rate established by the Secretary of the Treasury pursuant to the Prompt Payment Act;\n>\n> \\(b\\) Withhold or abate rent, in whole or in part, until the default is cured to the satisfaction of the Government\\'s Contracting Officer; or\n>\n> \\(c\\) Terminate this Lease upon written notice to the Lessor, effective as of the date specified in such notice.\n\nThe foregoing remedies are cumulative and are in addition to any other remedies available to the Government under applicable law or regulation.\n\n**Government Default.** The Government shall not be deemed in default of this Lease except for non-payment of rent when due. In the event of non-payment, the Lessor\\'s sole and exclusive remedy is to submit a claim in accordance with the Contract Disputes Act of 1978, as amended (41 U.S.C. § 7101 et seq.). The Lessor shall not have the right to terminate this Lease, to lock out or exclude the Government from the Premises, to withhold services, or to exercise any other self-help remedy on account of any default or alleged default by the Government.\n\nThe Lessor expressly acknowledges and agrees that the Government is immune from claims for consequential, incidental, punitive, or exemplary damages, and the Lessor waives any right to seek or recover such damages in any proceeding arising under or relating to this Lease. The Lessor further waives any right to terminate this Lease on account of any default by the Government.\n\n**[SECTION 15 --- QUIET ENJOYMENT]{.underline}**\n\nThe Lessor covenants and warrants that the Government, upon paying the rent and performing its obligations under this Lease, shall peaceably and quietly hold, occupy, and enjoy the Premises during the term of this Lease (including any holdover period) without hindrance, disturbance, or molestation by the Lessor, or by any person or entity claiming by, through, or under the Lessor, or by any person or entity having title paramount to the Lessor. The Lessor shall defend and indemnify the Government against any claims that interfere with the Government\\'s quiet enjoyment of the Premises.\n\n**[SECTION 16 --- SUBORDINATION AND NON-DISTURBANCE]{.underline}**\n\nThis Lease shall not be subordinate to any mortgage, deed of trust, deed to secure debt, or other lien or encumbrance now or hereafter placed upon the Building, the land upon which the Building is located, or the Lessor\\'s interest therein. The Lessor shall not grant any mortgage, deed of trust, or lien that would take priority over this Lease without the prior written consent of the Government\\'s Contracting Officer.\n\nIn the event of any foreclosure, deed in lieu of foreclosure, or other transfer of ownership of the Building arising from the exercise of remedies under any mortgage or lien, the Lessor shall ensure that this Lease remains in full force and effect and that the Government\\'s rights hereunder are not disturbed, diminished, or impaired. Any successor owner, whether by foreclosure, deed in lieu, or otherwise, shall be bound by all terms and conditions of this Lease and shall recognize the Government as tenant hereunder. The Lessor shall obtain from any existing or future mortgagee or lien holder a subordination, non-disturbance, and attornment agreement in form and substance satisfactory to the Government\\'s Contracting Officer, if requested by the Government.\n\n**[SECTION 17 --- ENVIRONMENTAL COMPLIANCE]{.underline}**\n\nThe Lessor represents and warrants that, to the best of the Lessor\\'s knowledge after due inquiry, the Building and the Premises are free from hazardous substances, toxic materials, mold, asbestos-containing materials, lead-based paint, and other environmental contaminants in concentrations or conditions that violate applicable federal, state, or local environmental laws, regulations, or standards.\n\nThe Lessor shall comply with all applicable federal, state, and local environmental laws and regulations throughout the term of this Lease, including without limitation the Comprehensive Environmental Response, Compensation, and Liability Act (CERCLA), the Resource Conservation and Recovery Act (RCRA), and the regulations of the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency and the Georgia Environmental Protection Division.\n\nThe Lessor shall promptly notify the Government\\'s Contracting Officer, in writing, of any environmental condition at the Building or on the property on which the Building is situated that may affect the health or safety of the Government\\'s employees or visitors, or that may affect the Government\\'s use and occupancy of the Premises. Such notice shall be provided within twenty-four (24) hours of the Lessor\\'s discovery of or notification regarding such condition.\n\nThe Government shall be responsible for the proper handling, storage, and disposal of any hazardous materials generated by the Government in connection with its use and occupancy of the Premises, in accordance with applicable law.\n\n**[SECTION 18 --- NOTICES]{.underline}**\n\nAll notices, demands, requests, consents, approvals, and other communications required or permitted under this Lease shall be in writing and shall be deemed duly given when delivered by hand (with written confirmation of receipt), by United States certified mail (return receipt requested, postage prepaid), or by nationally recognized overnight courier service (with tracking confirmation), addressed as follows:\n\n**To Lessor:**\n\n> Thornfield Realty Holdings LP\n>\n> Attn: Renata Thornfield-Kiess, President\n>\n> 500 South Tryon Street, Suite 2200 Charlotte, NC 28202\n\n**To Government:**\n\n> General Services Administration Region 4 (Southeast Sunbelt)\n>\n> Attn: James Hua, Contracting Officer\n>\n> 77 Forsyth Street SW, Suite 110 Atlanta, GA 30303\n\nEither party may change its notice address by providing written notice of such change to the other party in accordance with the provisions of this Section 18. A change of address shall be effective upon receipt of such notice by the other party.\n\nNotices shall be deemed received: (a) if delivered by hand, on the date of delivery; (b) if sent by certified mail, on the date shown on the return receipt; or (c) if sent by overnight courier, on the next business day following deposit with the courier service.\n\n**[SECTION 19 --- MISCELLANEOUS PROVISIONS]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 19.1 --- Governing Law]{.underline}**\n\nThis Lease shall be governed by and construed in accordance with Federal law, including the Federal Acquisition Regulation (FAR) and its supplements, the General Services Administration Acquisition Regulation (GSAR), and applicable provisions of the laws of the State of Georgia to the extent not preempted by Federal law.\n\n**[Section 19.2 --- Disputes]{.underline}**\n\nAny dispute arising under or relating to this Lease that is not resolved by agreement of the parties shall be resolved in accordance with the Contract Disputes Act of 1978, as amended (41 U.S.C. § 7101 et seq.). The Government\\'s Contracting Officer shall issue a written final decision on any claim submitted by the Lessor. The Lessor may appeal such final decision to the Civilian Board of Contract Appeals within ninety (90) days of receipt of the final decision, or may bring an action in the United States Court of Federal Claims within twelve (12) months of receipt of the final decision. Pending resolution of any dispute, the Lessor shall proceed diligently with performance of its obligations under this Lease.\n\n**[Section 19.3 --- Entire Agreement]{.underline}**\n\nThis Lease, together with all exhibits and attachments hereto (including Exhibit A --- Floor Plan of Premises, Exhibit B --- Rent Schedule, and Exhibit C --- Operating Expense Base Year Statement), constitutes the entire agreement between the parties with respect to the subject matter hereof and supersedes all prior negotiations, representations, warranties, and agreements, whether written or oral, between the parties relating to the leasing of the Premises.\n\n**[Section 19.4 --- Amendments]{.underline}**\n\nThis Lease may be amended, modified, or supplemented only by a written instrument executed by the Government\\'s Contracting Officer and the Lessor or their duly authorized representatives. No oral modification, waiver, or amendment shall be effective or binding upon either party.\n\n**[Section 19.5 --- Severability]{.underline}**\n\nIf any provision of this Lease is held to be invalid, illegal, or unenforceable by any court or tribunal of competent jurisdiction, the remaining provisions of this Lease shall continue in full force and effect, and the invalid provision shall be reformed to the minimum extent necessary to render it valid and enforceable.\n\n**[Section 19.6 --- No Waiver]{.underline}**\n\nThe failure of either party to enforce any provision of this Lease at any time or for any period of time shall not constitute a waiver of such provision or of the right of such party to enforce each and every provision of this Lease in the future.\n\n**[Section 19.7 --- Successors and Assigns]{.underline}**\n\nThis Lease shall be binding upon and inure to the benefit of the parties hereto and their respective heirs, executors, administrators, legal representatives, successors, and assigns, subject to the restrictions and requirements set forth in Section 8 (Assignment and Subletting) of this Lease.\n\n**[SECTION 20 --- PARKING]{.underline}**\n\nThe Lessor shall provide the Government with reasonable access to the Building\\'s parking facilities for use by Government employees and authorized visitors during the term of this Lease, including any holdover period. Parking shall be provided on a non-exclusive, unreserved basis within the Building\\'s parking garage or surface lot at no additional charge to the Government, to the extent spaces are available.\n\nThe Government shall not be required to pay any separate parking charges, fees, or assessments during the term of this Lease. Parking costs, to the extent incurred by the Lessor, are included within the operating expenses covered by the shell rent and operating expense escalation provisions of this Lease.\n\nThe Lessor shall maintain the parking facilities in a safe, clean, and well-lit condition, and shall provide directional signage and reasonable accommodations for persons with disabilities in compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act.\n\n**SIGNATURE PAGE**\n\n**IN WITNESS WHEREOF**, the parties hereto have executed this Lease as of the date first written above.\n\n**LESSOR:**\n\nTHORNFIELD REALTY HOLDINGS LP, a North Carolina limited partnership\n\nBy: Thornfield Management Corp., a North Carolina corporation, its General Partner\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: Renata Thornfield-Kiess\n\nTitle: President\n\nDate: September 30, 2020\n\n**LESSEE / THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA:**\n\nGENERAL SERVICES ADMINISTRATION Region 4 (Southeast Sunbelt)\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nName: James Hua\n\nTitle: Contracting Officer\n\nDate: September 30, 2020\n\n**EXHIBIT A**\n\n**FLOOR PLAN OF PREMISES**\n\nThe floor plan of the entire 5th Floor, Concord Office Tower, 300 Concord Plaza Drive, Atlanta, GA 30309, depicting approximately 15,000 rentable square feet, is attached hereto and incorporated herein by reference. The floor plan illustrates the general layout of the Premises, including the location of core areas, elevator lobbies, stairwells, and restrooms.\n\nSCIF-designated areas, if any, are not depicted on this floor plan for security purposes. The omission of SCIF areas from this Exhibit shall not affect the Government\\'s rights with respect to such areas under this Lease.\n\n**EXHIBIT B**\n\n**RENT SCHEDULE**\n\nThe following rent schedule sets forth the shell rent payable under this Lease for each Lease Year during both the Firm Term and the Soft Term:\n\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Lease Year**   **Period**                    **Shell Rent (\\$/RSF/Year)**   **Annual Rent**   **Monthly Rent**   **Term Type**\n  ---------------- ----------------------------- ------------------------------ ----------------- ------------------ ---------------\n  1                Jan 1, 2021 -- Dec 31, 2021   \\$31.50                        \\$472,500.00      \\$39,375.00        Firm\n\n  2                Jan 1, 2022 -- Dec 31, 2022   \\$31.50                        \\$472,500.00      \\$39,375.00        Firm\n\n  3                Jan 1, 2023 -- Dec 31, 2023   \\$31.50                        \\$472,500.00      \\$39,375.00        Firm\n\n  4                Jan 1, 2024 -- Dec 31, 2024   \\$31.50                        \\$472,500.00      \\$39,375.00        Firm\n\n  5                Jan 1, 2025 -- Dec 31, 2025   \\$31.50                        \\$472,500.00      \\$39,375.00        Firm\n\n  6                Jan 1, 2026 -- Dec 31, 2026   \\$32.29                        \\$484,350.00      \\$40,362.50        Soft\n\n  7                Jan 1, 2027 -- Dec 31, 2027   \\$33.10                        \\$496,500.00      \\$41,375.00        Soft\n\n  8                Jan 1, 2028 -- Dec 31, 2028   \\$33.93                        \\$508,950.00      \\$42,412.50        Soft\n\n  9                Jan 1, 2029 -- Dec 31, 2029   \\$34.78                        \\$521,700.00      \\$43,475.00        Soft\n\n  10               Jan 1, 2030 -- Dec 31, 2030   \\$35.64                        \\$534,600.00      \\$44,550.00        Soft\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Total Firm Term Rent (Years 1--5):** \\$2,362,500.00\n\n**Total Soft Term Rent (Years 6--10):** \\$2,546,100.00\n\n**Total Lease Term Rent (Years 1--10):** \\$4,908,600.00\n\nNote: Years 6 through 10 are designated as \\\"Soft\\\" term years and are subject to the Government\\'s right to terminate this Lease upon one hundred twenty (120) calendar days\\' prior written notice to the Lessor pursuant to Section 3.2. No termination fee or penalty is payable by the Government upon exercise of its termination right. Accordingly, the rental income set forth for Years 6 through 10 is not guaranteed and is contingent upon the Government\\'s continued occupancy during the Soft Term. Shell rent escalation rates during the Soft Term are calculated at 2.5% per annum, compounding annually, applied to the Firm Term base rate of \\$31.50/RSF/year. Annual and monthly rent figures have been rounded to the nearest dollar and cent, respectively.\n\n**EXHIBIT C**\n\n**OPERATING EXPENSE BASE YEAR STATEMENT**\n\nThe Base Year for operating expenses under this Lease is calendar year 2021. The Lessor shall provide the Government with a certified statement of actual operating expenses incurred for the Building during the Base Year within ninety (90) days following the end of calendar year 2021 (i.e., no later than March 31, 2022). Such statement shall include a reasonable itemization of all operating expense categories, including but not limited to real estate taxes, insurance premiums, utilities, janitorial services, common area maintenance, management fees, and other customary operating expense items.\n\nThe certified Base Year operating expense statement shall serve as the baseline for calculating the Government\\'s pro rata share of operating expense increases for each subsequent calendar year under Section 5.2 of this Lease. The Government reserves the right to audit the Base Year statement in accordance with the audit provisions set forth in Section 5.2.\n\n*\\[Remainder of this page intentionally left blank.\\]*\n"}
