ount, excluding taxes, insurance, utilities and snow/ice removal (\\\\u00a7 5.07) \\\\u2014 the only expense cap in the portfolio. **Gross-up to 95% occupancy** where the Building is less occupied (\\\\u00a7 5.08). Management fee capped at **4% of Building gross revenues**. Audit right; if an overcharge exceeds 5% Landlord pays the tenant's audit costs. Operating Expenses exclude capital except law-mandated or expense-reducing items amortised at 8%, debt service, income taxes, leasing commissions, other tenants' TI, and legal costs of tenant disputes.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(6) Renewal options\\\",\\n  \\\"**Two consecutive five-year options at 95% of fair market rent**, which must be exercised consecutively. Notice **not less than 12 months** before expiry \\\\u2014 first option by **30 June 2031**, second by 30 June 2036. No default condition at exercise or commencement. **No TI allowance in either Renewal Term** unless separately agreed. FMR by agreement or a three-MAI-appraiser process using the average of the two closest appraisals.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(7) Termination rights\\\",\\n  \\\"**No general early termination right for the tenant.** **Contraction right (\\\\u00a7 14):** at any time after the end of Lease Year 5 (i.e. on or after 1 July 2027) the tenant may, once, surrender up to **5,000 RSF** (26.7% of the premises) on **nine months' notice**, paying a contraction fee equal to the unamortised TI allowance allocable to that space (amortised at 7% over 120 months on $55.00/RSF) plus **six months' Base Rent** on the surrendered space; the premises, Base Rent, pro rata share and parking are then reduced proportionately. Landlord may terminate on default and may **accelerate** all remaining rent discounted at the Federal Reserve discount rate plus 1% (\\\\u00a7 20.02). **Casualty:** if Landlord determines repair will exceed **270 days**, either party may terminate; rent abates proportionately. **Condemnation:** termination on a total or disabling partial taking; awards to Landlord, with a separate claim for the tenant's moving costs, its own TI excess and business goodwill. Holdover **150%** plus damages.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(8) Assignment and subletting\\\",\\n  \\\"Assignment requires Landlord's consent, **not unreasonably withheld, conditioned or delayed**, and the assignor remains secondarily liable (\\\\u00a7 11.05). **No recapture right** \\\\u2014 \\\\u00a7 11.07 states expressly that Landlord has no recapture, termination or cancellation right in connection with any assignment or sublease. **Subletting up to 40% (7,500 RSF) is permitted without consent** subject to conditions: use consistent with the Permitted Use, no governmental entity, not an existing tenant or a prospect in active negotiation, and notice with a copy of the executed sublease within 10 business days (\\\\u00a7 11.02). Subletting above 40% requires consent within 20 business days, with **deemed consent on silence** (\\\\u00a7 11.03). **50% of Subletting Profits is payable to Landlord** (\\\\u00a7 11.04). **Affiliate transfers** permitted without consent subject to a tangible net-worth test measured against the tenant's net worth at the Lease Date and 15 days' notice (\\\\u00a7 11.06).\\\"),\\n (\\\"(9) Security deposit / guaranty\\\",\\n  \\\"**None.** Article I states \\\\u201cSecurity Deposit: None\\\\u201d and there is no guaranty. The largest tenancy in the portfolio (28.8% of aggregate rent) is supported only by the covenant of a Delaware corporation.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(10) Tenant improvements\\\",\\n  \\\"TI Allowance **$55.00/RSF = $1,031,250.00** (Exhibit C), drawn monthly against AIA G702/G703 applications and lien waivers with **10% retainage** released on substantial completion and certificate of occupancy. Unused allowance is **forfeited**, not credited. For recapture purposes the allowance is amortised at **7% per annum over 120 months** on a mortgage-style basis (\\\\u00a7 7.04). Improvements become Landlord's property; Landlord may designate non-standard improvements for removal at the time it approves plans. **Expansion Space allowance, if the option is exercised: $40.00/RSF \\\\u00d7 18,500 RSF = $740,000.00.**\\\"),\\n (\\\"(11) Special provisions\\\",\\n  \\\"**Expansion option (Article XIII) \\\\u2014 the principal encumbrance.** Verdana may require the Lessor to add the **entire 9th floor, approximately 18,500 RSF**, exercisable **at any time during Lease Years 3 to 5 (1 July 2024 \\\\u2013 30 June 2027)** on at least six months' notice. Expansion rent is **the same rate per RSF then in effect for the 8th floor** ($36.07/RSF in Year 3), escalating thereafter on the same 3% schedule, with a **$40.00/RSF allowance ($740,000.00)** and a term coterminous to 30 June 2032. The Lessor has no right to refuse. **Contraction right** (category 7). **Subordination:** subordination is expressly **conditioned** on the mortgagee delivering a commercially reasonable SNDA (\\\\u00a7 17.01), and the Landlord represented that **no mortgage encumbered the Building** as at 28 February 2022 (\\\\u00a7 17.02). **Estoppel:** either party within 15 business days, with deemed acknowledgment (\\\\u00a7 XXIV). After-hours HVAC $75.00 per hour per zone, adjustable. Alterations under $50,000 per project require no consent. Governing law: Georgia.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(12) Risk flags\\\",\\n  \\\"**R-06** 9th-floor expansion option at below-market fixed rent with a $740,000 allowance, window already open (high). **R-17** contraction right over up to 5,000 RSF. **R-21** no deposit or guaranty for the largest tenancy. **R-10** subordination conditional on an SNDA that does not exist; the no-mortgage representation is more than two years old. **R-09** estoppel exists but has not been obtained.\\\"),\\n], notes=[\\n \\\"Rent schedule recomputed on 3% compounding from $34.00 across all ten years: every stated rate and amount agrees to the nearest cent \\\\u2014 **the only lease in the portfolio with a fully conforming schedule**.\\\",\\n \\\"Pro rata share recomputed: 18,750 \\\\u00f7 150,000 = 12.50% \\\\u2014 correct. Parking ratio: 56 \\\\u00f7 18.75 = 2.99 per 1,000 RSF \\\\u2014 correct. Reserved parking: 8 \\\\u00d7 $175 \\\\u00d7 12 = $16,800.00 \\\\u2014 correct, and correctly excluded from the Rent Roll's base rent total (Rent Roll note 6).\\\",\\n \\\"TI allowance recomputed: 18,750 \\\\u00d7 $55.00 = $1,031,250.00 \\\\u2014 correct. Expansion allowance: 18,500 \\\\u00d7 $40.00 = $740,000.00 \\\\u2014 correct.\\\",\\n \\\"**Illustrative contraction fee.** Assuming contraction of the full 5,000 RSF effective 1 April 2028 (the earliest date consistent with notice given on 1 July 2027), the fee would be approximately $98,525 of Base Rent (six months at the Year 6 rate of $39.41/RSF \\\\u00d7 5,000 RSF) plus approximately $140,600 of unamortised TI allowance (7% mortgage-style amortisation of $275,000 over 120 months, after 69 months) \\\\u2014 in the order of **$239,000**. This is illustrative only: the fee depends on the contraction date and the rate then in effect.\\\",\\n \\\"Exhibit E contains a typographical error (\\\\u201cMartin Lueger King Jr. Day\\\\u201d). Cosmetic, noted for completeness of the document-integrity record.\\\",\\n],\\nrisk_line=\\\"R-06 (high), R-17, R-21, R-10, R-09.\\\")\\nPYEOF\\necho ok\"}", "result": "ok\n"}
{"turn": 28, "role": "assistant", "text": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_ET_E0RRwftvey0dFlKNsGlr9019", "name": "bash", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd $WORKSPACE_DIR && cat >> work/build.py <<'PYEOF'\\n\\n# ---------- 4.6 PINT ----------\\nh2(doc, \\\"4.6  The Pint & Platter Restaurant Group LLC \\\\u2014 Haywood Village Shops, Unit 4\\\")\\nsnapshot(doc, [\\n (\\\"Tenant\\\",\\\"The Pint & Platter Restaurant Group LLC (NC LLC)\\\"),\\n (\\\"Premises\\\",\\\"Unit 4, 4,400 RSF ground-floor retail + patio\\\"),\\n (\\\"Landlord\\\",\\\"Thornfield Realty Holdings LP\\\"),\\n (\\\"Property\\\",\\\"92 Haywood Street, Asheville, Buncombe Co., NC 28801\\\"),\\n (\\\"Lease date\\\",\\\"May 15, 2020\\\"),\\n (\\\"Source\\\",\\\"D8 \\\\u2014 Lease Agreement dated May 15, 2020 (incl. Guaranty, Ex. D)\\\"),\\n (\\\"Current annual rent\\\",\\\"$89,386.00  ($7,448.83 / month) plus % rent\\\"),\\n (\\\"Share of portfolio\\\",\\\"3.8%\\\"),\\n])\\nabstraction(doc, [\\n (\\\"(1) Tenant and premises\\\",\\n  \\\"The Pint & Platter Restaurant Group LLC, a North Carolina limited liability company whose sole member is Lance Whitford. **Unit 4**, approximately **4,400 RSF** of ground-floor retail space including an adjacent patio, in Haywood Village Shops \\\\u2014 a centre of approximately **62,000 RSF** of leasable retail and commercial space. Pro rata share **7.10%** (4,400 \\\\u00f7 62,000 = 7.0968%). Permitted use: full-service restaurant, bar and related food and beverage service, including on-premises and off-premises catering, private events and delivery.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(2) Term\\\",\\n  \\\"Lease Date 15 May 2020; Commencement **1 October 2020**; Expiration **30 September 2030**; **ten years**. **Buildout Period** 15 May \\\\u2013 30 September 2020 (approximately 4.5 months) during which no Base Rent or Percentage Rent accrued. Renewal Terms: 1 October 2030 \\\\u2013 30 September 2035 and 1 October 2035 \\\\u2013 30 September 2040.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(3) Rent\\\",\\n  \\\"Base Rent Year 1: **$18.000/RSF** = $79,200.00 per annum ($6,600.00 per month). Escalation: **CPI-U (1982\\\\u201384 = 100, All Items, U.S. City Average)** measured as the change between the index three months before each Adjustment Date and the same month a year earlier, subject to a **floor of 2% and a cap of 4%** per annum. Current Lease Year 5 (1 October 2024 \\\\u2013 30 September 2025): **$20.315/RSF = $89,386.00 per annum ($7,448.83 per month)**. Years 6 to 10 to be determined annually on the same mechanism. **Percentage Rent:** 6% of annual Gross Sales above the Breakpoint, payable annually within 60 days after each Lease Year end, commencing in Lease Year 2; certified annual sales statement within 30 days and monthly sales reports within 15 days.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(4) Lease type\\\",\\n  \\\"**Triple net plus percentage rent** \\\\u2014 Article 6 and \\\\u00a7 3.5.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(5) Operating expenses\\\",\\n  \\\"Tenant pays its 7.10% pro rata share of real estate taxes, insurance premiums and CAM Expenses, by monthly estimate with a 90-day reconciliation statement and a tenant audit right on 30 days' notice. **CAM management fee capped at 4% of gross rental revenue** of the Shopping Center. Operating Expenses exclude Landlord financing costs, depreciation, leasing commissions and capital improvements except those amortised as required by law or to reduce Operating Expenses. No cap on recoveries.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(6) Renewal options\\\",\\n  \\\"**Two** five-year options at **fair market rent**, mutually determined in good faith, subject to a **floor equal to the Base Rent payable in the final Lease Year of the preceding term**. Notice **not less than nine months** before expiry \\\\u2014 first option by **31 December 2029**. No default condition at exercise or commencement. If the parties cannot agree within 60 days, a single MAI appraiser jointly selected determines the rent **and the determination is binding**.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(7) Termination rights\\\",\\n  \\\"**Co-tenancy right (Article 10):** if aggregate occupancy of Haywood Village Shops falls below **70% of total leasable area for more than 180 consecutive days**, the tenant may elect either (a) to pay **75% of the then-applicable Base Rent** plus Percentage Rent (without adjustment to the Breakpoint) until occupancy is restored, or (b) to **terminate on 60 days' notice**. The right lapses if occupancy is restored before the remedy is exercised and revives on a new failure. **Casualty:** if more than 50% of the RSF is untenantable either party may terminate on 60 days' notice given within 90 days of the damage; at 50% or less Landlord must restore within 180 days, with proportional abatement. **Condemnation:** termination on a total or disabling taking. Landlord termination on default (Article 14) with damages including the present value at 6% of the rent differential. Holdover **150%**.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(8) Assignment and subletting\\\",\\n  \\\"Landlord's prior written consent required, **not to be unreasonably withheld, conditioned or delayed** (\\\\u00a7 9.1). The Landlord may consider the assignee's financial condition, business reputation, proposed use (which must conform to the Permitted Use) and restaurant operating experience, and may require the tenant to remain liable and the assignee to execute an assumption agreement (\\\\u00a7 9.2). **No release** on any transfer (\\\\u00a7 9.3). No recapture right; no profit share.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(9) Security deposit / guaranty\\\",\\n  \\\"**Security deposit $19,800.00**, stated as equal to three months of Lease Year 1 Base Rent \\\\u2014 verified: $6,600.00 \\\\u00d7 3 = $19,800.00. Non-interest-bearing, need not be segregated, with replenishment and return within 30 days after expiry. **Personal Guaranty of Lance Whitford, individually** (Exhibit D): unconditional and irrevocable, a guaranty of payment and performance, primary and direct, with full waivers including any statute of limitations defence. **It expires on the earlier of (a) 30 September 2025 or (b) the date the tenant achieves trailing-twelve-month Gross Sales of $2,000,000 or more**, with a recordable release to follow on request.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(10) Tenant improvements\\\",\\n  \\\"**No TI allowance is granted** and no allowance appears in Exhibit B. The tenant designed, permitted and constructed its leasehold improvements at its own cost during the Buildout Period. Improvements become Landlord's property other than trade fixtures, equipment, signage and personal property, which the tenant must remove at surrender and repair any resulting damage (\\\\u00a7 16).\\\"),\\n (\\\"(11) Special provisions\\\",\\n  \\\"**Exclusive use (\\\\u00a7 5.2):** the tenant holds the exclusive right to operate a full-service restaurant at Haywood Village Shops; Landlord may still lease to quick-service, fast-casual, coffee, bakery, ice cream or specialty food operators provided any such operator occupies **less than 2,000 RSF**. Breach entitles the tenant to specific performance, injunctive relief **and abatement of Base Rent**. **Radius restriction (\\\\u00a7 5.3):** the tenant may not operate a competing restaurant within **three miles** during the term and for 12 months after; a breach pulls the revenues of the competing restaurant into Gross Sales for Percentage Rent. **Co-tenancy** (category 7). **Liquor licensing:** the lease was contingent on the tenant obtaining an ABC permit within 120 days, and the lease records that the condition has been satisfied (\\\\u00a7 5.5); liquor liability insurance of $1,000,000 per occurrence is required. **Operating covenants:** quarterly grease trap servicing, delivery window 6:00\\\\u201310:00 a.m., patio maintenance, and noise restrictions after 10:00 p.m. (Exhibit C). **SNDA:** Landlord to use commercially reasonable efforts (Article 12). Governing law: North Carolina, venue Buncombe County.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(12) Risk flags\\\",\\n  \\\"**R-07** co-tenancy trigger on 70% of a 62,000 RSF centre whose occupancy cannot be verified from the documents provided (high). **R-14** conflict between the fixed $1,350,000 Breakpoint and the defined \\\\u201cNatural Breakpoint\\\\u201d. **R-21** guaranty may already have burned off. **R-08** exclusive-use covenant limits re-letting. **R-09** no estoppel obligation.\\\"),\\n], notes=[\\n \\\"Rent schedule recomputed against the CPI floor/cap band. Implied annual steps: Year 2 **+3.80%**, Year 3 **+4.00% (at the cap)**, Year 4 **+2.50%**, Year 5 **+2.00% (at the floor)**. All four fall within the 2%\\\\u20134% band, and the annual and monthly amounts agree ($82,209.60 / $85,496.40 / $87,634.80 / $89,386.00). Because Year 5 sits exactly on the floor, the underlying CPI-U movement for the July 2023 to July 2024 comparison should be confirmed against BLS data when the billing ledger is reconciled: if actual CPI exceeded 2%, the Year 5 rate is understated.\\\",\\n \\\"**Percentage rent breakpoint conflict.** \\\\u00a7 3.3 fixes the Breakpoint at **$1,350,000 for each Lease Year**, and Exhibit B repeats that figure. Article 1 separately defines the **\\\\u201cNatural Breakpoint\\\\u201d** as Base Rent \\\\u00f7 0.06, recalculated each Lease Year, and states that Gross Sales above the Natural Breakpoint are subject to Percentage Rent. For Year 5 the Natural Breakpoint would be $89,386.00 \\\\u00f7 0.06 = **$1,489,766.67**. The two provisions therefore differ by approximately $139,767 of sales, or about $8,386 of percentage rent per annum at the margin. The fixed figure is the specific provision and probably controls, but the ambiguity is worth resolving by estoppel.\\\",\\n \\\"Security deposit verified: three months of Lease Year 1 Base Rent ($79,200.00 \\\\u00f7 12 \\\\u00d7 3) = $19,800.00 \\\\u2014 agrees with \\\\u00a7 4 and with the Rent Roll.\\\",\\n \\\"Pro rata share recomputed: 4,400 \\\\u00f7 62,000 = 7.0968%, stated as approximately 7.10% \\\\u2014 correct.\\\",\\n \\\"Guaranty status: the burn-off date of 30 September 2025 falls less than seven months after the target closing. Seller should produce the certified gross sales reports for the trailing twelve months and any release already executed under Exhibit D \\\\u00a7 2.\\\",\\n],\\nrisk_line=\\\"R-07 (high), R-08, R-14, R-21, R-09.\\\")\\n\\n# ---------- 4.7 GSA ----------\\nh2(doc, \\\"4.7  United States of America (GSA) \\\\u2014 Concord Office Tower, 5th Floor\\\")\\nsnapshot(doc, [\\n (\\\"Tenant\\\",\\\"United States of America, acting through GSA Region 4\\\"),\\n (\\\"Premises\\\",\\\"Entire 5th floor, 15,000 RSF government office\\\"),\\n (\\\"Lessor\\\",\\\"Thornfield Realty Holdings LP\\\"),\\n (\\\"Property\\\",\\\"300 Concord Plaza Drive, Atlanta, Fulton Co., GA 30309\\\"),\\n (\\\"Lease\\\",\\\"GSA Lease No. GS-04B-15678 (Form L201D), executed 30 Sep 2020\\\"),\\n (\\\"Source\\\",\\\"D9 \\\\u2014 Lease for Real Property, GS-04B-15678\\\"),\\n (\\\"Current annual rent\\\",\\\"$472,500.00  ($39,375.00 / month, in arrears)\\\"),\\n (\\\"Share of portfolio\\\",\\\"20.1%\\\"),\\n])\\nabstraction(doc, [\\n (\\\"(1) Tenant and premises\\\",\\n  \\\"The United States of America, acting by and through the **General Services Administration, Region 4 (Southeast Sunbelt)**, Contracting Officer James Hua. The **entire 5th floor**, approximately **15,000 RSF** of government office space in Concord Office Tower (approximately 150,000 RSF per the Verdana lease). Measured under **ANSI/BOMA Z65.1-2017**, with any dispute resolved by the Contracting Officer's final determination, binding on the parties. Pro rata share is 15,000 divided by total Building RSF \\\\u2014 **the lease does not state the Building area or the resulting percentage** (10.00% if the 150,000 RSF figure is used). The premises may include **SCIF or SCIF-capable space**; classified areas are omitted from Exhibit A and the Lessor has no right to review or approve their design. The Government has non-exclusive common area access and **24/7/365 access** to the premises with no additional charge.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(2) Term\\\",\\n  \\\"Execution Date 30 September 2020; Lease Occupancy Date **1 January 2021**. **Firm Term** five years, 1 January 2021 \\\\u2013 **31 December 2025**. **Soft Term** five years, 1 January 2026 \\\\u2013 **31 December 2030**. Total lease term ten years. **There is no renewal option and no right of extension in favour of either party.**\\\"),\\n (\\\"(3) Rent\\\",\\n  \\\"**Shell rent $31.50/RSF = $472,500.00 per annum ($39,375.00 per month), fixed for the entire Firm Term** with no escalation. Soft Term escalates at **2.5% per annum compounding** off $31.50 (Year 6 $32.29 = $484,350.00 through Year 10 $35.64 = $534,600.00). **Rent is payable monthly in arrears by electronic funds transfer**, subject to the Prompt Payment Act. Firm Term total $2,362,500.00; Soft Term total $2,546,100.00; total term $4,908,600.00 \\\\u2014 all three figures verified.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(4) Lease type\\\",\\n  \\\"**Government lease (GSA Form L201D)** \\\\u2014 full service with a **Base Year 2021** operating expense stop; the shell rent is inclusive of base-year operating expenses.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(5) Operating expenses\\\",\\n  \\\"The Government pays its pro rata share of the **increase in actual operating expenses above calendar year 2021** (the Base Year), including taxes, insurance, utilities, janitorial, landscaping, pest control, management fees and CAM. Annual statement within 90 days; underpayment due within 30 days and overpayment credited to the next monthly rent. The Government may **audit the Lessor's books during the term and for three years after expiry**, with records to be produced within 15 business days. **No cap** on escalations and the **management fee is not capped in the lease**. The Lessor was to deliver a certified 2021 Base Year statement by 31 March 2022 (Exhibit C).\\\"),\\n (\\\"(6) Renewal options\\\",\\n  \\\"**None.** Neither party holds a renewal or extension option. At the end of the Soft Term (or earlier on 120 days' notice) the Government vacates and the space reverts; the Lessor has no contractual right to retain the tenancy.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(7) Termination rights\\\",\\n  \\\"**The Government may terminate at any time after 31 December 2025 on not less than 120 calendar days' prior written notice.** The right is absolute and unilateral; no cause need be given; and **\\\\u201cno termination fee, penalty, liquidated damages or other consideration of any kind\\\\u201d** is payable (\\\\u00a7 3.2). Rent obligations are further subject to the **availability of appropriated funds** and the Anti-Deficiency Act (\\\\u00a7 4.3). **Casualty:** the Government may terminate on 30 days' notice or require restoration within 180 days (\\\\u00a7 13.1). **Condemnation** (\\\\u00a7 13.2). **Holdover:** the Government becomes a tenant at sufferance paying **the existing rent with no premium**, and the Lessor **expressly waives any claim for holdover rent, premium or damages** (\\\\u00a7 3.3) \\\\u2014 the weakest holdover remedy in the portfolio. **Lessor default:** the Government may cure and deduct, withhold or abate rent, or terminate (\\\\u00a7 14). **Government default:** the Lessor's sole remedy is a claim under the Contract Disputes Act; the Lessor may not terminate, lock out or exercise self-help, and waives consequential, punitive and exemplary damages.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(8) Assignment and subletting\\\",\\n  \\\"**Government:** may assign or sublet to any agency or instrumentality of the United States without the Lessor's consent under the FAR, and the Lessor must recognise the assignee on written notice from the Contracting Officer (\\\\u00a7 8). **Lessor:** **may not assign the lease or delegate any obligation without the Contracting Officer's prior written consent**; an unconsented assignment is void; any permitted assignee must assume the Lessor's obligations in writing. On any sale or transfer of the Building the Lessor must give the Contracting Officer **30 days' prior written notice** of the transferee's identity, and the transferee must execute an assumption (\\\\u00a7 8).\\\"),\\n (\\\"(9) Security deposit / guaranty\\\",\\n  \\\"**None, and none required.** The Government is self-insured under the Federal Tort Claims Act and is not required to carry any insurance. Credit rests on the full faith and credit of the United States, which is the strongest credit in the portfolio \\\\u2014 but it attaches only for so long as the Government elects to remain.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(10) Tenant improvements\\\",\\n  \\\"**No Lessor allowance.** All Government Improvements are funded by the Government at its sole cost (\\\\u00a7 7.1). They include interior build-out, partitions, flooring, cabling, IT, security and access control, and any SCIF or classified installations. **All Government Improvements remain the property of the Government at all times, are not fixtures, and do not become the Lessor's property by attachment** (\\\\u00a7 7.2). The Government may remove them within **60 calendar days** after expiry and has **no obligation to restore** other than repair of structural damage caused by removal \\\\u2014 expressly excluding cosmetic finishes, non-structural partitions, ceilings and floor coverings. Improvements not removed are **deemed abandoned and become the Lessor's property at no cost**, and the Lessor \\\\u201cassumes all risk\\\\u201d that they may not be suitable for a subsequent tenant.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(11) Special provisions\\\",\\n  \\\"**Anti-subordination (\\\\u00a7 16) \\\\u2014 the critical provision for financing.** The lease is expressly **not subordinate** to any mortgage, deed of trust or other lien now or later placed on the Building, and the Lessor **may not grant any mortgage taking priority without the Contracting Officer's consent**. On foreclosure the Lessor must ensure the lease remains in force, and the Lessor must obtain an SNDA \\\\u201cin form and substance satisfactory to the Contracting Officer\\\\u201d if the Government requests one. **Lessor alterations (\\\\u00a7 12):** the Lessor may not alter the premises or any building system serving them \\\\u2014 including HVAC, electrical, plumbing, elevators, fire and life safety systems, structural elements and adjacent common areas \\\\u2014 without the Contracting Officer's prior written consent and 30 days' notice. **Telecommunications (\\\\u00a7 11):** the Government may install roof, riser and conduit equipment at no charge, and such equipment is a Government Improvement. **Insurance:** the Lessor carries all property, general liability and workers' compensation insurance at its own cost (\\\\u00a7 9). **Environmental:** the Lessor represents the Building is free of hazardous substances and must notify the Contracting Officer within 24 hours of any environmental condition (\\\\u00a7 17). Governing law: federal law (FAR/GSAR) together with Georgia law where not preempted; disputes under the Contract Disputes Act with appeal to the Civilian Board of Contract Appeals or the Court of Federal Claims.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(12) Risk flags\\\",\\n  \\\"**R-02** anti-subordination and lessor-assignment consent \\\\u2014 both are obstacles to acquisition financing and to an asset-level closing (high). **R-03** only twelve months of firm term; 20.1% of aggregate rent becomes terminable on 120 days' notice with no penalty (high). **R-16** no holdover premium and an express waiver of holdover damages; abandonment and SCIF condition risk on re-letting. **R-19** building area and the recovery percentage are not stated. No renewal option.\\\"),\\n], notes=[\\n \\\"Rent schedule and totals recomputed: firm term 5 \\\\u00d7 $472,500.00 = $2,362,500.00; soft term $484,350 + $496,500 + $508,950 + $521,700 + $534,600 = $2,546,100.00; total $4,908,600.00 \\\\u2014 all agree with Exhibit B.\\\",\\n \\\"**Rounding only.** Section 4.2 states the operative formula as $31.50 \\\\u00d7 (1.025)^(n\\\\u22125). Applied from the base year, that gives $33.095 / $33.922 / $34.770 for Years 7 to 9, whereas Exhibit B states $33.10 / $33.93 / $34.78 \\\\u2014 each one cent higher. Year 10 agrees ($35.64). The difference is $150 per annum in each affected year and has no economic significance, but it shows that the table was built by chaining the prior year's rounded rate rather than by applying the stated formula.\\\",\\n \\\"The lease contains **no rent schedule for any renewal term** because there is no renewal right. Any assumption of value beyond 31 December 2030 must therefore be based on market re-letting, not on the existing lease.\\\",\\n \\\"**Rent is payable in arrears**, unlike all six other leases. That affects closing prorations and the cash-flow profile in the first month after closing.\\\",\\n \\\"Deal-structure note: because \\\\u00a7 8 prohibits assignment of the lease without federal consent, an **asset-level acquisition** of Concord Office Tower would require GSA consent or a novation at closing, and the 30-day notice and written assumption requirements would need to be satisfied. An **equity-level acquisition** of Thornfield would not constitute an assignment of the lease and avoids the issue \\\\u2014 which is a further reason to confirm structure early (Section 10, item 1).\\\",\\n],\\nrisk_line=\\\"R-02 (high), R-03 (high), R-16, R-19.\\\")\\nPYEOF\\necho ok\"}"}], "input_tokens": 189255, "output_tokens": 5214, "finish_reason": "completed"}
{"turn": 28, "role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "call_00_ET_E0RRwftvey0dFlKNsGlr9019", "tool_name": "bash", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd $WORKSPACE_DIR && cat >> work/build.py <<'PYEOF'\\n\\n# ---------- 4.6 PINT ----------\\nh2(doc, \\\"4.6  The Pint & Platter Restaurant Group LLC \\\\u2014 Haywood Village Shops, Unit 4\\\")\\nsnapshot(doc, [\\n (\\\"Tenant\\\",\\\"The Pint & Platter Restaurant Group LLC (NC LLC)\\\"),\\n (\\\"Premises\\\",\\\"Unit 4, 4,400 RSF ground-floor retail + patio\\\"),\\n (\\\"Landlord\\\",\\\"Thornfield Realty Holdings LP\\\"),\\n (\\\"Property\\\",\\\"92 Haywood Street, Asheville, Buncombe Co., NC 28801\\\"),\\n (\\\"Lease date\\\",\\\"May 15, 2020\\\"),\\n (\\\"Source\\\",\\\"D8 \\\\u2014 Lease Agreement dated May 15, 2020 (incl. Guaranty, Ex. D)\\\"),\\n (\\\"Current annual rent\\\",\\\"$89,386.00  ($7,448.83 / month) plus % rent\\\"),\\n (\\\"Share of portfolio\\\",\\\"3.8%\\\"),\\n])\\nabstraction(doc, [\\n (\\\"(1) Tenant and premises\\\",\\n  \\\"The Pint & Platter Restaurant Group LLC, a North Carolina limited liability company whose sole member is Lance Whitford. **Unit 4**, approximately **4,400 RSF** of ground-floor retail space including an adjacent patio, in Haywood Village Shops \\\\u2014 a centre of approximately **62,000 RSF** of leasable retail and commercial space. Pro rata share **7.10%** (4,400 \\\\u00f7 62,000 = 7.0968%). Permitted use: full-service restaurant, bar and related food and beverage service, including on-premises and off-premises catering, private events and delivery.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(2) Term\\\",\\n  \\\"Lease Date 15 May 2020; Commencement **1 October 2020**; Expiration **30 September 2030**; **ten years**. **Buildout Period** 15 May \\\\u2013 30 September 2020 (approximately 4.5 months) during which no Base Rent or Percentage Rent accrued. Renewal Terms: 1 October 2030 \\\\u2013 30 September 2035 and 1 October 2035 \\\\u2013 30 September 2040.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(3) Rent\\\",\\n  \\\"Base Rent Year 1: **$18.000/RSF** = $79,200.00 per annum ($6,600.00 per month). Escalation: **CPI-U (1982\\\\u201384 = 100, All Items, U.S. City Average)** measured as the change between the index three months before each Adjustment Date and the same month a year earlier, subject to a **floor of 2% and a cap of 4%** per annum. Current Lease Year 5 (1 October 2024 \\\\u2013 30 September 2025): **$20.315/RSF = $89,386.00 per annum ($7,448.83 per month)**. Years 6 to 10 to be determined annually on the same mechanism. **Percentage Rent:** 6% of annual Gross Sales above the Breakpoint, payable annually within 60 days after each Lease Year end, commencing in Lease Year 2; certified annual sales statement within 30 days and monthly sales reports within 15 days.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(4) Lease type\\\",\\n  \\\"**Triple net plus percentage rent** \\\\u2014 Article 6 and \\\\u00a7 3.5.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(5) Operating expenses\\\",\\n  \\\"Tenant pays its 7.10% pro rata share of real estate taxes, insurance premiums and CAM Expenses, by monthly estimate with a 90-day reconciliation statement and a tenant audit right on 30 days' notice. **CAM management fee capped at 4% of gross rental revenue** of the Shopping Center. Operating Expenses exclude Landlord financing costs, depreciation, leasing commissions and capital improvements except those amortised as required by law or to reduce Operating Expenses. No cap on recoveries.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(6) Renewal options\\\",\\n  \\\"**Two** five-year options at **fair market rent**, mutually determined in good faith, subject to a **floor equal to the Base Rent payable in the final Lease Year of the preceding term**. Notice **not less than nine months** before expiry \\\\u2014 first option by **31 December 2029**. No default condition at exercise or commencement. If the parties cannot agree within 60 days, a single MAI appraiser jointly selected determines the rent **and the determination is binding**.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(7) Termination rights\\\",\\n  \\\"**Co-tenancy right (Article 10):** if aggregate occupancy of Haywood Village Shops falls below **70% of total leasable area for more than 180 consecutive days**, the tenant may elect either (a) to pay **75% of the then-applicable Base Rent** plus Percentage Rent (without adjustment to the Breakpoint) until occupancy is restored, or (b) to **terminate on 60 days' notice**. The right lapses if occupancy is restored before the remedy is exercised and revives on a new failure. **Casualty:** if more than 50% of the RSF is untenantable either party may terminate on 60 days' notice given within 90 days of the damage; at 50% or less Landlord must restore within 180 days, with proportional abatement. **Condemnation:** termination on a total or disabling taking. Landlord termination on default (Article 14) with damages including the present value at 6% of the rent differential. Holdover **150%**.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(8) Assignment and subletting\\\",\\n  \\\"Landlord's prior written consent required, **not to be unreasonably withheld, conditioned or delayed** (\\\\u00a7 9.1). The Landlord may consider the assignee's financial condition, business reputation, proposed use (which must conform to the Permitted Use) and restaurant operating experience, and may require the tenant to remain liable and the assignee to execute an assumption agreement (\\\\u00a7 9.2). **No release** on any transfer (\\\\u00a7 9.3). No recapture right; no profit share.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(9) Security deposit / guaranty\\\",\\n  \\\"**Security deposit $19,800.00**, stated as equal to three months of Lease Year 1 Base Rent \\\\u2014 verified: $6,600.00 \\\\u00d7 3 = $19,800.00. Non-interest-bearing, need not be segregated, with replenishment and return within 30 days after expiry. **Personal Guaranty of Lance Whitford, individually** (Exhibit D): unconditional and irrevocable, a guaranty of payment and performance, primary and direct, with full waivers including any statute of limitations defence. **It expires on the earlier of (a) 30 September 2025 or (b) the date the tenant achieves trailing-twelve-month Gross Sales of $2,000,000 or more**, with a recordable release to follow on request.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(10) Tenant improvements\\\",\\n  \\\"**No TI allowance is granted** and no allowance appears in Exhibit B. The tenant designed, permitted and constructed its leasehold improvements at its own cost during the Buildout Period. Improvements become Landlord's property other than trade fixtures, equipment, signage and personal property, which the tenant must remove at surrender and repair any resulting damage (\\\\u00a7 16).\\\"),\\n (\\\"(11) Special provisions\\\",\\n  \\\"**Exclusive use (\\\\u00a7 5.2):** the tenant holds the exclusive right to operate a full-service restaurant at Haywood Village Shops; Landlord may still lease to quick-service, fast-casual, coffee, bakery, ice cream or specialty food operators provided any such operator occupies **less than 2,000 RSF**. Breach entitles the tenant to specific performance, injunctive relief **and abatement of Base Rent**. **Radius restriction (\\\\u00a7 5.3):** the tenant may not operate a competing restaurant within **three miles** during the term and for 12 months after; a breach pulls the revenues of the competing restaurant into Gross Sales for Percentage Rent. **Co-tenancy** (category 7). **Liquor licensing:** the lease was contingent on the tenant obtaining an ABC permit within 120 days, and the lease records that the condition has been satisfied (\\\\u00a7 5.5); liquor liability insurance of $1,000,000 per occurrence is required. **Operating covenants:** quarterly grease trap servicing, delivery window 6:00\\\\u201310:00 a.m., patio maintenance, and noise restrictions after 10:00 p.m. (Exhibit C). **SNDA:** Landlord to use commercially reasonable efforts (Article 12). Governing law: North Carolina, venue Buncombe County.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(12) Risk flags\\\",\\n  \\\"**R-07** co-tenancy trigger on 70% of a 62,000 RSF centre whose occupancy cannot be verified from the documents provided (high). **R-14** conflict between the fixed $1,350,000 Breakpoint and the defined \\\\u201cNatural Breakpoint\\\\u201d. **R-21** guaranty may already have burned off. **R-08** exclusive-use covenant limits re-letting. **R-09** no estoppel obligation.\\\"),\\n], notes=[\\n \\\"Rent schedule recomputed against the CPI floor/cap band. Implied annual steps: Year 2 **+3.80%**, Year 3 **+4.00% (at the cap)**, Year 4 **+2.50%**, Year 5 **+2.00% (at the floor)**. All four fall within the 2%\\\\u20134% band, and the annual and monthly amounts agree ($82,209.60 / $85,496.40 / $87,634.80 / $89,386.00). Because Year 5 sits exactly on the floor, the underlying CPI-U movement for the July 2023 to July 2024 comparison should be confirmed against BLS data when the billing ledger is reconciled: if actual CPI exceeded 2%, the Year 5 rate is understated.\\\",\\n \\\"**Percentage rent breakpoint conflict.** \\\\u00a7 3.3 fixes the Breakpoint at **$1,350,000 for each Lease Year**, and Exhibit B repeats that figure. Article 1 separately defines the **\\\\u201cNatural Breakpoint\\\\u201d** as Base Rent \\\\u00f7 0.06, recalculated each Lease Year, and states that Gross Sales above the Natural Breakpoint are subject to Percentage Rent. For Year 5 the Natural Breakpoint would be $89,386.00 \\\\u00f7 0.06 = **$1,489,766.67**. The two provisions therefore differ by approximately $139,767 of sales, or about $8,386 of percentage rent per annum at the margin. The fixed figure is the specific provision and probably controls, but the ambiguity is worth resolving by estoppel.\\\",\\n \\\"Security deposit verified: three months of Lease Year 1 Base Rent ($79,200.00 \\\\u00f7 12 \\\\u00d7 3) = $19,800.00 \\\\u2014 agrees with \\\\u00a7 4 and with the Rent Roll.\\\",\\n \\\"Pro rata share recomputed: 4,400 \\\\u00f7 62,000 = 7.0968%, stated as approximately 7.10% \\\\u2014 correct.\\\",\\n \\\"Guaranty status: the burn-off date of 30 September 2025 falls less than seven months after the target closing. Seller should produce the certified gross sales reports for the trailing twelve months and any release already executed under Exhibit D \\\\u00a7 2.\\\",\\n],\\nrisk_line=\\\"R-07 (high), R-08, R-14, R-21, R-09.\\\")\\n\\n# ---------- 4.7 GSA ----------\\nh2(doc, \\\"4.7  United States of America (GSA) \\\\u2014 Concord Office Tower, 5th Floor\\\")\\nsnapshot(doc, [\\n (\\\"Tenant\\\",\\\"United States of America, acting through GSA Region 4\\\"),\\n (\\\"Premises\\\",\\\"Entire 5th floor, 15,000 RSF government office\\\"),\\n (\\\"Lessor\\\",\\\"Thornfield Realty Holdings LP\\\"),\\n (\\\"Property\\\",\\\"300 Concord Plaza Drive, Atlanta, Fulton Co., GA 30309\\\"),\\n (\\\"Lease\\\",\\\"GSA Lease No. GS-04B-15678 (Form L201D), executed 30 Sep 2020\\\"),\\n (\\\"Source\\\",\\\"D9 \\\\u2014 Lease for Real Property, GS-04B-15678\\\"),\\n (\\\"Current annual rent\\\",\\\"$472,500.00  ($39,375.00 / month, in arrears)\\\"),\\n (\\\"Share of portfolio\\\",\\\"20.1%\\\"),\\n])\\nabstraction(doc, [\\n (\\\"(1) Tenant and premises\\\",\\n  \\\"The United States of America, acting by and through the **General Services Administration, Region 4 (Southeast Sunbelt)**, Contracting Officer James Hua. The **entire 5th floor**, approximately **15,000 RSF** of government office space in Concord Office Tower (approximately 150,000 RSF per the Verdana lease). Measured under **ANSI/BOMA Z65.1-2017**, with any dispute resolved by the Contracting Officer's final determination, binding on the parties. Pro rata share is 15,000 divided by total Building RSF \\\\u2014 **the lease does not state the Building area or the resulting percentage** (10.00% if the 150,000 RSF figure is used). The premises may include **SCIF or SCIF-capable space**; classified areas are omitted from Exhibit A and the Lessor has no right to review or approve their design. The Government has non-exclusive common area access and **24/7/365 access** to the premises with no additional charge.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(2) Term\\\",\\n  \\\"Execution Date 30 September 2020; Lease Occupancy Date **1 January 2021**. **Firm Term** five years, 1 January 2021 \\\\u2013 **31 December 2025**. **Soft Term** five years, 1 January 2026 \\\\u2013 **31 December 2030**. Total lease term ten years. **There is no renewal option and no right of extension in favour of either party.**\\\"),\\n (\\\"(3) Rent\\\",\\n  \\\"**Shell rent $31.50/RSF = $472,500.00 per annum ($39,375.00 per month), fixed for the entire Firm Term** with no escalation. Soft Term escalates at **2.5% per annum compounding** off $31.50 (Year 6 $32.29 = $484,350.00 through Year 10 $35.64 = $534,600.00). **Rent is payable monthly in arrears by electronic funds transfer**, subject to the Prompt Payment Act. Firm Term total $2,362,500.00; Soft Term total $2,546,100.00; total term $4,908,600.00 \\\\u2014 all three figures verified.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(4) Lease type\\\",\\n  \\\"**Government lease (GSA Form L201D)** \\\\u2014 full service with a **Base Year 2021** operating expense stop; the shell rent is inclusive of base-year operating expenses.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(5) Operating expenses\\\",\\n  \\\"The Government pays its pro rata share of the **increase in actual operating expenses above calendar year 2021** (the Base Year), including taxes, insurance, utilities, janitorial, landscaping, pest control, management fees and CAM. Annual statement within 90 days; underpayment due within 30 days and overpayment credited to the next monthly rent. The Government may **audit the Lessor's books during the term and for three years after expiry**, with records to be produced within 15 business days. **No cap** on escalations and the **management fee is not capped in the lease**. The Lessor was to deliver a certified 2021 Base Year statement by 31 March 2022 (Exhibit C).\\\"),\\n (\\\"(6) Renewal options\\\",\\n  \\\"**None.** Neither party holds a renewal or extension option. At the end of the Soft Term (or earlier on 120 days' notice) the Government vacates and the space reverts; the Lessor has no contractual right to retain the tenancy.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(7) Termination rights\\\",\\n  \\\"**The Government may terminate at any time after 31 December 2025 on not less than 120 calendar days' prior written notice.** The right is absolute and unilateral; no cause need be given; and **\\\\u201cno termination fee, penalty, liquidated damages or other consideration of any kind\\\\u201d** is payable (\\\\u00a7 3.2). Rent obligations are further subject to the **availability of appropriated funds** and the Anti-Deficiency Act (\\\\u00a7 4.3). **Casualty:** the Government may terminate on 30 days' notice or require restoration within 180 days (\\\\u00a7 13.1). **Condemnation** (\\\\u00a7 13.2). **Holdover:** the Government becomes a tenant at sufferance paying **the existing rent with no premium**, and the Lessor **expressly waives any claim for holdover rent, premium or damages** (\\\\u00a7 3.3) \\\\u2014 the weakest holdover remedy in the portfolio. **Lessor default:** the Government may cure and deduct, withhold or abate rent, or terminate (\\\\u00a7 14). **Government default:** the Lessor's sole remedy is a claim under the Contract Disputes Act; the Lessor may not terminate, lock out or exercise self-help, and waives consequential, punitive and exemplary damages.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(8) Assignment and subletting\\\",\\n  \\\"**Government:** may assign or sublet to any agency or instrumentality of the United States without the Lessor's consent under the FAR, and the Lessor must recognise the assignee on written notice from the Contracting Officer (\\\\u00a7 8). **Lessor:** **may not assign the lease or delegate any obligation without the Contracting Officer's prior written consent**; an unconsented assignment is void; any permitted assignee must assume the Lessor's obligations in writing. On any sale or transfer of the Building the Lessor must give the Contracting Officer **30 days' prior written notice** of the transferee's identity, and the transferee must execute an assumption (\\\\u00a7 8).\\\"),\\n (\\\"(9) Security deposit / guaranty\\\",\\n  \\\"**None, and none required.** The Government is self-insured under the Federal Tort Claims Act and is not required to carry any insurance. Credit rests on the full faith and credit of the United States, which is the strongest credit in the portfolio \\\\u2014 but it attaches only for so long as the Government elects to remain.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(10) Tenant improvements\\\",\\n  \\\"**No Lessor allowance.** All Government Improvements are funded by the Government at its sole cost (\\\\u00a7 7.1). They include interior build-out, partitions, flooring, cabling, IT, security and access control, and any SCIF or classified installations. **All Government Improvements remain the property of the Government at all times, are not fixtures, and do not become the Lessor's property by attachment** (\\\\u00a7 7.2). The Government may remove them within **60 calendar days** after expiry and has **no obligation to restore** other than repair of structural damage caused by removal \\\\u2014 expressly excluding cosmetic finishes, non-structural partitions, ceilings and floor coverings. Improvements not removed are **deemed abandoned and become the Lessor's property at no cost**, and the Lessor \\\\u201cassumes all risk\\\\u201d that they may not be suitable for a subsequent tenant.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(11) Special provisions\\\",\\n  \\\"**Anti-subordination (\\\\u00a7 16) \\\\u2014 the critical provision for financing.** The lease is expressly **not subordinate** to any mortgage, deed of trust or other lien now or later placed on the Building, and the Lessor **may not grant any mortgage taking priority without the Contracting Officer's consent**. On foreclosure the Lessor must ensure the lease remains in force, and the Lessor must obtain an SNDA \\\\u201cin form and substance satisfactory to the Contracting Officer\\\\u201d if the Government requests one. **Lessor alterations (\\\\u00a7 12):** the Lessor may not alter the premises or any building system serving them \\\\u2014 including HVAC, electrical, plumbing, elevators, fire and life safety systems, structural elements and adjacent common areas \\\\u2014 without the Contracting Officer's prior written consent and 30 days' notice. **Telecommunications (\\\\u00a7 11):** the Government may install roof, riser and conduit equipment at no charge, and such equipment is a Government Improvement. **Insurance:** the Lessor carries all property, general liability and workers' compensation insurance at its own cost (\\\\u00a7 9). **Environmental:** the Lessor represents the Building is free of hazardous substances and must notify the Contracting Officer within 24 hours of any environmental condition (\\\\u00a7 17). Governing law: federal law (FAR/GSAR) together with Georgia law where not preempted; disputes under the Contract Disputes Act with appeal to the Civilian Board of Contract Appeals or the Court of Federal Claims.\\\"),\\n (\\\"(12) Risk flags\\\",\\n  \\\"**R-02** anti-subordination and lessor-assignment consent \\\\u2014 both are obstacles to acquisition financing and to an asset-level closing (high). **R-03** only twelve months of firm term; 20.1% of aggregate rent becomes terminable on 120 days' notice with no penalty (high). **R-16** no holdover premium and an express waiver of holdover damages; abandonment and SCIF condition risk on re-letting. **R-19** building area and the recovery percentage are not stated. No renewal option.\\\"),\\n], notes=[\\n \\\"Rent schedule and totals recomputed: firm term 5 \\\\u00d7 $472,500.00 = $2,362,500.00; soft term $484,350 + $496,500 + $508,950 + $521,700 + $534,600 = $2,546,100.00; total $4,908,600.00 \\\\u2014 all agree with Exhibit B.\\\",\\n \\\"**Rounding only.** Section 4.2 states the operative formula as $31.50 \\\\u00d7 (1.025)^(n\\\\u22125). Applied from the base year, that gives $33.095 / $33.922 / $34.770 for Years 7 to 9, whereas Exhibit B states $33.10 / $33.93 / $34.78 \\\\u2014 each one cent higher. Year 10 agrees ($35.64). The difference is $150 per annum in each affected year and has no economic significance, but it shows that the table was built by chaining the prior year's rounded rate rather than by applying the stated formula.\\\",\\n \\\"The lease contains **no rent schedule for any renewal term** because there is no renewal right. Any assumption of value beyond 31 December 2030 must therefore be based on market re-letting, not on the existing lease.\\\",\\n \\\"**Rent is payable in arrears**, unlike all six other leases. That affects closing prorations and the cash-flow profile in the first month after closing.\\\",\\n \\\"Deal-structure note: because \\\\u00a7 8 prohibits assignment of the lease without federal consent, an **asset-level acquisition** of Concord Office Tower would require GSA consent or a novation at closing, and the 30-day notice and written assumption requirements would need to be satisfied. An **equity-level acquisition** of Thornfield would not constitute an assignment of the lease and avoids the issue \\\\u2014 which is a further reason to confirm structure early (Section 10, item 1).\\\",\\n],\\nrisk_line=\\\"R-02 (high), R-03 (high), R-16, R-19.\\\")\\nPYEOF\\necho ok\"}", "result": "ok\n"}
{"turn": 29, "role": "assistant", "text": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_QJvrjhcWNaPYuZI7D2v06546", "name": "bash", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd $WORKSPACE_DIR && cat >> work/build.py <<'PYEOF'\\n\\n# ================= 5. RENT ROLL CROSS-CHECK =================\\nh1(doc, \\\"SECTION 5\\\", \\\"Rent Roll Cross-Check\\\")\\n\\nh2(doc, \\\"5.1  Approach\\\", space_before=2)\\npara(doc, \\\"Section 2(b) of the Engagement Letter requires the firm to cross-reference the abstracted lease terms against the Rent Roll prepared by Thornfield's property manager and to identify inconsistencies. We compared **sixteen data fields** for each of the seven leases against the underlying lease, and separately recomputed the Rent Roll's own totals, percentages and statistics from its component cells. Every field was traced to a specific article, section or exhibit of the relevant lease.\\\", size=9.8)\\n\\nh2(doc, \\\"5.2  Field-level verification\\\")\\nrows = [\\n [\\\"Tenant legal name and entity type\\\",\\\"All seven leases\\\",\\\"Agrees in all seven cases, including entity type (Delaware corporation, North Carolina PA, LLC, corporation, GSA).\\\"],\\n [\\\"Property name and street address\\\",\\\"Recitals / Section 1 of each lease\\\",\\\"Agrees in all seven cases, including suite and unit designations.\\\"],\\n [\\\"Rentable square footage\\\",\\\"Premises articles and exhibits\\\",\\\"Agrees in all seven cases (87,500 / 3,200 / 5,800 / 22,000 / 18,750 / 4,400 / 15,000 = 156,650).\\\"],\\n [\\\"Lease date\\\",\\\"Definitions / Section 1\\\",\\\"Agrees in all seven cases.\\\"],\\n [\\\"Commencement date\\\",\\\"Definitions / Section 1\\\",\\\"Agrees in all seven cases.\\\"],\\n [\\\"Expiration date\\\",\\\"Definitions / Section 1\\\",\\\"Agrees in all seven cases and is consistent with commencement plus the stated term.\\\"],\\n [\\\"Original term (years)\\\",\\\"Term article\\\",\\\"Agrees: 10 / 7 / 15 / 10 / 10 / 10 / 10. GSA correctly shown as \\\\u201c10 (5 firm / 5 soft)\\\\u201d.\\\"],\\n [\\\"Remaining term from 1/1/2025\\\",\\\"Computed from expiration dates\\\",\\\"Agrees with our computation for all seven (4.42, 3.83, 7.25, n/a, 7.5, 5.75 and 6.0/1.0 years).\\\"],\\n [\\\"Lease status\\\",\\\"Term article and expiry\\\",\\\"Agrees, including the Southeastern holdover and the GSA firm-term expiry.\\\"],\\n [\\\"Lease type\\\",\\\"Operating expense article\\\",\\\"Agrees in all seven cases (NNN, modified gross, NNN, NNN, full-service gross, NNN + percentage rent, government).\\\"],\\n [\\\"Escalation type and rate\\\",\\\"Base rent article and rent schedule\\\",\\\"Agrees as a description in all seven cases **but see Section 6**: three schedules do not follow the stated mechanism.\\\"],\\n [\\\"Year 1 and current rent per RSF\\\",\\\"Rent schedule\\\",\\\"Agrees in all seven cases.\\\"],\\n [\\\"Current annual and monthly base rent\\\",\\\"Rent schedule\\\",\\\"Agrees in all seven cases; each annual figure recomputes exactly as rate \\\\u00d7 RSF and each monthly figure as annual \\\\u00f7 12.\\\"],\\n [\\\"Security deposit\\\",\\\"Deposit / guaranty article\\\",\\\"Agrees, including the blanks for Apex, Soto, Southeastern, Verdana and GSA, each of which is confirmed by the lease.\\\"],\\n [\\\"Renewal options\\\",\\\"Renewal article\\\",\\\"Agrees in substance in all seven cases, although no notice deadline is stated other than the Southeastern lapse.\\\"],\\n [\\\"Percentage rent and breakpoint\\\",\\\"\\\\u00a7 3.3 and Exhibit B (Pint & Platter)\\\",\\\"Agrees as to rate (6%) and the fixed breakpoint ($1,350,000); the lease's separate \\\\u201cNatural Breakpoint\\\\u201d definition is not reflected (R-14).\\\"],\\n [\\\"Notes column\\\",\\\"Various\\\",\\\"Accurate as far as it goes, but omits several provisions with material consequences \\\\u2014 see Section 5.5.\\\"],\\n]\\nadd_table(doc, [\\\"Rent Roll field\\\",\\\"Source used for verification\\\",\\\"Result\\\"], rows, [1.62,1.75,3.33], size=7.9, head_size=7.7, keep_rows=True)\\nsource_note(doc, \\\"The Rent Roll is, on the whole, an accurate transcription of the lease documents. Sixteen of the seventeen fields examined agree for all seven leases.\\\")\\n\\nh2(doc, \\\"5.3  Differences identified\\\")\\npara(doc, \\\"Six differences were found. Only two are differences **between** the Rent Roll and the lease; the others are points where the Rent Roll faithfully reproduces a problem that exists in the lease itself, and so are matters for the lease, not for the property manager.\\\", size=9.8)\\nrows = [\\n [\\\"1\\\",\\\"Weighted average remaining lease term\\\",\\\"Summary sheet: ~5.7 years (soft) / ~4.6 years (firm)\\\",\\\"Recomputation from the Rent Roll's own data and stated method\\\",\\\"**Rent Roll error.** Correct figures 5.25 / 4.25 years. Not reproducible on any alternative weighting basis. Overstates term by ~0.4 years.\\\"],\\n [\\\"2\\\",\\\"Carry-forward of the Southeastern Year 10 rate\\\",\\\"$9.25/RSF; $305,250 holdover\\\",\\\"Exhibit B states $9.25, but 2% compounding from Year 9 gives $8.97 and the lease's \\\\u00a7 4.1 makes Exhibit B controlling\\\",\\\"**Lease error reproduced faithfully.** Rent Roll is correct to the lease; the lease is internally inconsistent. Corrected holdover base would be $295,871 per annum.\\\"],\\n [\\\"3\\\",\\\"Carry-forward of the BrightPath Year 8 rate\\\",\\\"$25.90/RSF; $150,220\\\",\\\"Exhibit B states $25.90; 2.5% compounding from Year 7 gives $26.15\\\",\\\"**Lease error reproduced faithfully.** Understates current rent by $1,456 per annum.\\\"],\\n [\\\"4\\\",\\\"Carry-forward of the Apex Year 6 rate\\\",\\\"$6.37/RSF; $557,375\\\",\\\"Exhibit B states $6.370; the 3% formula applied to Year 4 gives $6.376, of which $6.370 is a carry-forward of an erroneous Year 5\\\",\\\"**Lease error reproduced faithfully.** Understates current rent by $526 per annum.\\\"],\\n [\\\"5\\\",\\\"BrightPath security deposit\\\",\\\"$25,440.00\\\",\\\"Described in the lease as \\\\u201ctwo (2) months of Base Rent\\\\u201d; two months of current rent is $25,036.67\\\",\\\"**Lease internal inconsistency.** Amount equals two months at $26.317/RSF, a rate not in the schedule. Confirm amount held.\\\"],\\n [\\\"6\\\",\\\"GSA current Lease Year\\\",\\\"\\\\u201cYear 5 (Jan 2025 \\\\u2013 Dec 2025) \\\\u2014 Final Firm Year\\\\u201d\\\",\\\"As at the Rent Roll date of 1 December 2024 the current year is Year 4; Year 5 begins 1 January 2025\\\",\\\"**Presentation only.** Consistent with the Rent Roll's 1 January 2025 reference convention; no substantive difference.\\\"],\\n]\\nadd_table(doc, [\\\"#\\\",\\\"Item\\\",\\\"Rent Roll value\\\",\\\"Lease document value\\\",\\\"Finding\\\"], rows, [0.28,1.42,1.32,1.75,1.93], size=7.6, head_size=7.4,\\n          align_center_cols=(0,), keep_rows=True)\\nsource_note(doc, \\\"For completeness: we found **no** difference between the Rent Roll and any lease as to tenant identity, property, RSF, lease date, commencement, expiration, term, lease type, Year 1 rent, current rent per RSF, annual or monthly rent, security deposit amount, renewal option description, percentage rent rate or breakpoint.\\\")\\n\\nh2(doc, \\\"5.4  Arithmetic verification of the Rent Roll's own figures\\\")\\nrows = [\\n [\\\"Aggregate annual base rent\\\",\\\"557,375.00 + 96,800.00 + 150,220.00 + 305,250.00 + 676,312.50 + 89,386.00 + 472,500.00\\\",\\\"2,347,843.50\\\",\\\"\\\\u2713 agrees with the stated total and with Engagement Letter \\\\u00a7 2(f)\\\"],\\n [\\\"Aggregate monthly base rent\\\",\\\"Sum of the seven monthly figures\\\",\\\"195,653.63\\\",\\\"\\\\u2713 (aggregate annual \\\\u00f7 12 = 195,653.625, rounded to 195,653.63)\\\"],\\n [\\\"Total RSF\\\",\\\"Sum of the seven RSF figures\\\",\\\"156,650\\\",\\\"\\\\u2713 agrees with Engagement Letter \\\\u00a7 2(a)\\\"],\\n [\\\"Share of full portfolio\\\",\\\"156,650 \\\\u00f7 243,650\\\",\\\"64.29%\\\",\\\"\\\\u2713 computed; the Rent Roll does not state it\\\"],\\n [\\\"Each tenant's percentage\\\",\\\"Tenant rent \\\\u00f7 2,347,843.50\\\",\\\"28.81 / 23.74 / 20.12 / 13.00 / 6.40 / 4.12 / 3.79\\\",\\\"\\\\u2713 agrees with the Rent Roll's rounded figures and sums to 100.0%\\\"],\\n [\\\"Each annual rent\\\",\\\"Rate per RSF \\\\u00d7 RSF\\\",\\\"See Table 6.2\\\",\\\"\\\\u2713 all seven recompute exactly\\\"],\\n [\\\"Each monthly rent\\\",\\\"Annual \\\\u00f7 12\\\",\\\"See Table 6.2\\\",\\\"\\\\u2713 all seven recompute to the cent\\\"],\\n [\\\"Southeastern holdover\\\",\\\"$16,958.33 \\\\u00d7 150% = $25,437.50 per month \\\\u00d7 12\\\",\\\"305,250.00\\\",\\\"\\\\u2713 correct on the stated base (see the Year 10 issue at R-12)\\\"],\\n [\\\"Verdana parking income\\\",\\\"8 spaces \\\\u00d7 $175 \\\\u00d7 12\\\",\\\"16,800.00\\\",\\\"\\\\u2713 correct, and correctly excluded from base rent\\\"],\\n [\\\"Implied cap rate\\\",\\\"2,347,843.50 \\\\u00f7 187,500,000\\\",\\\"1.25%\\\",\\\"\\\\u2713 arithmetically correct \\\\u2014 but see Section 5.6 as to its usefulness\\\"],\\n [\\\"Number in holdover / firm term expiring within 12 months\\\",\\\"Count\\\",\\\"1 / 1\\\",\\\"\\\\u2713 agrees with the leases\\\"],\\n]\\nadd_table(doc, [\\\"Check\\\",\\\"Recomputation\\\",\\\"Rent Roll figure\\\",\\\"Result\\\"], rows, [1.42,2.2,0.95,2.13], size=7.6, head_size=7.5,\\n          align_center_cols=(2,), keep_rows=True)\\n\\nh2(doc, \\\"5.5  Items not verifiable, and provisions the Rent Roll omits\\\")\\npara(doc, \\\"**Not capable of verification from the documents provided:**\\\", size=9.8, space_before=2)\\nbullets(doc, [\\n \\\"**Occupation status of Buildings A and B at Thornfield Distribution Center.** The Rent Roll covers seven leases across the Portfolio but does not show the other tenants in the 240,000 RSF Distribution Center. Without that information the Apex exclusive-use covenant (\\\\u00a7 6.2) cannot be tested (R-08).\\\",\\n \\\"**Occupancy of Haywood Village Shops** (62,000 RSF). Only the Pint & Platter lease is shown. The 70% co-tenancy test cannot be applied and it is unknown whether the 180-day clock has already started (R-07).\\\",\\n \\\"**Status of the 9th floor at Concord Office Tower.** Not covered by any of the seven leases. If it is leased to a third party through 30 June 2032, the Lessor may be unable to perform the Verdana expansion option (R-06).\\\",\\n \\\"**Whether any ROFR notice was delivered to Apex**, whether Apex responded, and whether any memorandum of ROFR has been recorded in Mecklenburg County (R-01).\\\",\\n \\\"**Actual amounts of the two security deposits held** and the current status of the Pint & Platter guaranty (R-15, R-21).\\\",\\n \\\"**Percentage rent history for Pint & Platter** \\\\u2014 no gross sales statements were provided, so no percentage rent can be assumed (R-14).\\\",\\n \\\"**Operating expense recoveries** for any tenant: no base-year statements, estimates or reconciliations were produced (R-19).\\\",\\n], size=9.2)\\npara(doc, \\\"**Material provisions the Rent Roll does not disclose:**\\\", size=9.8, space_before=4)\\nbullets(doc, [\\n \\\"**Apex ROFR \\\\u2014 portfolio-transaction trigger.** The Notes column says \\\\u201cROFR on Building C\\\\u201d, which understates the provision: the ROFR expressly extends to a sale of Building C as part of a portfolio transaction and requires notice before the Seller executes any agreement (R-01).\\\",\\n \\\"**Apex exclusive use across Buildings A, B and C** \\\\u2014 not mentioned at all (R-08).\\\",\\n \\\"**Soto early termination notice deadline of 30 April 2025** \\\\u2014 the Notes column says only \\\\u201cEarly termination right after Year 4\\\\u201d. The deadline, the $40,657.14 fee and the net exposure are not disclosed (R-05).\\\",\\n \\\"**Soto personal guaranty through 2033** \\\\u2014 mentioned, but without the duration or the fact that it survives amendments and assignments.\\\",\\n \\\"**BrightPath renewal cap** (first Renewal Term at the lesser of FMR or 3% compounding) \\\\u2014 not mentioned; it caps recovery at renewal (R-18).\\\",\\n \\\"**BrightPath recapture right and 50% assignment profit share** \\\\u2014 not mentioned.\\\",\\n \\\"**Southeastern environmental regime** \\\\u2014 AFFF, foam concentrates and PFAS are expressly permitted on the premises under Article 14, with a surviving indemnity. This is the single most significant omission in the Rent Roll given its 13.0% share of rent (R-25).\\\",\\n \\\"**GSA anti-subordination, lessor-assignment consent and 120-day termination mechanics** \\\\u2014 the Notes column refers to the termination right but not to the two provisions that most affect a purchaser and a lender (R-02).\\\",\\n \\\"**Pint & Platter guaranty burn-off** \\\\u2014 described as \\\\u201cPersonal guaranty by Lance Whitford through earlier of Yr 5 or $2M TTM sales\\\\u201d, which is accurate but does not state the 30 September 2025 date (R-21).\\\",\\n \\\"**Estoppel and SNDA position for any tenant** \\\\u2014 no field and no note (R-09, R-10).\\\",\\n], size=9.2)\\n\\nh2(doc, \\\"5.6  The Rent Roll's weighted average term and implied cap rate\\\")\\npara(doc, \\\"**Weighted average remaining term** is dealt with at Section 1.5: the stated figures of ~5.7 and ~4.6 years are not reproducible, and the independently computed figures are **5.25 years** (GSA soft term included) and **4.25 years** (GSA firm term only). The Rent Roll's own methodology note acknowledges that the treatment of the Southeastern holdover and of the GSA firm versus soft term \\\\u201cmaterially affects the calculation\\\\u201d, but does not explain the ~0.4 year gap.\\\", size=9.8)\\npara(doc, \\\"**Implied cap rate of ~1.25%.** The Rent Roll correctly flags that this figure reflects annual **rent** of $2,347,843.50 over the full portfolio purchase price of $187,500,000 and that it is not indicative of the portfolio cap rate. We agree, and would go further: the figure has no analytical value and should not be carried into any internal Galleon material, because (i) it uses rent rather than net operating income, so it ignores recoveries and expenses entirely; and (ii) it sets the rent of seven leases against the price of twelve properties. For reference only, the acquisition price equates to **$769.55 per RSF** across the whole portfolio (243,650 RSF) and **$1,196.94 per RSF** if notionally allocated to the 156,650 RSF covered by these seven leases. Neither figure is a valuation conclusion.\\\", size=9.8)\\nPYEOF\\necho ok\"}"}], "input_tokens": 194483, "output_tokens": 4265, "finish_reason": "completed"}
