{"turn": 54, "role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "call_00_ET_yukTf0ITeK9BW4QkJdCS6165", "tool_name": "bash", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd $WORKSPACE_DIR && pdftotext -f 9 -l 9 -layout work/pdf/lease-abstraction-report.pdf - | sed -n '1,70p'\"}", "result": "BIRCHWOOD & HALE LLP | Lease Abstraction Report — Thornfield Portfolio | Privileged & Confidential\n\n\n\n ID   Document                            Format    Parties / addressee          Date     Scope of document reviewed\n\n D9   GSA Lease No. GS-04B-15678          DOCX      Lessor: Thornfield Realty   30 Sep    5th Floor, Concord Office\n      (Form L201D)                                  Holdings LP / Lessee:        2020     Tower; Ex. A–C\n                                                    USA (GSA Region 4)\n\nNo amendments, side letters, other guaranties, SNDAs, estoppel certificates, subleases, commencement date memoranda or\noperating expense reconciliations were provided. See Section 10.\n\n\n2.3 Method\n   • Inventory and control. All nine documents were inventoried before analysis and are listed in\n     Section 2.2 and Appendix B. The Engagement Letter was treated as the controlling instruction for\n     the form and content of this Report.\n   • Full-text review. Each lease was read in full, including recitals and exhibits, rather than relying\n     on the Rent Roll's summary. Facts were extracted with section citations into a source-to-issue log.\n   • Independent recomputation. Every rent schedule was recomputed year by year from the stated\n     starting rent, the stated escalation mechanism and the stated rentable area, and compared with\n     the rate table in the lease and with the Rent Roll (Section 6 and Appendix A). Every percentage\n     share, allowance, amortisation and fee was recomputed from first principles.\n   • Date arithmetic. Commencement and expiration dates, option notice deadlines and notice\n     periods were recomputed from the operative clauses rather than accepted as stated, including the\n     Engagement Letter's own due diligence period.\n   • Reconciliation and flagging. Differences were classified by cause (rounding, a drafting error in\n     one year, an error propagating through the term, or a genuine conflict between provisions). Where\n     a lease conflicts with itself, the conflict is described and the probable controlling provision\n     identified; where it cannot be resolved on the documents it is flagged for follow-up rather than\n     silently resolved.\n   • Separation of fact from judgement. Extracted facts are presented in the abstraction tables; our\n     characterisation and conclusions appear in Sections 1, 3 and 7; open questions are collected in\n     Section 10. Risk ratings are our assessment and are explained in Section 7.1.\n\n2.4 Conventions and limitations\n   • Reference date. Rent, term and option positions are stated as at January 1, 2025, consistent\n     with the Rent Roll. Remaining terms are measured from that date.\n   • Rent convention. “Current annual base rent” means the contractual base or shell rent for the\n     Lease Year in effect on the reference date, excluding operating expense recoveries, percentage\n     rent and parking income. For Southeastern and GSA it is the Rent Roll figure, which is at-will rent\n     rather than term rent; this is noted wherever it matters.\n   • Lease Year convention. Each lease defines its own Lease Year from its own commencement date;\n     the “current Lease Year” stated for each tenant therefore falls on a different calendar boundary.\n   • Rounding. Per-square-foot rates are quoted as stated in the leases. Where we recompute a rate,\n     we round to the nearest $0.001 per RSF per annum for the Apex and BrightPath leases (which use\n     that convention) and to the nearest cent elsewhere, matching each lease's own stated convention.\n     Annual and monthly amounts are rounded to the nearest cent.\n   • Area convention. Rentable square footage is taken as stipulated in each lease. Neither Millbrook\n     lease, the Greystone lease nor the GSA lease states the building or park denominator used for the\n     tenant's proportionate share, so the recovery percentage for those four leases could not be verified\n     (R-19).\n   • Not a title, environmental or financial review. Title, survey, environmental, structural, zoning\n     and tenant credit review are outside scope (Engagement Letter § 2) and are being handled by\n     Stonebridge Title & Escrow LLC, Hanover Environmental Consultants Inc. and Crestline\n     Accounting Group LLP. Where this Report touches on those matters it does so only to refer a\n     lease-based issue.\n   • No verification of tenant assertions. Nothing in this Report verifies any fact stated in a lease by\n     a tenant, or the truth of any representation made by the Seller or the Rent Roll. See Section 11.\n\n\n\n\n                           Galleon Capital Advisors LLC · December 20, 2024 · Page 9 of 49\n\f"}
